068 #edb/ref 17592186308092 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186120254 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186120254 1071 "A nonzero vector $⟨a, b, 0⟩$ is parallel to the plane $r \\cdot ⟨6,-2, 0⟩ =-14$. What is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}$?" 13194139533415 17592186045585 false] [17592186120254 1071 "A nonzero vector $⟨a,\\,b,\\,0⟩$ is parallel to the plane $r\\cdot ⟨6,-2,0⟩=-14.$ What is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186120254 1074 "We need a vector $v=⟨{t}_{1},{t}_{2},{t}_{3}⟩$ that is parallel to the given plane. \n\nFrom the equation $r\\cdot ⟨6,-2,0⟩=-14$ of the plane, we get the normal vector $n=⟨6,-2,0⟩.$ So, $v⊥n$ and we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨{t}_{1},{t}_{2},{t}_{3}⟩\\cdot ⟨6,-2,0⟩ & =0 \\\\ 6{t}_{1}-2{t}_{2} & =0 \\\\ {t}_{2} & =3{t}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we obtain \n\n$$\nv=⟨{t}_{1},3{t}_{1},{t}_{3}⟩,\n$$\n\nwhere ${t}_{1},{t}_{3}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ \n\nWe are told that the third component of $v$ eqauls $0.$ So, we must set ${t}_{3}=0.$ \n\nSince $v$ is nonzero, we can pick any ${t}_{1}\\ne 0.$ For example, setting ${t}_{1}=1$, we get\n\n$$\nv=⟨1,\\,3,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{1}{3}.$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186120257 1068 #edb/ref 17592186117671 13194139533415 17592186045585 false] [17592186120257 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308218 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186120257 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186120257 1071 "Find the shortest distance between the point $P(3, 5,-2)$ and the plane $Π$ with equation $(r - \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}) \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} = 0$." 13194139533415 17592186045585 false] [17592186120257 1071 "Find the shortest distance between the point $P(3,5,-2)$ and the plane $Π$ with equation $(r-[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}])\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]=0.$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186120257 1074 "Let's pick a point on the plane, for instance, $Q(5,9,0).$ Then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, from the equation of the plane, we get a normal vector $n=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].$\n\nFinally, the shortest distance from the point $P$ to the plane $Π$ is the absolute value of the scalar projection of $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ onto the normal vector of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|{\\text{comp}}_{n}\\,\\overset{\\to }{PQ}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot n|}{|n|} \\\\  & =\\frac{|2\\cdot (-2)+4\\cdot 2+2\\cdot (-4)|}{\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-4)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|-4+8-8|}{\\sqrt{4+4+16}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|-4|}{\\sqrt{24}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186120259 1068 #edb/ref 17592186117683 13194139533415 17592186045585 false] [17592186120259 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307966 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186120259 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186120259 1071 "Given the curve $r(t) = ti + (1 + \\frac{1}{2}t^{2})j$, find all of the points at which $r^{′}(t)$ and $r(t)$ are perpendicular." 13194139533415 17592186045585 false] [17592186120259 1071 "Given the curve $r(t)=t\\,i+(1+\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2})\\,j$, find all of the points at which ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $r(t)$ are perpendicular." 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186120259 1074 "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =i+t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors $r(t)$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular for some value of the parameter $t$ if and only if $r(t)\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ [t\\,i+(1+\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2})\\,j]\\cdot (i+t\\,j) & =0 \\\\ t\\cdot 1+(1+\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2})\\cdot t & =0 \\\\ 2t+\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{3} & =0 \\\\ t(4+{t}^{2}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only solution is $t=0$ since $4+{t}^{2}>0$ for any $t.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0)=0\\,i+(1+\\frac{1}{2}(0)^{2})\\,j=j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n we conclude that $r(t)$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular at the point $(0,1).$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186124680 1038 #edb/ref 17592186308744 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186124680 1038 #edb/ref 17592186308745 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186124680 1038 #edb/ref 17592186308746 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186152283 1038 #edb/ref 17592186308550 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186152285 1038 #edb/ref 17592186308565 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186152285 1038 #edb/ref 17592186308567 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186152285 1038 #edb/ref 17592186308568 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186152285 1038 #edb/ref 17592186308569 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186152286 1038 #edb/ref 17592186308581 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186152286 1038 #edb/ref 17592186308592 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186152286 1038 #edb/ref 17592186308593 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186152286 1038 #edb/ref 17592186308595 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186152286 1038 #edb/ref 17592186308596 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186152571 1068 #edb/ref 17592186149833 13194139533415 17592186045585 false] [17592186152571 1068 #edb/ref 17592186149837 13194139533415 17592186045585 false] [17592186152571 1068 #edb/ref 17592186149841 13194139533415 17592186045585 false] [17592186152571 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307624 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186152571 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307788 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186152571 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307832 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186152571 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307899 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186152571 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308299 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186152571 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186152571 1071 "Given the set of functions ${x, e^{x}, 2x}$ defined on $x ∈ R$, select the correct options in the following reasoning.For all $x ∈ R$, we have that\n$2$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}} {{field-3}}" 13194139533415 17592186045585 false] [17592186152571 1071 "Given the set of functions ${x,\\,{e}^{x},\\,2x}$ defined on $x\\in R,$ select the correct options in the following reasoning.For all $x\\in R,$  we have that\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}\\cdot x+{{field-2}}\\cdot {e}^{x}+{{field-3}}\\cdot 2x=0.\n$$\n\nSince \n{{field-4}} \nthe scalar coefficients in the linear combination are zero, the given set of functions is linearly \n{{field-5}}." 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186152571 1074 "Recall that the set of functions ${{f}_{1}(x),{f}_{2}(x),…,{f}_{n}(x)},$ which are defined on some interval $I,$ is *linearly dependent* if there exist real scalars ${k}_{1},{k}_{2},…,{k}_{n}$ not all zero such that the linear combination ${k}_{1}\\cdot {f}_{1}+{k}_{2}\\cdot {f}_{2}+…+{k}_{n}\\cdot {f}_{n}$ is identically zero, i.e, \n\n$$\n{k}_{1}\\cdot {f}_{1}(x)+{k}_{2}\\cdot {f}_{2}(x)+…+{k}_{n}\\cdot {f}_{n}(x)=0\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in I.$ Otherwise, if ${k}_{1}={k}_{2}=⋯={k}_{n}=0$ is the only solution, the set is *linearly independent*.\n\nNow, for all $x\\in R,$ we have that \n\n$$\n2\\cdot x+0\\cdot {e}^{x}+(-1)\\cdot 2x=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $\\text{not all}$ the scalar coefficients in the linear combination are zero, the given set of functions is linearly $\\text{dependent}.$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186152575 1068 #edb/ref 17592186149877 13194139533415 17592186045585 false] [17592186152575 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307713 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186152575 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186152575 1071 "$$\n{y}^{″}+y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n- ${y}_{2}(x)=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n- The functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation." 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186152575 1071 "$y^{″} + y = 0$\nConsider the differential equation above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $y_{1}(x) = \\sin (x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n2. $y_{2}(x) = 3\\sin (x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n3. The functions $y_{1}(x)$ and $y_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation." 13194139533415 17592186045585 false] [17592186152575 1074 "Recall that to determine whether $y(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute $y$ and its derivatives into the equation. \n\nLet's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{1}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement II is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{2}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement III is false. Both ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the equation. To check if these functions are linearly independent, we compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) & 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) & 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\cdot 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the  Wronskian equals $0$ for all $x\\in R,$ the functions are not linearly independent. Hence, ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ don't form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186152576 1068 #edb/ref 17592186149883 13194139533415 17592186045585 false] [17592186152576 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307869 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186152576 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186152576 1071 "$$\n{y}^{″}+7{y}^{′}+6y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{-x}$ is a solution to the equation.\n- ${y}_{2}(x)=1+{e}^{-x}$ is a solution to the equation.\n- The functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation." 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186152576 1071 "$y^{″} + 7y^{′} + 6y = 0$\nConsider the differential equation above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $y_{1}(x) = e^{-x}$ is a solution to the equation.\n2. $y_{2}(x) = 1 + e^{-x}$ is a solution to the equation.\n3. The functions $y_{1}(x)$ and $y_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation." 13194139533415 17592186045585 false] [17592186152576 1074 "Recall that to determine whether $y(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute $y$ and its derivatives into the equation. \n\nLet's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. We find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{1}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =-{e}^{-x} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-x}+7(-{e}^{-x})+6{e}^{-x} & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement II is false. We find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{2}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =-{e}^{-x} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-x}+7(-{e}^{-x})+6(1+{e}^{-x}) & =0 \\\\ 6 & =0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}(x)$ is not a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${y}_{2}(x)$ is not a solution of the given differential equation, ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ don't form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186157404 1038 #edb/ref 17592186308608 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186157404 1038 #edb/ref 17592186308609 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186157404 1038 #edb/ref 17592186308610 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186157404 1038 #edb/ref 17592186308611 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186157404 1038 #edb/ref 17592186308612 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186157404 1038 #edb/ref 17592186308613 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186157404 1038 #edb/ref 17592186308614 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186157404 1038