ac{1}{2}.$ Then, the best upper bound we can form is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|f(x)| & <{{field-6}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $|f(x)|\\le 3$ whenever $|x|<\\frac{1}{2}.$ Thus, taking {{field-7}} and {{field-8}} satisfies the definition, and we conclude that $f(x)=O(1)$ as $x\\to 0.$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186157755 1074 "We say $f(x)$ is of order $g(x)$ as $x\\to a,$ and we write \n\n$$\nf(x)=O(g(x))\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\nif there exist $\\text{positive}$ constants $K$ and $\\delta $ such that\n\n$$\n|f(x)|\\le K|g(x)|\\,\\text{for all}\\,0<|x-a|<\\delta .\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, in our case, we need to show that\n\n$$\n|f|\\le K\\cdot 1\\,\\text{for}\\,0<|x|<\\delta \n$$\n\ni.e.,\n\n$$\n|f|\\le K\\,\\text{for}\\,0<|x|<\\delta .\n$$\n\nBy the triangle inequality, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|f(x)| & =|8{x}^{4}-2x+1| \\\\  & \\le |8{x}^{4}|+|2x|+|1| \\\\  & =8|x{|}^{4}+2|x|+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let $|x|<\\frac{1}{2}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|f(x)| & =|8{x}^{4}-2x+1| \\\\  & \\le 8|x{|}^{4}+2|x|+1 \\\\  & <8{|\\frac{1}{2}|}^{4}+2|\\frac{1}{2}|+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+1+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2} \\\\  & <3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $|f(x)|\\le 3$ whenever $|x|<\\frac{1}{2}.$ Thus, taking $K=3$ and $\\delta =\\frac{1}{2}$ satisfies the definition, and we conclude that $f(x)=O(1)$ as $x\\to 0.$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186157759 1068 #edb/ref 17592186155381 13194139533415 17592186045585 false] [17592186157759 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308317 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186157759 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186157759 1071 "Suppose $f(x) = x + 2e^{2x}$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $f = O(x)$ as $x → ∞$\n2. $f = O(e^{2x})$ as $x → ∞$\n3. $f = O(e^{3x})$ as $x → ∞$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 false] [17592186157759 1071 "Suppose $f(x)=x+2{e}^{2x}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f=O(x)$ as $x\\to \\infty $\n- $f=O({e}^{2x})$ as $x\\to \\infty $\n- $f=O({e}^{3x})$ as $x\\to \\infty $" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186157759 1074 "Suppose we wish to test whether the following statement is true:\n\n$$\nf(x)=O(g(x))\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is either finite or $+\\infty .$ To do this, we can examine the following limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}\n$$\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- If the limit is *finite*, the statement is true.\n\n- If the limit is *infinite*, the statement is false.\n\n- If the limit does not exist, the test is inconclusive.\n\nHere, we must consider the function\n\n$$\nf(x)=x+2{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Let $g(x)=x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x+2{e}^{2x}}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince direct evaluation leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty },$ we can evaluate the limit using L'Hopital's rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x+2{e}^{2x}}{x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(x+2{e}^{2x})^{′}}{(x)^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1+4{e}^{2x}}{1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(1+4{e}^{2x}) \\\\  & =\\infty \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f\\ne O(x)$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\n- Statement II is true. Let $g(x)={e}^{2x}.$ Then, using L'Hopital's rule where needed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x+2{e}^{2x}}{{e}^{2x}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{x}{{e}^{2x}}+\\frac{2{e}^{2x}}{{e}^{2x}}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x}{{e}^{2x}}+2 \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(x)^{′}}{({e}^{2x})^{′}}+2 \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{2{e}^{2x}}+2 \\\\  & =0+2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f=O({e}^{2x})$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\n- Statement III is true. Let $g(x)={e}^{3x}.$ Then, using L'Hopital's rule where needed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x+2{e}^{2x}}{{e}^{3x}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{x}{{e}^{3x}}+\\frac{2{e}^{2x}}{{e}^{3x}}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x}{{e}^{3x}}+{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }2{e}^{-x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(x)^{′}}{({e}^{3x})^{′}}+0 \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{3{e}^{3x}} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f=O({e}^{3x})$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186166910 1038 #edb/ref 17592186308363 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186166910 1038 #edb/ref 17592186308364 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186166910 1038 #edb/ref 17592186308365 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186166911 1038 #edb/ref 17592186308476 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186166911 1038 #edb/ref 17592186308478 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186166911 1038 #edb/ref 17592186308479 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186166911 1038 #edb/ref 17592186308480 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186166911 1038 #edb/ref 17592186308485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186166911 1038 #edb/ref 17592186308486 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186166911 1038 #edb/ref 17592186308488 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186166912 1038 #edb/ref 17592186308519 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186166912 1038 #edb/ref 17592186308520 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186166912 1038 #edb/ref 17592186308530 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186166912 1038 #edb/ref 17592186308531 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186166913 1038 #edb/ref 17592186308431 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186166913 1038 #edb/ref 17592186308432 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186166913 1038 #edb/ref 17592186308433 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [175921861