d{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda =1\\pm 6\\text{i}.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.$\\,$\nSince \n{{field-1}},\nthe equilibrium point (origin) is {{field-2}}." 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186167236 1071 "${x^{′}(t) = x(t) - 4y(t); y^{′}(t) = 9x(t) + y(t)$\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $λ = 1 ± 6\\textrm{i}$. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\nSince\n{{field-1}},\nthe equilibrium point (origin) is {{field-2}}." 13194139533415 17592186045585 false] [17592186167236 1074 "Let $v=p+q\\text{i},$ where $p,q\\in {R}^{2},$ be an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =a+b\\text{i}.$\n\nThen, a complex solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t)=v{e}^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nUsing Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t) & =v{e}^{(a+b\\text{i})t} \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt))(p+q\\text{i}) \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)+\\text{i}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\text{x}(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)+{c}_{2}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q),\n$$\n\nwhere the parts $(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)$ and $(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q)$ are bounded.\n\nNow, notice that $a=\\text{Re}(\\lambda )=1>0$ and ${e}^{at}={e}^{t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty .$ Thus, we have a spiral that is traversed toward infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ \n\nTherefore, since $\\text{Re}(\\lambda )>0,$ the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{an unstable spiral (source)}.$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186167238 1068 #edb/ref 17592186164469 13194139533415 17592186045585 false] [17592186167238 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308327 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186167238 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186167238 1071 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=3{x}_{2}(t)+4,\\end{aligned}\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform $L$ to both sides of each equation in our system, we get a system with variables ${X}_{1}(s)=L{{x}_{1}(t)}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L{{x}_{2}(t)}.$ In terms of the parameter $s,$ the solution of the new system is\n\n${X}_{1}(s)=$ {{field-1}} $,\\,$\n${X}_{2}(s)=\\frac{2s+4}{s(s-3)}.$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186167238 1071 "${x_{1}^{′}(t) = x_{1}(t) + 2x_{2}(t); x_{2}^{′}(t) = 3x_{2}(t) + 4,x(0) = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform $L$ to both sides of each equation in our system, we get a system with variables $X_{1}(s) = L{x_{1}(t)}$ and $X_{2}(s) = L{x_{2}(t)}$. In terms of the parameter $s$, the solution of the new system is$X_{1}(s) =$ {{field-1}} $$,\n$X_{2}(s) = (2s + 4)/(s(s - 3))$." 13194139533415 17592186045585 false] [17592186167238 1074 "First, we compute the Laplace transforms of both sides of each equation in our system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}L{{x}_{1}^{′}(t)}=L{{x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t)} \\\\ L{{x}_{2}^{′}(t)}=L{3{x}_{2}(t)+4}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\n$$\n\nUsing the derivative property of the Laplace transform, we obtain the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}sL{{x}_{1}(t)}-{x}_{1}(0)=L{{x}_{1}(t)}+2L{{x}_{2}(t)} \\\\ sL{{x}_{2}(t)}-{x}_{2}(0)=3L{{x}_{2}(t)}+4L{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich can be simplified to the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}s{X}_{1}(s)+1={X}_{1}(s)+2{X}_{2}(s) \\\\ s{X}_{2}(s)-2=3{X}_{2}(s)+\\frac{4}{s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${X}_{1}(s)=L{{x}_{1}(t)}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L{{x}_{2}(t)},$ and we have used the fact that $L{1}=\\frac{1}{s}.$\n\nNow, solving the second equation for ${X}_{2}(s),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s{X}_{2}(s)-2 & =3{X}_{2}(s)+\\frac{4}{s} \\\\ (s-3){X}_{2}(s) & =2+\\frac{4}{s} \\\\ (s-3){X}_{2}(s) & =\\frac{2s+4}{s} \\\\ {X}_{2}(s) & =\\frac{2s+4}{s(s-3)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this in the first equation and solving for ${X}_{1}(s)$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s{X}_{1}(s)+1 & ={X}_{1}(s)+2{X}_{2}(s) \\\\ (s-1){X}_{1}(s) & =2{X}_{2}(s)-1 \\\\ (s-1){X}_{1}(s) & =2(\\frac{2s+4}{s(s-3)})-1 \\\\ (s-1){X}_{1}(s) & =\\frac{4s+8}{s(s-3)}-1 \\\\ (s-1){X}_{1}(s) & =\\frac{4s+8-s(s-3)}{s(s-3)} \\\\ (s-1){X}_{1}(s) & =\\frac{4s+8-{s}^{2}+3s}{s(s-3)} \\\\ (s-1){X}_{1}(s) & =\\frac{-{s}^{2}+7s+8}{s(s-3)} \\\\ {X}_{1}(s) & =\\frac{-{s}^{2}+7s+8}{s(s-1)(s-3)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution of the new system in terms of parameter $s$ is \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}(s)=\\frac{-{s}^{2}+7s+8}{s(s-1)(s-3)},\\,{X}_{2}(s)=\\frac{2s+4}{s(s-3)}.\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186167240 1068 #edb/ref 17592186164472 13194139533415 17592186045585 false] [17592186167240 1068 #edb/ref 17592186164473 13194139533415 17592186045585 false] [17592186167240 1068 #edb/ref 17592186164474 13194139533415 17592186045585 false] [17592186167240 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307570 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186167240 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307647 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186167240 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308082 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186167240 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186167240 1071 "An initial value problem has the exact solution $y_{exact}(x) = e^{x}$. A numerical method is used to approximate the solution to the initial value problem, resulting in the table below, where $y$ denotes the approximate solution at each step, and $τ_{n}$ is the local truncation error at each step. At each step, the exact value is used to approximate the new value.\n\nCalculate the local truncation error at each step. Round your answers to three decimal places.\n\n| $n$ | $x$ | $y$ | $τ_{n}$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1.000$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $0.25$ | $1.283$ | {{field-1}} |\n| $2$ | $0.5$ | $1.644$ | {{field-2}} |\n| $3$ | $0.75$ | $2.104$ | {{field-3}} |\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533415 17592186045585 false] [17592186167240 1071 "An initial value problem has the exact solution ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)={e}^{x}.$ A numerical method is used to approximate the solution to the initial value problem, resulting in the table below, where $y$ denotes the approximate solution at each step, and ${τ}_{n}$ is the local truncation error at each step. At each step, the exact value is used to approximate the new value. \n\nCalculate the local truncation error at each step. Round your answers to three decimal places.\n\n| $n$ | $x$ | $y$ | ${τ}_{n}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1.000$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $0.25$ | $1.283$ | {{field-1}} |\n| $2$ | $0.5$ | $1.644$ | {{field-2}} |\n| $3$ | $0.75$ | $2.104$ | {{field-3}} |" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186167240 1074 "The local truncation error (LTE) is the error made in a *single step* of a numerical method, when the step begins from an *exact value*. The LTE of a numerical method at step $n$ from $x$ with exact value ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)$ to ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{n} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-({y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)+Δy).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe find the LTE at each step, rounding to three decimal places in each case:\n\n- After the first step, we have ${y}_{\\text{new}}=1.283.$ So, the LTE of the first step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{1} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(0.25)-1.283 \\\\  & ={e}^{0.25}-1.283 \\\\  & ≈0.001.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- After the second step, we have ${y}_{\\text{new}}=1.644.$ So, the LTE of the second step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{2} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(0.5)-1.644 \\\\  & ={e}^{0.5}-1.644 \\\\  & ≈0.005.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- After the third step, we have ${y}_{\\text{new}}=2.104.$ So, the LTE of the third step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{3} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(0.75)-2.104 \\\\  & ={e}^{0.75}-2.104 \\\\  & ≈0.013.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed table is as given below.\n\n| $n$ | $x$ | $y$ | ${τ}_{n}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1.000$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $0.25$ | $1.283$ | $0.001$ |\n| $2$ | $0.5$ | $1.644$ | $0.005$ |\n| $3$ | $0.75$ | $2.104$ | $0.013$ |" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186167242 1068 #edb/ref 17592186164481 13194139533415 17592186045585 false] [17592186167242 1068 #edb/ref 17592186164484 13194139533415 17592186045585 false] [17592186167242 1068 #edb/ref 17592186164488 13194139533415 17592186045585 false] [17592186167242 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307589 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186167242 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307644 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186167242 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307887 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186167242 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186167242 1071 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=2y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options below regarding the given decoupled system of linear differential equations.$\\,$\nThe $x$-axis is a {{field-1}} line.\n\n$\\,$\nThe $y$-axis is a {{field-2}} line.\n\n$\\,$\nThe equilibrium point (origin) is {{field-3}}." 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186167242 1071 "${x^{′}(t) = x(t); y^{′}(t) = 2y(t)$\nSelect the correct options below regarding the given decoupled system of linear differential equations.\nThe $x$-axis is a {{field-1}} line.\n\nThe $y$-axis is a {{field-2}} line.\n\nThe equilibrium point (origin) is {{field-3}}." 13194139533415 17592186045585 false] [17592186167242 1074 "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nRecall that a homogeneous system of linear differential equations has a single equilibrium (fixed point) at the origin.\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ with the eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $x$-axis and move away from the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, the $x$-axis is the $\\text{unstable}$ line.\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=2$ with the eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $y$-axis and also move away from the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, the $y$-axis is the $\\text{unstable}$ line.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{an unstable node (source)}.$ \n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/19/19cd34ffc84e4a904d87cdfe0d8b6721861a0a39025484946dbe471a5ea4ad87.png)" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186167246 1068 #edb/ref 17592186164514 13194139533415 17592186045585 false] [17592186167246 1068 #edb/ref 17592186164517 13194139533415 17592186045585 false] [17592186167246 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307573 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186167246 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308318 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186167246 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186167246 1071 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. Select the correct options below regarding the given system.$\\,$ The equilibrium point (origin) is a \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}\nnode." 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186167246 1071 "${x^{′}(t) = x(t); y^{′}(t) = y(t)$\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. Select the correct options below regarding the given system. The equilibrium point (origin) is a\n{{field-1}} {{field-2}}\nnode." 13194139533415 17592186045585 false] [17592186167246 1074 "The matrix of our system is\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- Since the only (repeated) eigenvalue of $A$ is $\\lambda =1$ and it's positive, we have a $\\text{unstable}$ node.\n\n- Since $A$ is diagonal and has a repeated eigenvalue, there are infinitely many linearly independent eigenvectors. So, we have a $\\text{star}$ node.\n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/0e/0e2180fe05500bcff2d0a1e2c066e2364eea0fd481a63866578a07e8f46b3825.png)" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186167247 1068 #edb/ref 17592186164520 13194139533415 17592186045585 false] [17592186167247 1068 #edb/ref 17592186164521 13194139533415 17592186045585 false] [17592186167247 1068 #edb/ref 17592186164522 13194139533415 17592186045585 false] [17592186167247 1068 #edb/ref 17592186164523 13194139533415 17592186045585 false] [17592186167247 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307522 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186167247 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307834 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186167247 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308041 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186167247 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308136 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186167247 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186167247 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem for the functions $y = y(x), z = z(x)$:\n${y^{′} = 4x + y - z,\\begin{vmatrix}y(0) = 1 \\\\ z^{′} = x + 4y + z,\\end{vmatrix}z(0) = 0$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method.\nWhat entries should be placed in the columns for $Δy$ and $Δz$ in the table below if the step size is $Δx = 2$?\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | $z^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0$ |  |  | {{field-1}} |  |  | {{field-2}} |\n\nTherefore, $y(2) \\approx$\n{{field-3}}\nand $z(2) \\approx$\n{{field-4}}." 13194139533415 17592186045585 false] [17592186167247 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem for the functions $y=y(x),z=z(x):$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=4x+y-z,\\, & y(0)=1 \\\\ {z}^{′}=x+4y+z,\\, & z(0)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method.\nWhat entries should be placed in the columns for $Δy$ and $Δz$ in the table below if the step size is $Δx=2?$\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ |  |  | {{field-1}} |  |  | {{field-2}} |\n\nTherefore, $y(2)≈$\n{{field-3}}\nand $z(2)≈$\n{{field-4}}." 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186167247 1074 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=f(x,y,z),\\, & y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′}=g(x,y,z),\\, & z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEuler's method approximates the solution by applying the update rule component-wise to each equation. More precisely, the increments on $y$ and $z$ are\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}Δy={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx, \\\\ Δz={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe new values are then given by ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}=z+Δz.$\n\nFirst, because the initial conditions are $y(0)=1$ and $z(0)=0,$ we place $y=1$ and $z=0$ in the table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ |  |  | $0$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute both ${y}^{′}$ and ${z}^{′}$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =4x+y-z \\\\  & =4\\cdot 0+1-0 \\\\  & =0+1-0 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =x+4y+z \\\\  & =0+4\\cdot 1+0 \\\\  & =0+4+0 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ |  | $0$ | $4$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ and $Δz$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =4\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $0$ | $4$ | $8$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x,$ $Δy$ to $y,$ and $Δz$ to $z,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+2=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+2=3 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=0+8=8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $0$ | $4$ | $8$ |\n| $2$ | $3$ |  |  | $8$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(2)≈3$ and $z(2)≈8.$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186167248 1068 #edb/ref 17592186164524 13194139533415 17592186045585 false] [17592186167248 1068 #edb/ref 17592186164527 13194139533415 17592186045585 false] [17592186167248 1068 #edb/ref 17592186164530 13194139533415 17592186045585 false] [17592186167248 1068 #edb/ref 17592186164533 13194139533415 17592186045585 false] [17592186167248 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307579 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186167248 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307792 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186167248 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308102 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186167248 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308239 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186167248 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308277 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186167248 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308320 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186167248 