d-3}}" 13194139533415 17592186045585 false] [17592186217785 1071 "The function ${y}_{0}(x)=-x$ is a particular solution of the Riccati differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-{y}^{2}-(2x+1)y-(1+x+{x}^{2}).\n$$\n\nExpress this equation in the Bernoulli form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x){z}^{n}\n$$\n\nand state the functions $P(x)$ and $Q(x),$ and the value of $n.$\n\n$P(x)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$,\\,Q(x)=$\n{{field-2}}\n$,\\,n=$\n{{field-3}}" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186217785 1074 "A Riccati differential equation has the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=a(x){y}^{2}+b(x)y+c(x).\n$$\n\nTo reduce the given Riccati equation to a Bernoulli equation, we use the substitution\n\n$$\ny(x)=z(x)+{y}_{0}(x),\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{0}(x)$ is a known particular solution.\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)={z}^{′}(x)+{y}_{0}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nIn our case, ${y}_{0}(x)=-x.$ Since ${y}_{0}^{′}(x)=-1$, we have\n\n$$\ny=z-x\\,⇒\\,{y}^{′}={z}^{′}-1.\n$$\n\nLet's substitute these expressions into our Riccati equation:\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}-1=-(z-x)^{2}-(2x+1)(-x+z)-(1+x+{x}^{2}).\n$$\n\nNow, we expand the right-hand side, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{′}-1 & =-({x}^{2}+{z}^{2}-2xz)-(-2{x}^{2}+2xz-x+z)-(1+x+{x}^{2}) \\\\ {z}^{′}-1 & =-{z}^{2}-z-1 \\\\ {z}^{′}+z & =-{z}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a Bernoulli equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x){z}^{n}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(x)=1,\\,Q(x)=-1,\\,n=2.\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186217786 1068 #edb/ref 17592186216558 13194139533415 17592186045585 false] [17592186217786 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308278 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186217786 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186217786 1071 "Which of the following equations is a Riccati differential equation?\n\n- ${y}^{′}={y}^{3}-2xy+4$\n- ${y}^{′}=6x{y}^{2}+3y-x$\n- ${y}^{′}+5y=2{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+7$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186217786 1071 "Which of the following equations is a Riccati differential equation?\n\n1. $y^{′} = y^{3} - 2xy + 4$\n2. $y^{′} = 6xy^{2} + 3y - x$\n3. $y^{′} + 5y = 2x^{2}y^{2} + 7$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 false] [17592186217786 1074 "A first-order differential equation is a Riccati differential equation if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=a(x){y}^{2}+b(x)y+c(x).\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is not a Riccati differential equation. Writing the equation in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={y}^{3}-2xy+4.\n$$\n\nThis is not a Riccati differential equation since it includes a ${y}^{3}$ term.\n\n- Equation II is a Riccati differential equation with\n\n$$\na(x)=6x,\\,b(x)=3,\\,c(x)=-x.\n$$\n\n- Equation III is a Riccati differential equation. Writing the equation in standard form, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+7-5y.\n$$\n\nThis is a Riccati differential equation with\n\n$$\na(x)=2{x}^{2},\\,b(x)=-5,\\,c(x)=7.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186217787 1068 #edb/ref 17592186216564 13194139533415 17592186045585 false] [17592186217787 1068 #edb/ref 17592186216565 13194139533415 17592186045585 false] [17592186217787 1068 #edb/ref 17592186216566 13194139533415 17592186045585 false] [17592186217787 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307678 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186217787 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307800 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186217787 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308167 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186217787 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186217787 1071 "The function $y_{0}(x) = 1$ is a particular solution of the Riccati differential equation\n$y^{′} = y^{2} - 3y + 2$.\nExpress this equation in the Bernoulli form\n$\\frac{dz}{dx} + P(x)z = Q(x)z^{n}$\nand state the functions $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$, and the value of $n$.$P(x) =$\n{{field-1}}\n$, Q(x) =$\n{{field-2}}\n$, n =$\n{{field-3}}" 13194139533415 17592186045585 false] [17592186217787 1071 "The function ${y}_{0}(x)=1$ is a particular solution of the Riccati differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={y}^{2}-3y+2.\n$$\n\nExpress this equation in the Bernoulli form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x){z}^{n}\n$$\n\nand state the functions $P(x)$ and $Q(x),$ and the value of $n.$\n\n$P(x)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$,\\,Q(x)=$\n{{field-2}}\n$,\\,n=$\n{{field-3}}" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186217787 1074 "A Riccati differential equation has the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=a(x){y}^{2}+b(x)y+c(x).\n$$\n\nTo reduce the given Riccati equation to a Bernoulli equation, we use the substitution\n\n$$\ny(x)=z(x)+{y}_{0}(x),\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{0}(x)$ is a known particular solution.\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)={z}^{′}(x)+{y}_{0}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nIn our case, ${y}_{0}(x)=1.$ Since ${y}_{0}^{′}(x)=0$, we have\n\n$$\ny=z+1\\,⇒\\,{y}^{′}={z}^{′}.\n$$\n\nLet's substitute these expressions into our Riccati equation:\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}=(z+1)^{2}-3(z+1)+2.\n$$\n\nNow, we expand the right-hand side, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{′} & =(z+1)^{2}-3(z+1)+2 \\\\ {z}^{′} & ={z}^{2}-z \\\\ {z}^{′}+z & ={z}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a Bernoulli equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x){z}^{n}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(x)=1,\\,Q(x)=1,\\,n=2.\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186217790 1068 #edb/ref 17592186216579 13194139533415 17592186045585 false] [17592186217790 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307968 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186217790 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186217790 1071 "Find the general solution of the Clairaut differential equation\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+4({y}^{′})^{2}-{y}^{′}.\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186217790 1071 "Find the general solution of the Clairaut differential equation\n$y = xy^{′} + 4(y^{′})^{2} - y^{′}$." 13194139533415 17592186045585 false] [17592186217790 1074 "To solve the Clairaut differential equation\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+4({y}^{′})^{2}-{y}^{′},\n$$\n\nwe start by setting\n$p={y}^{′}.$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny=xp+4{p}^{2}-p.\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =(xp+4{p}^{2}-p)^{′} \\\\ p & =p+x{p}^{′}+8p{p}^{′}-{p}^{′} \\\\ 0 & ={p}^{′}(x+8p-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe product on the right-hand side equals zero, which means at least one of the factors must be zero.\n\nThe general solution of the Clairaut differential equation is obtained if ${p}^{′}=0.$ Then, $p$ is constant, say \n\n$$\np=c.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $p=c$ into $y=xp+4{p}^{2}-p,$ we get the general solution\n\n$$\ny=cx+4{c}^{2}-c.\n$$\n\nThis represents a family of straight lines, one line for each constant value of $c.$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186217791 1068 #edb/ref 17592186216585 13194139533415 17592186045585 false] [17592186217791 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307912 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186217791 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186217791 1071 "Find the singular solution to the following Clairaut differential equation:\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+({y}^{′})^{2}.\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186217791 1071 "Find the singular solution to the following Clairaut differential equation:\n$y = xy^{′} + (y^{′})^{2}$." 13194139533415 17592186045585 false] [17592186217791 1074 "A Clairaut differential equation has the form\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $g$ is a differentiable function of ${y}^{′}.$\n\nTo solve the Clairaut differential equation\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+({y}^{′})^{2},\n$$\n\nwe start by setting\n$p={y}^{′}.$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny=xp+{p}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =(xp+{p}^{2})^{′} \\\\ p & =p+x{p}^{′}+2p{p}^{′} \\\\ 0 & ={p}^{′}(x+2p)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe product on the right-hand side equals zero, so at least one factor must be zero.\n\nThe singular solution of the Clairaut differential equation is obtained if $x+2p=0.$ Then,\n\n$$\nx=-2p.\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute this into $y=xp+{p}^{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =xp+{p}^{2} \\\\  & =(-2p)p+{p}^{2} \\\\  & =-2{p}^{2}+{p}^{2} \\\\  & =-{p}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the singular solution is given parametrically by\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-2p \\\\ y=-{p}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo eliminate $p,$ we first solve the first equation for $p:$\n\n$$\np=-\\frac{x}{2}\n$$\n\nNext, let's substitute this expression for $p$ into the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-{p}^{2} \\\\  & =-{(-\\frac{x}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the explicit form of the singular solution is\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}.\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186221407 1038 #edb/ref 17592186308645 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186221407 1038 #edb/ref 17592186308646 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186221407 1038 #edb/ref 17592186308647 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186221407 1038 #edb/ref 17592186308648 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186221408 1038 #edb/ref 17592186308669 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186221408 1038 #edb/ref 17592186308670 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186221408 1038 #edb/ref 17592186308671 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186221408 1038 #edb/ref 17592186308672 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186221408 1038 #edb/ref 17592186308673 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186221409 1038 #edb/ref 17592186308499 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186221409 1038 #edb/ref 17592186308682 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186221409 1038 #edb/ref 17592186308683 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186221409 1038 #edb/ref 17592186308684 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186221409 1038 #edb/ref 17592186308685 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17