,\\,y(a)=c,\\,{y}^{′}(a)=d,\n$$\n\nwe can use Euler's method to approximate a solution by first reducing the second-order ODE to a system of first-order ODEs,\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′}=f(x,y,z)\\, & z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nbefore applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nTo apply Euler’s method, we first isolate ${y}^{″}$ so it can serve as the derivative of a new variable $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{y}^{″}+3y{y}^{′} & =2x \\\\ x{y}^{″} & =2x-3y{y}^{′} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =\\frac{2x-3y{y}^{′}}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variable $z={y}^{′},{z}^{′}={y}^{″}$ in the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}=\\frac{2x-3yz}{x}.\n$$\n\nNote that the initial value of $z$ is $z(1)={y}^{′}(1)=-2.$\nSo, the initial value problem reduces to the following system of first-order ODEs:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(1)=0, \\\\ {z}^{′}=\\frac{2x-3yz}{x},\\, & z(1)=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed with Euler's method by applying the update rule component-wise.\n\nSince we want to find the values of $y$ and ${y}^{′}=z$ at $x=\\frac{5}{4},$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{5}{4}-1=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we place the initial condition data in the table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ |  |  | $-2$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute both ${y}^{′}$ and ${z}^{′}$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=-2 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =\\frac{2x-3yz}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot 1-3\\cdot 0\\cdot (-2)}{1} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $-2$ |  | $-2$ | $2$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δy$ and $Δz$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $-2$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $-2$ | $2$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x,$ $Δy$ to $y,$ and $Δz$ to $z,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+\\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=0+(-\\frac{1}{2})=-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=(-2)+\\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $-2$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $-2$ | $2$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{5}{4}$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ |  |  | $-\\frac{3}{2}$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(\\frac{5}{4})≈-\\frac{1}{2}$\nand\n${y}^{′}(\\frac{5}{4})=z(\\frac{5}{4})≈-\\frac{3}{2}.$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186221964 1068 #edb/ref 17592186220398 13194139533415 17592186045585 false] [17592186221964 1068 #edb/ref 17592186220399 13194139533415 17592186045585 false] [17592186221964 1068 #edb/ref 17592186220400 13194139533415 17592186045585 false] [17592186221964 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307838 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186221964 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308100 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186221964 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308183 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186221964 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186221964 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2x-2y-1,\\,y(0)=-1\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method. Given that the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{4},$ and that the first step has already been computed usin