 our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $-\\frac{3}{4}$ | $1$ |  |  |\n\nFrom the table, we see that the slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})=(0,-1)$ is ${y}_{-1}^{′}=1.$\n\nNext, we compute $Δ{y}_{p}$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot 1-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this value to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $-\\frac{3}{4}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |  |\n\nHence, according to the predictor equation, the initially predicted new $y$-value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed table is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $-\\frac{3}{4}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ |" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186221965 1068 #edb/ref 17592186220401 13194139533415 17592186045585 false] [17592186221965 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308042 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186221965 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186221965 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=4x-2y-2,\\,y(1)=0\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method. Given that the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{4},$ and that one preliminary step has already been computed using the Runge-Kutta method, complete the second row of the table below.\n\n*Hint: The corrector equation of the two-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method is defined as*\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\n*where ${y}_{p}$ denotes the predictor at the current step, and ${y}_{-1}^{′}$ is the estimated slope at the previous point.* \n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{5}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $2$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $2$ | {{field-1}} |" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186221965 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$y^{′} = 4x - 2y - 2, y(1) = 0$\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method. Given that the step size is $Δx = \\frac{1}{4}$, and that one preliminary step has already been computed using the Runge-Kutta method, complete the second row of the table below.\n\n*Hint: The corrector equation of the two-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method is defined as*\n$Δy = \\frac{1}{12}(5y_{p}^{′} + 8y^{′} - y_{-1}^{′}) \\cdot Δx$\n*where $y_{p}$ denotes the predictor at the current step, and $y_{-1}^{′}$ is the estimated slope at the previous point.*\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $y^{′}$ | $Δy_{p}$ | $y_{p}$ | $y_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{5}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $2$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $2$ | {{field-1}} |" 13194139533415 17592186045585 false] [17592186221965 1074 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe $2$-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton (ABM2) method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{-1}^{′}=f({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point (${x}_{-1},{y}_{-1}),$ and\n\n- ${y}_{p}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})$ is the estimated slope at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})=(x+Δx,y+Δ{y}_{p}).$\n\nLooking at the partially filled table, we note the following:\n\n- From the second row, the slope at the current point $(x,y)=(\\frac{5}{4},\\frac{1}{2})$ is ${y}^{′}=2.$\n\n- From the first row, the slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})=(1,0)$ is ${y}_{-1}^{′}=2.$\n\n- From the second row, the slope at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})$ is ${y}_{p}^{′}=2.$\n\nNow, we compute $Δy$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(5\\cdot 2+8\\cdot 2-2)\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{48}\\cdot 24 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed row is as given below.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{5}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $2$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $2$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186221968 1068 #edb/ref 17592186220414 13194139533415 17592186045585 false] [17592186221968 1068 #edb/ref 17592186220418 13194139533415 17592186045585 false] [17592186221968 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308147 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186221968 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308152 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186221968 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186221968 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}-x{y}^{′}+y=0,\\,y(1)=-2,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=1\n$$\n\nUse Euler's Method with two steps to approximate $y(2)$ and ${y}^{′}(2).$\n\n$$\ny(2)≈{{field-1}},\\,{y}^{′}(2)≈{{field-2}}\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186221968 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$x^{2}y^{″} - xy^{′} + y = 0, y(1) =-2, y^{′}(1) = 1$\nUse Euler's Method with two steps to approximate $y(2)$ and $y^{′}(2)$.$-1$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}}\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533415 17592186045585 false] [17592186221968 1074 "For a second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=f(x,y,{y}^{′}),\\,y(a)=c,\\,{y}^{′}(a)=d,\n$$\n\nwe can use Euler's method to approximate a solution by first reducing the second-order ODE to a system of first-order ODEs,\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′}=f(x,y,z)\\, & z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nbefore applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nTo apply Euler’s method, we first isolate ${y}^{″}$ so it can serve as the derivative of a new variable $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{″}-x{y}^{′}+y & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =x{y}^{′}-y \\\\ {y}^{″} & =\\frac{x{y}^{′}-y}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variable $z={y}^{′},{z}^{′}={y}^{″}$ in the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}=\\frac{xz-y}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nNote that the initial value of $z$ is $z(1)={y}^{′}(1)=1.$\nSo, the initial value problem reduces to the following system of first-order ODEs:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(1)=-2, \\\\ {z}^{′}=\\frac{xz-y}{{x}^{2}},\\, & z(1)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed with Euler's method by applying the update rule component-wise.\n\nFirst, note that we are given the values of $y$ and $z={y}^{′}$ at $x=1$ and asked to find their values at $x=2.$ The distance from $x=1$ to $x=2$ is $1,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{2-1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(1)=-2$ and $z(1)=1.$\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-2$ |  |  | $1$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nWe compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy,$ ${z}^{′}$ and $Δz$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the first row), and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}},$ ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=1 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =\\frac{xz-y}{{x}^{2}}=\\frac{1\\cdot 1-(-2)}{{1}^{2}}=3 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=1\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=3\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-2+\\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=1+\\frac{3}{2}=\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-2$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $3$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $-\\frac{3}{2}$ |  |  | $\\frac{5}{2}$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nWe compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy,$ ${z}^{′}$ and $Δz$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the second row), and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}},$ ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ {z}^{′} & =\\frac{xz-y}{{x}^{2}}=\\frac{(3/2)\\cdot (5/2)-(-3/2)}{(-3/2)^{2}}=\\frac{7}{3} \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=\\frac{7}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{7}{6} \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{5}{4}=-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=\\frac{5}{2}+\\frac{7}{6}=\\frac{11}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-2$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $3$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $-\\frac{3}{2}$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ | $\\frac{5}{4}$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ | $\\frac{7}{3}$ | $\\frac{7}{6}$ |\n| $2$ | $-\\frac{1}{4}$ |  |  | $\\frac{11}{3}$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(2)≈-\\frac{1}{4}$\nand\n${y}^{′}(2)=z(2)≈\\frac{11}{3}.$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186221969 1068 #edb/ref 17592186220422 13194139533415 17592186045585 false] [17592186221969 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307762 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186221969 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186221969 1074 "Let's recall the definitions of stability:\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *stable* if for any neighborhood $O$ of ${x}^{∗},$ there exists a neighborhood ${O}_{1}⊆O$ such that every orbit that begins inside ${O}_{1}$ at some point in time $t={t}_{0}$ remains inside $O$ for all $t>{t}_{0}.$\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close, but may not converge to the equilibrium.\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *asymptotically stable* if it is stable and there exists a neighborhood $O$ of ${x}^{∗}$ such that every orbit that begins inside $O$ approaches ${x}^{∗}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions that start sufficiently close to the equilibrium remain close and converge to the equilibrium as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- An equilibrium point ${x}^{∗}$ is called *unstable* if it is not stable.\n\nIn plain words, this means that solutions eventually move away from the equilibrium for some initial conditions arbitrarily close to it.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"Unstable.\"" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186221970 1068 #edb/ref 17592186220428 13194139533415 17592186045585 false] [17592186221970 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308276 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186221970 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186221970 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={e}^{x}-3xy,\\,y(-2)=3\n$$\n\nThe trapezoidal method is applied with step size $Δx=1.$ Find the general formula for the new $y$ value, ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ in terms of the current values $x$ and $y,$ and the new value ${x}_{\\text{new}}.$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186221970 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$y^{′} = e^{x} - 3xy, y(-2) = 3$\nThe trapezoidal method is applied with step size $Δx = 1$. Find the general formula for the new $y$ value, $y_{new}$, in terms of the current values $x$ and $y$, and the new value $x_{new}$." 13194139533415 17592186045585 false] [17592186221970 1074 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nIn this example, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}-3xy+({e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}-3{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}}))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}+{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}-3xy)-\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}+{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}-3xy)-\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}+{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}-3xy+2y)-\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}}+\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}+{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}-3xy+2y) \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}(2+3{x}_{\\text{new}}) & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}+{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}-3xy+2y) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{{e}^{x}+{e}^{{x}_{\\text{new}}}-3xy+2y}{2+3{x}_{\\text{new}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186221981 1068 #edb/ref 17592186220503 13194139533415 17592186045585 false] [17592186221981 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307655 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186221981 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186221981 1071 "Which of the following local truncation error expressions represent a first-order numerical method?\n\n- $τ(Δx)=6(Δx)^{3}$\n- $τ(Δx)=0.3(Δx)^{2}+2(Δx)^{3}$\n- $τ(Δx)=9(Δx)^{3}+4(Δx)^{5}$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186221981 1071 "Which of the following local truncation error expressions represent a first-order numerical method?\n\n1. $τ(Δx) = 6(Δx)^{3}$\n2. $τ(Δx) = 0.3(Δx)^{2} + 2(Δx)^{3}$\n3. $τ(Δx) = 9(Δx)^{3} + 4(Δx)^{5}$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 false] [17592186221981 1074 "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option in turn:\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=6(Δx)^{3},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $6(Δx)^{3}=6(Δx)^{2+1}.$ So, statement I corresponds to an order $2$ method.\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=0.3(Δx)^{2}+2(Δx)^{3},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $0.3(Δx)^{2}=0.3(Δx)^{1+1}.$ So, statement II corresponds to an order $1$ method.\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=9(Δx)^{3}+4(Δx)^{5},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $9(Δx)^{3}=9(Δx)^{2+1}.$ So, statement III corresponds to an order $2$ method.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186221984 1068 #edb/ref 17592186220521 13194139533415 17592186045585 false] [17592186221984 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308232 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186221984 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186221984 1071 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)={x}^{2}(t)-2x(t)+y(t)-1 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=3x(t)-{y}^{2}(t)+4y(t)-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of ODEs above. An equilibrium point of the system is $[1,\\,2]^{T}.$ Which of the following is the system obtained by shifting this equilibrium to the origin?" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186221984 1071 "${x^{′}(t) = x^{2}(t) - 2x(t) + y(t) - 1; y^{′}(t) = 3x(t) - y^{2}(t) + 4y(t) - 7$\nConsider the system of ODEs above. An equilibrium point of the system is $[1, 2]^{T}$. Which of the following is the system obtained by shifting this equilibrium to the origin?" 13194139533415 17592186045585 false] [17592186221984 1074 "To shift the system's equilibrium from ${x}^{∗}$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗},\n$$\n\nwhere $x=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]$ and $u=[\\begin{aligned}u(t) \\\\ v(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nu=x-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,⇒\\,x=u+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}={u}^{′}.\n$$\n\nIn coordinate form, this can be written as follows:\n\n$$\nx(t)=u(t)+1,\\,y(t)=v(t)+2,\\,{x}^{′}(t)={u}^{′}(t),\\,{v}^{′}(t)={y}^{′}(t)\n$$\n\nLet's now substitute these into the given system.\n\n- Substituting into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & ={x}^{2}(t)-2x(t)+y(t)-1 \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & =(u(t)+1)^{2}(t)-2(u(t)+1)+(v(t)+2)-1 \\\\  & ={u}^{2}(t)+2u(t)+1-2u(t)-2+v(t)+2-1 \\\\  & ={u}^{2}(t)+v(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Substituting into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(t) & =3x(t)-{y}^{2}(t)+4y(t)-7 \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) & =3(u(t)+1)-(v(t)+2)^{2}+4(v(t)+2)-7 \\\\  & =3u(t)+3-{v}^{2}(t)-4v(t)-4+4v(t)+8-7 \\\\  & =3u(t)-{v}^{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)={u}^{2}(t)+v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=3u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186221986 1068 #edb/ref 17592186220533 13194139533415 17592186045585 false] [17592186221986 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308145 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186221986 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186221986 1071 "A linear system of ODEs has an equilibrium at $x^{∗}$. One of the nullclines of the system has the equation $y = 2x - 3$ in the corresponding phase space. The system is then shifted using $u = x - x^{∗}$. Which of the following is **guaranteed** to be a nullcline of the shifted system?" 13194139533415 17592186045585 false] [17592186221986 1071 "A linear system of ODEs has an equilibrium at ${x}^{∗}.$ One of the nullclines of the system has the equation $y=2x-3$ in the corresponding phase space. The system is then shifted using $u=x-{x}^{∗}.$ Which of the following is **guaranteed** to be a nullcline of the shifted system?" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186221986 1074 "After shifting a system, the geometric structure of the vector field is preserved. In particular, each nullcline is translated but retains its orientation, meaning its slope does not change.\n\nBecause the equilibrium is moved to the origin by the shift, the translated nullclines must pass through the origin in the shifted coordinates.\n\nThus, the nullclines of the shifted system pass through the origin and have the same slopes as the corresponding nullclines of the original system.\n\nAmong the given options, the only line that satisfied the above conditions is $y=2x.$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186221988 1068 #edb/ref 17592186220545 13194139533415 17592186045585 false] [17592186221988 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308008 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186221988 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186221988 1071 "Which of the following global truncation error expressions represent a second-order numerical method?\n\n- $e(Δx)=6(Δx)^{2}$\n- $e(Δx)=0.3Δx+2(Δx)^{2}$\n- $e(Δx)=9(Δx)^{2}+4(Δx)^{5}$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186221988 1071 "Which of the following global truncation error expressions represent a second-order numerical method?\n\n1. $e(Δx) = 6(Δx)^{2}$\n2. $e(Δx) = 0.3Δx + 2(Δx)^{2}$\n3. $e(Δx) = 9(Δx)^{2} + 4(Δx)^{5}$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 false] [17592186221988 1074 "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option in turn:\n\n- For $e(Δx)=6(Δx)^{2},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $6(Δx)^{2}.$ So, statement I corresponds to an order $2$ method.\n\n- For $e(Δx)=0.3Δx+2(Δx)^{2},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $0.3Δx.$ So, statement II corresponds to an order $1$ method.\n\n- For $e(Δx)=9(Δx)^{2}+4(Δx)^{5},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is  $9(Δx)^{2}.$ So, statement III corresponds to an order $2$ method.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"." 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186221990 1068 #edb/ref 17592186220552 13194139533415 17592186045585 false] [17592186221990 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307731 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186221990 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186221990 1071 "$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe Wronskian of the vector-valued functions given above is $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=${{field-1}}." 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186221990 1071 "$x_{1}(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}e^{t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}, x_{2}(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ e^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}$\nThe Wronskian of the vector-valued functions given above is $W(x_{1}, x_{2}) =$ {{field-1}}." 13194139533415 17592186045585 false] [17592186221990 1074 "The Wronskian $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})$ of two vector-valued functions \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t)\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{22}(t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis defined as\n\n$$\nW({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=det({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11}(t) & {x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t) & {x}_{22}(t)\\end{aligned}|.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Wronskian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({x}_{1},{x}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={e}^{t}\\cdot {e}^{-t}-0\\cdot 0 \\\\  & ={e}^{t-t} \\\\  & ={e}^{0} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186221992 1068 #edb/ref 17592186220559 13194139533415 17592186045585 false] [17592186221992 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307690 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186221992 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186221992 1071 "A numerical method is used to approximate the solution to an initial value problem.\n\n- With step size $Δx = 0.08$, the observed local truncation error is $τ(0.08) = 0.13$.\n- With step size $Δx = 0.02$, the observed local truncation error is $τ(0.02) = 0.002$.\n\nEstimate the order of the numerical method.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533415 17592186045585 false] [17592186221992 1071 "A numerical method is used to approximate the solution to an initial value problem.\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.08,$ the observed local truncation error is $τ(0.08)=0.13.$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.02,$ the observed local truncation error is $τ(0.02)=0.002.$\n\nEstimate the order of the numerical method." 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186221992 1074 "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.08$ to $0.02.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.08}{0.02}=4.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the LTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p+1}={4}^{p+1}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the LTE actually decreases from $0.13$ to $0.002.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.13}{0.002}=65.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the LTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4}^{p+1} & ≈65 \\\\ p+1 & ≈{log}_{4}⁡(65) \\\\ p+1 & ≈3.011… \\\\ p & ≈2.011….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is $2.$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186221993 1068 #edb/ref 17592186220565 13194139533415 17592186045585 false] [17592186221993 1068 #edb/ref 17592186220571 13194139533415 17592186045585 false] [17592186221993 1068 #edb/ref 17592186220577 13194139533415 17592186045585 false] [17592186221993 1068 #edb/ref 17592186220583 13194139533415 17592186045585 false] [17592186221993 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307530 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186221993 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307660 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186221993 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307909 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186221993 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308062 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186221993 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186221993 1071 "The local truncation error (LTE) of a numerical method with step size $Δx$ is\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=7(Δx)^{4}-2(Δx)^{5}.\n$$\n\nComplete the following sentences.$\\,$ With step size $Δx=0.5,$ the local truncation error is\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ With step size $Δx=0.25,$ the local truncation error is $≈$\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Decreasing from $Δx=0.5$ to $Δx=0.25,$ the LTE is reduced by a factor of $≈$\n{{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ According to this data, the order of the method is approximately\n{{field-4}}." 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186221993 1071 "The local truncation error (LTE) of a numerical method with step size $Δx$ is\n$τ(Δx) = 7(Δx)^{4} - 2(Δx)^{5}$.\nComplete the following sentences. With step size $Δx = 0.5$, the local truncation error is\n{{field-1}}.\n\nWith step size $Δx = 0.25$, the local truncation error is $\\approx$\n{{field-2}}.\n\nDecreasing from $Δx = 0.5$ to $Δx = 0.25$, the LTE is reduced by a factor of $\\approx$\n{{field-3}}.\n\nAccording to this data, the order of the method is approximately\n{{field-4}}.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533415 17592186045585 false] [17592186221993 1074 "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nFirst, we compute the LTE for the given step sizes:\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.5,$ the LTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}τ(0.5) & =7(0.5)^{4}-2(0.5)^{5} \\\\  & =7\\cdot 0.062\\,5-2\\cdot 0.031\\,25 \\\\  & =0.375.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.25,$ the LTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}τ(0.25) & =7(0.25)^{4}-2(0.25)^{5} \\\\  & =7\\cdot (0.003\\,906…)-2\\cdot (0.000\\,976…) \\\\  & =0.025\\,39… \\\\  & ≈0.025.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the order by comparing the errors. The step size decreases from $0.5$ to $0.25.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.5}{0.25}=2.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the LTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p+1}={2}^{p+1}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the LTE actually decreases from $0.375$ to $0.025\\,39….$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.375}{(0.025\\,39…)}=14.769\\,23…≈14.769.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the LTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{p+1} & ≈14.769\\,23… \\\\ p+1 & ≈{log}_{2}⁡(14.769\\,23…) \\\\ p+1 & ≈3.884… \\\\ p & ≈2.884….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is\n$3.$\n\nIndeed, this coincides with the definition of the order from the expression for the LTE:\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=7(Δx)^{4}-2(Δx)^{5}\\,⇒\\,τ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{4})=O\\,((Δx)^{3+1}),\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nHence, the order of the method is $3.$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186221995 1068 #edb/ref 17592186220593 13194139533415 17592186045585 false] [17592186221995 1068 #edb/ref 17592186220594 13194139533415 17592186045585 false] [17592186221995 1068 #edb/ref 17592186220595 13194139533415 17592186045585 false] [17592186221995 1068 #edb/ref 17592186220596 13194139533415 17592186045585 false] [17592186221995 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307706 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186221995 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307707 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186221995 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307877 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186221995 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308295 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186221995 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186221995 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear ODEs ${x}^{′}=Ax$ with the matrix $A$ shown above. Compute the **standard** fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ for this system.\n\n| ${Φ}_{0}(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186221995 1071 "$A = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the system of linear ODEs $x^{′} = Ax$ with the matrix $A$ shown above. Compute the **standard** fundamental matrix $Φ_{0}(t)$ for this system.\n\n| $Φ_{0}(t) =$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |" 13194139533415 17592186045585 false] [17592186221995 1074 "Suppose $A$ is a constant $n\\times n$ matrix and $x(t)$ is an $n\\times 1$ column vector. The standard fundamental matrix of the linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ denoted ${Φ}_{0}(t),$ is the unique matrix solution of the initial value problem \n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}^{′}(t)=A{Φ}_{0}(t),\\,{Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere ${I}_{n}$ is the identity matrix. \n\nFurthermore, it can be shown that ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}.$\n\nWe have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}]t+{\\alpha }_{0}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0} & 2{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ -2{\\alpha }_{1}t & {\\alpha }_{0}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are constants to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues of $At$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=2\\text{i}t,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-2\\text{i}t,\n$$\n\nwhich are complex conjugates. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r((2\\text{i})t) & ={e}^{(2\\text{i})t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}(2\\text{i})t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{0}+2\\text{i}{\\alpha }_{1}t & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we equate the real and imaginary parts, and solve for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ 2{\\alpha }_{1}t=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}=\\frac{1}{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ 2{\\alpha }_{1}t & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\cdot t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186221997 1068 #edb/ref 17592186220613 13194139533415 17592186045585 false] [17592186221997 1068 #edb/ref 17592186220618 13194139533415 17592186045585 false] [17592186221997 1068 #edb/ref 17592186220623 13194139533415 17592186045585 false] [17592186221997 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308005 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186221997 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308018 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186221997 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308251 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186221997 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186221997 1071 "Consider the system $x^{′}(t) = Ax(t)$, where $A$ is a constant $2 \\cdot 2$ matrix. Let $Φ(t)$ be a fundamental matrix for this system. A derivation showing that every solution $x(t)$ can be written in the form $x(t) = Φ(t)c$ for some constant vector $c = [c_{1}, c_{2}]^{T}$ is given below.\n\n> $L1$: Let $x_{1}(t)$ and $x_{2}(t)$ be the columns of $Φ(t)$\n> $L2$: $x_{1}(t)$ and $x_{2}(t)$ form a basis of solutions\n> $L3$: $x(t)$ can be written as $x(t) = c_{1}x_{1}(t) + c_{2}x_{2}(t)$\n> $L4$: $Φ(t)c = c_{1}x_{1}(t) + c_{2}x_{2}(t)$\n> $L5$: $x(t) = Φ(t)c$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\nLine $L2$ follows from $L1$ by the {{field-1}}.\n\nLine $L3$ follows from line $L2$ by {{field-2}}.\n\nLine $L4$ follows from line $L1$ by the {{field-3}}." 13194139533415 17592186045585 false] [17592186221997 1071 "Consider the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant $2\\times 2$ matrix. Let $Φ(t)$ be a fundamental matrix for this system. A derivation showing that every solution $x(t)$ can be written in the form $x(t)=Φ(t)\\,c$ for some constant vector $c=[{c}_{1},\\,{c}_{2}]^{T}$ is given below.\n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ Let ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ be the columns of $Φ(t)$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ form a basis of solutions\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ $x(t)$ can be written as $x(t)={c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)$\n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ $Φ(t)\\,c={c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ $x(t)=Φ(t)\\,c$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L2}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by the {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L3}$ follows from line $\\text{L2}$ by {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L4}$ follows from line $\\text{L1}$ by the {{field-3}}." 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186221997 1074 "Let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first consider lines $\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L2}.$ Since $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix, its columns ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ form a basis of the solution space for our system. Thus, $\\text{L2}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by the $\\text{definition of a fundamental matrix}.$\n\n- Next, we consider lines $\\text{L2}$ and $\\text{L3}.$ Once ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ form a basis, every solution $x(t)$ of the system can be expressed uniquely as a linear combination\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{L3}$ follows from $\\text{L2}$ by $\\text{the definition of a basis}.$\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L4}.$ Since ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are the columns of $Φ(t),$ we can write\n\n$$\nΦ(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\,| & | \\\\ {x}_{1}(t) & {x}_{2}(t) \\\\ \\,| & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThen by the definition of matrix multiplication,\n\n$$\nΦ(t)\\,c=[\\begin{aligned}\\,| & | \\\\ {x}_{1}(t) & {x}_{2}(t) \\\\ \\,| & |\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t).\n$$\n\nThus, $\\text{L4}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by the $\\text{definition of matrix multiplication}.$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186221998 1068 #edb/ref 17592186220628 13194139533415 17592186045585 false] [17592186221998 1068 #edb/ref 17592186220629 13194139533415 17592186045585 false] [17592186221998 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307669 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186221998 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307687 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186221998 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186221998 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\nx{y}^{″}+2y{y}^{′}=3x,\\,y(1)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=-1\n$$\n\nUse Euler's Method with one step to approximate $y(2)$ and ${y}^{′}(2).$\n\n$y(2)≈$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,{y}^{′}(2)≈$\n{{field-2}}" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186221998 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$xy^{″} + 2yy^{′} = 3x, y(1) = 1, y^{′}(1) =-1$\nUse Euler's Method with one step to approximate $y(2)$ and $y^{′}(2)$.$y(2) \\approx$\n{{field-1}},\n$y^{′}(2) \\approx$\n{{field-2}}" 13194139533415 17592186045585 false] [17592186221998 1074 "For a second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=f(x,y,{y}^{′}),\\,y(a)=c,\\,{y}^{′}(a)=d,\n$$\n\nwe can use Euler's method to approximate a solution by first reducing the second-order ODE to a system of first-order ODEs,\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′}=f(x,y,z)\\, & z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nbefore applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nTo apply Euler’s method, we first isolate ${y}^{″}$ so it can serve as the derivative of a new variable $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{y}^{″}+2y{y}^{′} & =3x \\\\ x{y}^{″} & =3x-2y{y}^{′} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =\\frac{3x-2y{y}^{′}}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variable $z={y}^{′},{z}^{′}={y}^{″}$ in the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}=\\frac{3x-2yz}{x}.\n$$\n\nNote that the initial value of $z$ is\n$z(1)={y}^{′}(1)=-1.$\nSo, the initial value problem reduces to the following system of first-order ODEs:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(1)=1, \\\\ {z}^{′}=\\frac{3x-2yz}{x},\\, & z(1)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed with Euler's method by applying the update rule component-wise.\n\nSince we want to find the values of $y$ and ${y}^{′}=z$ at $x=2,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=2-1=1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we place the initial condition data in the table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ |  |  | $-1$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute both ${y}^{′}$ and ${z}^{′}$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=-1 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =\\frac{3x-2yz}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\cdot 1-2\\cdot 1\\cdot (-1)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3+2}{1} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $-1$ |  | $-1$ | $5$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δy$ and $Δz$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-1 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =5\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ | $5$ | $5$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x,$ $Δy$ to $y,$ and $Δz$ to $z,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+(-1)=0 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=(-1)+5=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ | $5$ | $5$ |\n| $2$ | $0$ |  |  | $4$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(2)≈0$\nand ${y}^{′}(2)=z(2)≈4.$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186222002 1068 #edb/ref 17592186220647 13194139533415 17592186045585 false] [17592186222002 1068 #edb/ref 17592186220652 13194139533415 17592186045585 false] [17592186222002 1068 #edb/ref 17592186220655 13194139533415 17592186045585 false] [17592186222002 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307651 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186222002 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308027 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186222002 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308210 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186222002 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186222002 1071 "$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in R\n$$\n\nConsider the vector-valued functions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ above. Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are solutions of the same system of linear ODEs, select the correct options in the reasoning below.$\\,$ The Wronskian is $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=$ \n{{field-1}},\n\n$\\,$ which is\n{{field-2}}.  \n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the set ${{x}_{1}(t),{x}_{2}(t)}$ is linearly \n{{field-3}} \non $R.$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186222002 1071 "$x_{1}(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}\\cos t \\\\ \\sin t\\end{bmatrix}, x_{2}(t) = \\begin{bmatrix}-\\sin t \\\\ \\cos t\\end{bmatrix}, t ∈ R$\nConsider the vector-valued functions $x_{1}(t)$ and $x_{2}(t)$ above. Given that $x_{1}$ and $x_{2}$ are solutions of the same system of linear ODEs, select the correct options in the reasoning below. The Wronskian is $W(x_{1}, x_{2}) =$\n{{field-1}},\n\nwhich is\n{{field-2}}.\n\nTherefore, the set ${x_{1}(t), x_{2}(t)}$ is linearly\n{{field-3}}\non $R$." 13194139533415 17592186045585 false] [17592186222002 1074 "The Wronskian $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})$ of two vector-valued functions \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t)\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{22}(t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis defined as\n\n$$\nW({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=det({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11}(t) & {x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t) & {x}_{22}(t)\\end{aligned}|.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- If the Wronskian is not identically zero, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If the Wronskian is identically zero, and it's known that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are solutions to the same system of linear ODEs, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, our Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({x}_{1},{x}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t & \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot (\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is $\\text{nonzero for at least one }t\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the set ${{x}_{1}(t),{x}_{2}(t)}$ is linearly $\\text{independent}$ on $R.$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186222003 1068 #edb/ref 17592186220658 13194139533415 17592186045585 false] [17592186222003 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307946 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186222003 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186222003 1071 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-x(t)-y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-2x(t)-2y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3,$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ${v}_{1}=[-1,\\text{ }1]^{T}$ and ${v}_{2}=[1,\\text{ }2]^{T},$ respectively. Which of the