08720 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186229656 1068 #edb/ref 17592186228116 13194139533415 17592186045585 false] [17592186229656 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307675 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186229656 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186229656 1071 "Given that $I(t) = \\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}(2x + t^{3})^{2}dx$, calculate $I^{′}(t)$ for $t ∈ R$ by differentiating under the integral sign.$I^{′}(t) =$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533415 17592186045585 false] [17592186229656 1071 "Given that $I(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+{t}^{3})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x,$ calculate ${I}^{′}(t)$ for $t\\in R$ by differentiating under the integral sign.\n\n${I}^{′}(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186229656 1074 "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWe wish to compute ${I}^{′}(t),$ where\n\n$$\nI(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+{t}^{3})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating under the integral sign, we get\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(2x+{t}^{3})^{2}]\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNext, let's compute the partial derivative with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(2x+{t}^{3})^{2}] & =2(2x+{t}^{3})\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂t}(2x+{t}^{3}) \\\\  & =2(2x+{t}^{3})\\cdot 3{t}^{2} \\\\  & =6{t}^{2}(2x+{t}^{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this back into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{′}(t) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}6{t}^{2}(2x+{t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6{t}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+{t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's evaluate the remaining integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+{t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2x\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}{|}_{0}^{1}+{t}^{3}x{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =1+{t}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}(t)=6{t}^{2}(1+{t}^{3}).\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186229660 1068 #edb/ref 17592186228125 13194139533415 17592186045585 false] [17592186229660 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307628 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186229660 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186229660 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2x-{y}^{3},\\,y(0.5)=1\n$$\n\nUse the trapezoidal method with one step and Newton's method to approximate $y(1).$ Round your answer to two decimal places.\n\n$y(1)≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186229660 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$y^{′} = 2x - y^{3}, y(0.5) = 1$\nUse the trapezoidal method with one step and Newton's method to approximate $y(1)$. Round your answer to two decimal places.$y(1) \\approx$\n{{field-1}}\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533415 17592186045585 false] [17592186229660 1074 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nSince we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=1,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=1-0.5=0.5.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}((2x-{y}^{3})+(2{x}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}))\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =0.5x-0.25{y}^{3}+0.5{x}_{\\text{new}}-0.25({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+0.5x-0.25{y}^{3}+0.5{x}_{\\text{new}}-0.25({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand moving all terms to the left-hand side gives the equation\n\n$$\n0.25({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+{y}_{\\text{new}}+0.25{y}^{3}-y-0.5{x}_{\\text{new}}-0.5x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, the general form of the function $g$ for step size $Δx=0.5$ is\n\n$$\ng({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.25({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+{y}_{\\text{new}}+0.25{y}^{3}-y-0.5{x}_{\\text{new}}-0.5x.\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is\n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=0.5+0.5=1.\n$$\n\nThen, the function $g$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({y}_{\\text{new}}) & =0.25({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+{y}_{\\text{new}}+0.25(1)^{3}-1-0.5\\cdot 1-0.5\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =0.25({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-1.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use a root-finding method with starting point $y=1$ to solve $g({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.$ We'll use Newton's method:\n\n$$\n({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n+1}=({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-\\frac{g({y}_{\\text{new}})}{{g}^{′}({y}_{\\text{new}})}\n$$\n\nNote that the derivative of $g$ is\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.75({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton's method iterative scheme is as follows:\n\n$$\n({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n+1}=({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-\\frac{0.25(({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n})^{3}+({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-1.5}{0.75(({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n})^{2}+1}\n$$\n\nApplying several iterations of Newton's method starting with $y(0.5)=1,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({y}_{\\text{new}})_{1} & =1 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{2} & ≈1.142\\,857 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{3} & ≈1.134\\,757 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{4} & ≈1.134\\,728\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(1)≈1.13,$ rounded to two decimal places." 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186229661 1068 #edb/ref 17592186228126 13194139533415 17592186045585 false] [17592186229661 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307982 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186229661 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186229661 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2x{y}^{2},\\,y(1)=1\n$$\n\nUsing the trapezoidal method with step size $Δx=1,$ the new value ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ is obtained by solving the equation $g({y}_{\\text{new}})=0$ at each step. Which of the following is the **specific function** $g({y}_{\\text{new}})$ used in the first step?" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186229661 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$y^{′} = 2xy^{2}, y(1) = 1$\nUsing the trapezoidal method with step size $Δx = 1$, the new value $y_{new}$ is obtained by solving the equation $g(y_{new}) = 0$ at each step. Which of the following is the **specific function** $g(y_{new})$ used in the first step?" 13194139533415 17592186045585 false] [17592186229661 1074 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nIn this example, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2x{y}^{2}+2{x}_{\\text{new}}({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2})\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =x{y}^{2}+{x}_{\\text{new}}({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+x{y}^{2}+{x}_{\\text{new}}({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand moving all terms to the left-hand side gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}_{\\text{new}}-{x}_{\\text{new}}({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-y-x{y}^{2}=0.\n$$\n\nSo, the general form of the function $g$ for step size $Δx=1$ is\n\n$$\ng({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-{x}_{\\text{new}}({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-y-x{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is\n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=1+1=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function used in the first step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({y}_{\\text{new}}) & ={y}_{\\text{new}}-2({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-1-1(1)^{2} \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{new}}-2({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186229662 1068 #edb/ref 17592186228132 13194139533415 17592186045585 false] [17592186229662 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308047 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186229662 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186229662 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=4{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y},\\,y(0)=2\n$$\n\nUsing the trapezoidal method with step size $Δx=0.5,$ the new value ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ is obtained by solving the equation $g({y}_{\\text{new}})=0$ at each step. Which of the following is the **general form** of $g({y}_{\\text{new}})$ in terms of the new values ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and the current values $x$ and $y?$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186229662 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n$y^{′} = 4x^{2}\\sqrt{y}, y(0) = 2$\nUsing the trapezoidal method with step size $Δx = 0.5$, the new value $y_{new}$ is obtained by solving the equation $g(y_{new}) = 0$ at each step. Which of the following is the **general form** of $g(y_{new})$ in terms of the new values $x_{new}$ and $y_{new}$, and the current values $x$ and $y$?" 13194139533415 17592186045585 false] [17592186229662 1074 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nIn this example, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & 