t}x(t)$  \n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${y}^{′}(t)=-A{e}^{-At}x(t)+{e}^{-At}{x}^{′}(t)$  \n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ ${y}^{′}(t)={e}^{-At}g(t)$  \n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ $y(t)-y(0)={\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{-As}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ $y(0)=x(0)$ \n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ $y(t)=x(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{-As}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s$  \n\n$\\text{L7}:\\,$ ${e}^{At}y(t)={e}^{At}x(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s$\n\n$\\text{L8}:\\,$ ${e}^{At}y(t)=x(t)$\n\n$\\text{L9}:\\,$ $x(t)={e}^{At}x(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s$\n\nSelect the correct options to justify each step of the reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L2}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by  \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L4}$ follows from line\n{{field-2}}\nby integrating both sides from $0$ to \n{{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L7}$ follows from line $\\text{L6}$ by  \n{{field-4}}." 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186229668 1071 "Consider the inhomogeneous system $x^{′}(t) = Ax(t) + g(t)$, where $A$ is a constant square matrix. A derivation of the formula for the solution $x(t)$ passing through $x(0)$ is presented below.\n\n> $L1$: Let $y(t) = e^{-At}x(t)$\n> $L2$: $y^{′}(t) =-Ae^{-At}x(t) + e^{-At}x^{′}(t)$\n> $L3$: $y^{′}(t) = e^{-At}g(t)$\n> $L4$: $y(t) - y(0) = \\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{t}e^{-As}g(s)ds$\n> $L5$: $y(0) = x(0)$\n> $L6$: $y(t) = x(0) + \\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{t}e^{-As}g(s)ds$\n> $L7$: $e^{At}y(t) = e^{At}x(0) + \\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{t}e^{A(t - s)}g(s)ds$\n> $L8$: $e^{At}y(t) = x(t)$\n> $L9$: $x(t) = e^{At}x(0) + \\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{t}e^{A(t - s)}g(s)ds$\n\nSelect the correct options to justify each step of the reasoning.\n\nLine $L2$ follows from $L1$ by\n{{field-1}}.\n\nLine $L4$ follows from line\n{{field-2}}\nby integrating both sides from $0$ to\n{{field-3}}.\n\nLine $L7$ follows from line $L6$ by\n{{field-4}}." 13194139533415 17592186045585 false] [17592186229668 1074 "Let's justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first consider lines $\\text{L1}$ and $\\text{L2}.$ Differentiating $y(t)={e}^{-At}x(t)$ and applying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(t) & =({e}^{-At})^{′}x(t)+{e}^{-At}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(-A){e}^{-At}x(t)+{e}^{-At}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =-A{e}^{-At}x(t)+{e}^{-At}{x}^{′}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $\\text{L2}$ follows from $\\text{L1}$ by $\\text{differentiating and using the product rule}.$\n\n- Next, we consider lines $\\text{L3}$ and $\\text{L4}.$ Integrating ${y}^{′}(t)={e}^{-At}g(t)$ from $0$ to $t$ in $\\text{L3}$ yields\n\n$$\ny(t)-y(0)={\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{-As}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSo, $\\text{L4}$ follows from $\\text{L3}$ by integrating both sides from $0$ to $t.$\n\n- Finally, we consider lines $\\text{L6}$ and $\\text{L7}.$ Multiplying both sides of\n\n$$\ny(t)=x(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{-As}g(s)\\,ds\n$$\n\nfrom $\\text{L6}$ by ${e}^{At}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At}y(t) & ={e}^{At}x(0)+{e}^{At}{\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{-As}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={e}^{At}x(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{L7}$ follows from $\\text{L6}$ by $\\text{multiplying both sides by }{e}^{At}.$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186229673 1068 #edb/ref 17592186228202 13194139533415 17592186045585 false] [17592186229673 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308031 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186229673 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186229673 1071 "For which of the following functions is it valid to differentiate under the integral sign?\n\n- ${F}_{1}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{tx}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in R$\n- ${F}_{2}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\ge 1$\n- ${F}_{3}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}|x-t|\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\ge 0$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186229673 1071 "For which of the following functions is it valid to differentiate under the integral sign?\n\n1. $F_{1}(t) = \\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}e^{tx}\\sin xdx, t ∈ R$\n2. $F_{2}(t) = \\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}\\frac{x^{2}}{x^{2} + t^{2}}dx, t \\ge 1$\n3. $F_{3}(t) = \\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1} \\mid x - t \\mid dx, t \\ge 0$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 false] [17592186229673 1074 "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each function:\n\n- For the first function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x,t)={e}^{tx}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in R$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{1}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂t}=x{e}^{tx}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{1}^{′}(t)$ for all real $t.$\n\n- For the second function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x,t)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}>1$ and choose $[c,d]⊂[1,\\infty )$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{2}$ is continuous on $R$ (since the denominator never vanishes on $R$). Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂t}=-\\frac{2t{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+{t}^{2})^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{2}^{′}(t)$ for all real $t\\ge 1.$\n\n- For the third function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x,t)=|x-t|.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in (0,1)$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d$ such that $[c,d]⊆[0,1],$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{3}$ is continuous on $R.$ However, the partial derivative\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂t}={\\begin{aligned}-1,\\,x>t \\\\ 1,\\,x<t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ndoes *not* exist whenever $x=t={t}_{0},$ and is therefore not defined everywhere on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is *not* valid.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186229682 1068 #edb/ref 17592186228249 13194139533415 17592186045585 false] [17592186229682 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307747 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186229682 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186229682 1071 "Given that $I(t) = \\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{1}(2x + e^{t})^{2}dx$, calculate $I^{′}(t)$ for $t ∈ R$ by differentiating under the integral sign.$I^{′}(t) =$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533415 17592186045585 false] [17592186229682 1071 "Given that $I(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+{e}^{t})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x,$ calculate ${I}^{′}(t)$ for $t\\in R$ by differentiating under the integral sign.\n\n${I}^{′}(t)=2{e}^{t}(\\,$\n{{field-1}}$)$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186229682 1074 "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWe wish to compute ${I}^{′}(t),$ where\n\n$$\nI(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+{e}^{t})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating under the integral sign, we get\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(2x+{e}^{t})^{2}]\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNext, let's compute the partial derivative with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(2x+{e}^{t})^{2}] & =2(2x+{e}^{t})\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂t}(2x+{e}^{t}) \\\\  & =2(2x+{e}^{t})\\cdot {e}^{t} \\\\  & =2{e}^{t}(2x+{e}^{t})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this back into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{′}(t) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2{e}^{t}(2x+{e}^{t})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{e}^{t}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+{e}^{t})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's evaluate the remaining integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+{e}^{t})\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2x\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}{|}_{0}^{1}+{e}^{t}\\cdot x{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =1+{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}(t)=2{e}^{t}(1+{e}^{t}).\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186229683 1068 #edb/ref 17592186228250 13194139533415 17592186045585 false] [17592186229683 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308037 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186229683 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186229683 1071 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=3y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=3x(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives the general solution to the system of ODEs above?\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(3t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(3t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}].$*" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186229683 1071 "${x^{′}(t) = 3y(t); y^{′}(t) = 3x(t)$\nWhich of the following gives the general solution to the system of ODEs above?\n\n*Hint: $e^{At} = \\begin{bmatrix}\\cosh (3t) & \\sinh (3t) \\\\ \\sinh (3t) & \\cosh (3t)\\end{bmatrix}$, where $A = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$.*" 13194139533415 17592186045585 false] [17592186229683 1074 "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the general solution of the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)=Φ(t)\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix of the system, and $c$ is a constant vector.\n\nNow, using the given information, the standard fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(3t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(3t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,c \\\\  & ={e}^{At}\\,c \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(3t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(3t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186305158 1081 "\\frac{3(s+2)}{s(s-2)(s-5)}" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186305158 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186305158 1084 #uuid "0039aee6-98b7-59c0-8d03-5b2d50aa3e57" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186305159 1081 "-{e}^{-2x}+8x{e}^{-2x}" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186305159 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186305159 1084 #uuid "0041758a-5182-50fb-a219-5d3f3ef02024" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186305160 1081 "-\\frac{1}{2}x" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186305160 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186305160 1084 #uuid "00555be1-a348-5be5-af69-456ed748a4ae" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186305161 1081 "y=2x" 13194139533415 1759218604