c.png)\n\nA sketch of the phase space with nullclines and the corresponding direction field of tangents to the solutions of a system of differential equations is shown above (the tangents are not drawn to scale). Which of the following could represent the trajectory starting at the point marked on the graph?" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308348 1072 #uuid "2dfbad27-2f3a-51d2-ab57-9054aa8268d1" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308348 1074 "Let's sketch the trajectory.\n\n- We start at the given point, going along with the flow (upward and slightly to the right) until the trajectory intersects the $x$-nullcline, where the tangents are vertical.\n\n![](images/6d/6d897c2e9249447cc2d0c902a592d04442b0928c315163b6ab80cff288c84657.png)\n\n- Next, the flow continues going upward but then starts turning  to the left.\n\n![](images/be/bed6896ecc0edcdb157b648108d403e1b8e29463d9f61aa0e0c26e8f5e2dd8c6.png)\n\n- After that, the trajectory continues upward and slightly to the left, suggesting that it may converge to the origin\n\n![](images/cc/cc53577a278595e5f0155119aa5eb1a7b27ec01847a6daa1ba6ade6792c5f5e9.png)" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308349 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307610 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308349 1069 "q-332936" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308349 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308349 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2y=8{e}^{-2x},\\,y(0)=-1\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2y=8{e}^{-2x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{-2x},$ solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308349 1072 #uuid "d63971f5-c416-5771-8c44-552fcfc50a79" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308349 1074 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+2{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $8{e}^{-2x},$ and ${e}^{-2x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{-2x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{-2x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{-2x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{-2x}-2x{e}^{-2x}) \\\\  & =\\alpha (1-2x){e}^{-2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1-2x){e}^{-2x})+2(\\alpha x{e}^{-2x}) & =8{e}^{-2x} \\\\ (1-2x+2x)\\alpha {e}^{-2x} & =8{e}^{-2x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{-2x} & =8{e}^{-2x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=8x{e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-2x}+8x{e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nWe can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=-1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+0 & =-1 \\\\ A & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=-{e}^{-2x}+8x{e}^{-2x}.\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308350 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307933 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308350 1069 "q-332937" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308350 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308350 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-4y=-2{e}^{4x},\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-4y=-2{e}^{4x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{4x},$ solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308350 1072 #uuid "d4df5e5b-65c9-5002-87ac-cf021a213a55" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308350 1074 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-4{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{4x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $-2{e}^{4x},$ and ${e}^{4x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{4x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{4x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{4x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{4x}+4x{e}^{4x}) \\\\  & =\\alpha (1+4x){e}^{4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1+4x){e}^{4x})-4(\\alpha x{e}^{4x}) & =-2{e}^{4x} \\\\ (1+4x-4x)\\alpha {e}^{4x} & =-2{e}^{4x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{4x} & =-2{e}^{4x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-2x{e}^{4x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{4x}-2x{e}^{4x}.\n$$\n\nWe can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+0 & =1 \\\\ A & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{4x}-2x{e}^{4x}.\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308351 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307728 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308351 1069 "q-332938" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308351 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308351 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-5y=15{e}^{5x},\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-5y=15{e}^{5x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{5x},$ solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308351 1072 #uuid "ceb57bf0-d702-511f-a8e6-4fe4eee1bf79" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308351 1074 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-5{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{5x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $15{e}^{5x},$ and ${e}^{5x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{5x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{5x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{5x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{5x}+5x{e}^{5x}) \\\\  & =\\alpha (1+5x){e}^{5x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1+5x){e}^{5x})-5(\\alpha x{e}^{5x}) & =15{e}^{5x} \\\\ (1+5x-5x)\\alpha {e}^{5x} & =15{e}^{5x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{5x} & =15{e}^{5x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=15x{e}^{5x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{5x}+15x{e}^{5x}.\n$$\n\nWe can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+0 & =1 \\\\ A & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{5x}+15x{e}^{5x}.\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308352 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307854 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308352 1069 "q-332940" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308352 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308352 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-2y=-10{e}^{2x},\\,y(0)=0\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-2y=-10{e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{2x},$ solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308352 1072 #uuid "d0bb4554-9b00-5a89-bf22-7eaf49db9dad" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308352 1074 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-2{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $-10{e}^{2x},$ and ${e}^{2x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{2x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{2x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{2x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{2x}+2x{e}^{2x}) \\\\  & =\\alpha (1+2x){e}^{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1+2x){e}^{2x})-2(\\alpha x{e}^{2x}) & =-10{e}^{2x} \\\\ (1+2x-2x)\\alpha {e}^{2x} & =-10{e}^{2x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{2x} & =-10{e}^{2x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =-10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-10x{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{2x}-10x{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nWe can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+0 & =0 \\\\ A & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=-10x{e}^{2x}.\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308353 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308316 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308353 1069 "q-332941" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308353 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308353 1071 "![](images/7a/7ad36c695bc150b580caa81f71fa358163a51ca3ed788d