iven by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-3x}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{-3\\cdot 0}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot 0)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot 0) & =1 \\\\ A-2-0 & =1 \\\\ A & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=3{e}^{-3x}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308359 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308127 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308359 1069 "q-332974" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308359 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308359 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+4y=-30\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x),\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+4y=-30\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{-4x},$ solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308359 1072 #uuid "856856d3-2b95-50a8-b5ae-6342338b14ea" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308359 1074 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+4{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-4x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $-30\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).$ So, we assume the particular solution to be \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}=-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n(-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))+4(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))=-30\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(2\\beta +4\\alpha )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+(-2\\alpha +4\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)=-30\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2\\beta +4\\alpha =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)) \\\\ -2\\alpha +4\\beta =-30\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =3,\\beta =-6.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-4x}+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{-4\\cdot 0}+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot 0)-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot 0) & =1 \\\\ A+3-0 & =1 \\\\ A & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=-2{e}^{-4x}+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [175921