 equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-2\\alpha =2\\,(\\text{equating the coefficients of }{x}^{2}) \\\\ 2\\alpha -2\\beta =0\\,(\\text{equating the coefficients of }x) \\\\ \\beta -2\\gamma =3\\,(\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-1,$ $\\beta =-1,$ and $\\gamma =-2.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-{x}^{2}-x-2.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{2x}-{x}^{2}-x-2.\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the constant $A$ using the initial condition.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{2(0)}-(0)^{2}-(0)-2 & =1 \\\\ A-2 & =1 \\\\ A & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=3{e}^{2x}-{x}^{2}-x-2.\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308361 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307873 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308361 1069 "q-332995" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308361 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308361 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-6y=20\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),\\,y(0)=2\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-6y=20\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{6x},$ solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308361 1072 #uuid "f7c02cd8-b481-5b32-9d31-1ef517736aad" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308361 1074 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-6{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving th