\nUsing the implicit Euler method with step size $Δx=0.5,$ the new value ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ is obtained by solving the equation $g({y}_{\\text{new}})=0$ at each step.\nWhich of the following is the **specific function** $g({y}_{\\text{new}})$ used in the first step?" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308392 1072 #uuid "a9a47ae6-ee59-581c-a76f-f2251d0aa676" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308392 1074 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nIn general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =({x}_{\\text{new}}({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-{y}_{\\text{new}})\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =0.5{x}_{\\text{new}}({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-0.5{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and move all terms to the left-hand side and define the resulting expression as $g({y}_{\\text{new}}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+0.5{x}_{\\text{new}}({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-0.5{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {{1.5{y}_{\\text{new}}-0.5{x}_{\\text{new}}({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-y}_{⏟}}_{g({y}_{\\text{new}})} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general form of the function $g$ for step size $Δx=0.5$ is\n\n$$\ng({y}_{\\text{new}})=1.5{y}_{\\text{new}}-0.5{x}_{\\text{new}}({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-y.\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=1+0.5=1.5.$ Therefore, the function used in the first step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({y}_{\\text{new}}) & =1.5{y}_{\\text{new}}-0.5\\cdot 1.5\\,({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-1 \\\\  & =1.5{y}_{\\text{new}}-0.75({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308393 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307536 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308393 1069 "q-333119" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308393 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308393 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}y-{y}^{3},\\,y(1)=3\n$$\n\nUsing the implicit Euler method with step size $Δx=0.5,$ the new value ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ is obtained by solving the equation $g({y}_{\\text{new}})=0$ at each step.\nWhich of the following is the **specific function** $g({y}_{\\text{new}})$ used in the first step?" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308393 1072 #uuid "ae3b87bc-68e4-5606-a94b-221bbad8f4e1" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308393 1074 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nIn general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3})\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =0.5({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}-0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and move all terms to the left-hand side and define the resulting expression as $g({y}_{\\text{new}}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+0.5({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}-0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3} \\\\ {{0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-0.5({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}-y}_{⏟}}_{g({y}_{\\text{new}})} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general form of the function $g$ for step size $Δx=0.5$ is\n\n$$\ng({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-0.5({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}-y.\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=1+0.5=1.5.$ Therefore, the function used in the first step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({y}_{\\text{new}}) & =0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-0.5\\cdot (1.5)^{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}-3 \\\\  & =0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-1.125{y}_{\\text{new}}-3 \\\\  & =0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-0.125{y}_{\\text{new}}-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308394 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307845 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308394 1069 "q-33312" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308394 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308394 1071 "Find an equation of the plane that contains the point $Q(3,2,2)$ and is perpendicular to the vector $n=⟨2,2,3⟩.$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308394 1072 #uuid "641cc290-5f71-543f-a832-4e80117c9730" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308394 1074 "First, notice that the position vector of $Q$ is $q=⟨3,2,2⟩,$ $n=⟨2,2,3⟩$ is normal to the plane.\n\nThe equation of the plane that passes though the point $Q({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ and is perpendicular to the vector $n$ can be given as\n\n$$\n(r-q)\\cdot n=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is the position vector of any point on the plane, and $q$ is the position vector of $Q.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-q)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n-q\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =q\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨2,2,3⟩ & =⟨3,2,2⟩\\cdot ⟨2,2,3⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨2,2,3⟩ & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308395 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308309 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308395 1069 "q-33313" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308395 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308395 1071 "A nonzero vector $⟨a,\\,0,\\,c⟩$ is parallel to the plane $r\\cdot ⟨4,0,2⟩=16.$ What is the value of $\\frac{a}{c}?$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308395 1072 #uuid "a973cbbd-df7e-5f41-a87c-5c205b31db4a" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308395 1074 "We need a vector $v=⟨{t}_{1},{t}_{2},{t}_{3}⟩$ that is parallel to the given plane. \n\nFrom the equation $r\\cdot ⟨4,0,2⟩=16$ of the plane, we get the normal vector $n=⟨4,0,2⟩.$ So, $v⊥n$ and we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨{t}_{1},{t}_{2},{t}_{3}⟩\\cdot ⟨4,0,2⟩ & =0 \\\\ 4{t}_{1}+2{t}_{3} & =0 \\\\ 2{t}_{3} & =-4{t}_{1} \\\\ {t}_{3} & =-2{t}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we obtain \n\n$$\nv=⟨{t}_{1},\\,{t}_{2},\\,-2{t}_{1}⟩,\n$$\n\nwhere ${t}_{1},{t}_{2}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ \n\nWe are told that the second component of $v$ equals $0.$ So, we must set ${t}_{2}=0.$ \n\nSince $v$ is nonzero, we can pick any ${t}_{1}\\ne 0.$ For example, setting ${t}_{1}=1$, we get\n\n$$\nv=⟨1,\\,0,\\,-2⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\frac{a}{c}=-\\frac{1}{2}.$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308396 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308039 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308396 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308197 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308396 1069 "q-333133" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308396 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308396 1071 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{1}(t)+9{x}_{2}(t)+9 \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=2{x}_{2}(t)+2,\\end{aligned}\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform to both sides of each equation in our system, we get a transformed system with the solution \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}(s)=L{{x}_{1}(t)}=\\frac{1}{s+1}+\\frac{3}{s-2},\\,{X}_{2}(s)=L{{x}_{2}(t)}=\\frac{1}{s-2}-\\frac{1}{s}.\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the original initial value problem.\n\n| $x(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  |  |" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308396 1072 #uuid "12cb134e-509f-512f-9761-fbb450604687" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [1759