oach $\\text{the origin}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/44/44d0f3d13ca03a0644b2f28df9200cd521b7b44d625f346e981d2a54165284f5.png)\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=0\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line. \n\nSince ${e}^{-3t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach $\\text{the origin}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/6a/6a4c15275ba80d01a90b2e010ed95e95f9a75cfa13f8e51db3433f6356949057.png)\n\nFor this particular system, we have\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{2}<{\\lambda }_{1}<0\n$$\n\nthus, ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ is the eigenvalue that determines the long-term behavior (dominant eigenvalue).\n\nSince the dominant eigenvalue is ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1,$ solutions become parallel to the straight-line solution containing the vector ${v}_{1}$ as they move towards the origin.\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/f8/f838a055e525a264bf36b8e0478d186fe17753bba8c0088fea01ac7c5c1f8d7a.png)" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308403 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307852 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308403 1069 "q-333160" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308403 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308403 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+3y=12{e}^{-3x},\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+3y=12{e}^{-3x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{-3x},$ solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308403 1072 #uuid "8b67c45f-7435-5b3b-b8a6-35875cc6d705" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308403 1074 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+3{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $12{e}^{-3x},$ and ${e}^{-3x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{-3x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{-3x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{-3x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{-3x}-3x{e}^{-3x}) \\\\  & =\\alpha (1-3x){e}^{-3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1-3x){e}^{-3x})+3(\\alpha x{e}^{-3x}) & =12{e}^{-3x} \\\\ (1-3x+3x)\\alpha {e}^{-3x} & =12{e}^{-3x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{-3x} & =12{e}^{-3x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=12x{e}^{-3x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-3x}+12x{e}^{-3x}.\n$$\n\nWe can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+0 & =1 \\\\ A & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-3x}+12x{e}^{-3x}.\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308404 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308281 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308404 1069 "q-333168" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308404 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308404 1071 "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-7y=5{e}^{7x}.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{7x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308404 1072 #uuid "cafd04c6-8f34-538e-bfbf-4314bcafa2be" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308404 1074 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-7{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{7x}.\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $5{e}^{7x},$ and ${e}^{7x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{7x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{7x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{7x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{7x}+7x{e}^{7x}) \\\\  & =\\alpha (1+7x){e}^{7x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1+7x){e}^{7x})-7\\alpha x{e}^{7x} & =5{e}^{7x} \\\\ (1+7x-7x)\\alpha {e}^{7x} & =5{e}^{7x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{7x} & =5{e}^{7x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=5x{e}^{7x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{7x}+5x{e}^{7x}.\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308405 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307980 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308405 1069 "q-33317" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308405 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308405 1071 "Find an equation of the plane that passes through the point $Q(1,-4,2)$ and is perpendicular to the line that goes through the points \n$A(2,1,-3)$ and $B(1,6,-4).$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308405 1072 #uuid "4553242a-e32b-5422-9dd6-251f47f5a320" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308405 1074 "First, we note that the position vector of $Q$ is $q=⟨1,-4,2⟩.$\n\nSince the line  passes through the points $A(2,1,-3)$ and $B(1,6,-4)$ we can find a vector parallel to the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =⟨1,6,-4⟩-⟨2,1,-3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,5,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, a normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨-1,5,-1⟩.$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-q)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n-q\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =q\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-1,5,-1⟩ & =⟨1,-4,2⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,5,-1⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-1,5,-1⟩ & =-23.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308406 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307931 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308406 1069 "q-333171" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308406 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308406 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-3y=8{e}^{x},\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-3y=8{e}^{x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{3x},$ solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308406 1072 #uuid "a04ef1bf-4028-5cae-b670-2200bafd2729" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308406 1074 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-3{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $8{e}^{x},$ and ${e}^{x}$ is not a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}=\\alpha {e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha {e}^{x})-3(\\alpha {e}^{x}) & =8{e}^{x} \\\\ (1-3)\\alpha {e}^{x} & =8{e}^{x} \\\\ -2\\alpha {e}^{x} & =8{e}^{x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-4{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{3x}-4{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nWe can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4 & =1 \\\\ A & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=5{e}^{3x}-4{e}^{x}.\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308407 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308204 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308407 1069 "q-333176" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308407 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308407 1071 "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-5y=2{e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{5x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308407 1072 #uuid "85a59aa1-fae1-52f3-8e86-a52159252cf3" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308407 1074 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-5{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{5x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $2{e}^{3x},$ and ${e}^{3x}$ is not a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{3x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′}=3\\alpha {e}^{3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\alpha {e}^{3x}-5\\alpha {e}^{3x} & =2{e}^{3x} \\\\ -2\\alpha {e}^{3x} & =2{e}^{3x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-{e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{5x}-{e}^{3x}.\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308408 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307716 13194139533415