axis is the $\\text{unstable}$ line.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{a saddle point}.$ \n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/8c/8c350c21a6206ec73244dd58601b20a816dcb69557a030099870e2c824aba772.png)" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308416 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307685 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308416 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307689 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308416 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308026 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308416 1069 "q-333214" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308416 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308416 1071 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options below regarding the given decoupled system of linear differential equations.$\\,$\nThe $x$-axis is a {{field-1}} line.\n\n$\\,$\nThe $y$-axis is a {{field-2}} line.\n\n$\\,$\nThe equilibrium point (origin) is {{field-3}}." 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308416 1072 #uuid "59947953-f704-5cbc-beaf-56322b9434f5" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308416 1074 "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nRecall that a homogeneous system of linear differential equations has a single equilibrium (fixed point) at the origin.\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ with the eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $x$-axis and move away from the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, the $x$-axis is the $\\text{unstable}$ line.\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3$ with the eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $y$-axis and approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, the $y$-axis is the $\\text{stable}$ line.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{a saddle point}.$ \n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/6d/6dc8ceb9a1c771f18d6e90d450cd837fe80c6bab7943ab7d397d90eb45754594.png)" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308417 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307765 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308417 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307885 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308417 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308191 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308417 1069 "q-333218" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308417 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308417 1071 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=3x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options below regarding the given decoupled system of linear differential equations.$\\,$\nThe $x$-axis is a {{field-1}} line.\n\n$\\,$\nThe $y$-axis is a {{field-2}} line.\n\n$\\,$\nThe equilibrium point (origin) is {{field-3}}." 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308417 1072 #uuid "2f2e900b-fa9b-5f5e-ba9a-c9406243da7a" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308417 1074 "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nRecall that a homogeneous system of linear differential equations has a single equilibrium (fixed point) at the origin.\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ with the eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $x$-axis and move away from the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, the $x$-axis is the $\\text{unstable}$ line.\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=1$ with the eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $y$-axis and also move away from the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, the $y$-axis is the $\\text{unstable}$ line.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{an unstable node (source)}.$ \n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/65/657508496ddc73c386545152b45cc628d9abddd13ec069daf169d1dc61c1f7be.png)" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308418 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308198 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308418 1069 "q-33322" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308418 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308418 1071 "Find an equation of the plane that contains the point  $Q(5,2,3)$ and is perpendicular to the vector $n=6i+5j+4k.$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308418 1072 #uuid "dc9756ae-93f4-5e8e-b7df-21ca51316b9d" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308418 1074 "First, notice that the position vector of $Q$ is $q=5i+2j+3k$ and $n=6i+5j+4k$ is normal to the plane. \n\nThe equation of the plane that passes though the point $Q({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ and is perpendicular to the vector $n$ can be given as\n\n$$\n(r-q)\\cdot n=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is the position vector of any point on the plane, and $q$ is the position vector of $Q.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-q)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n-q\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =q\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot (6i+5j+4k) & =(5i+2j+3k)\\cdot (6i+5j+4k) \\\\ r\\cdot (6i+5j+4k) & =52.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308419 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307571 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308419 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308114 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308419 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308122 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308419 1069 "q-333231" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308419 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308419 1071 "An initial value problem has the exact solution ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)=\\sqrt{1+x}.$ A numerical method is used to approximate the solution to the initial value problem, resulting in the table below, where $y$ denotes the approximate solution at each step, and ${τ}_{n}$ is the local truncation error at each step. At each step, the exact value is used to approximate the new value. \n\nCalculate the local truncation error at each step. Round your answers to three decimal places.\n\n| $n$ | $x$ | $y$ | ${τ}_{n}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1.000$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $0.22$ | $1.117$ | {{field-1}} |\n| $2$ | $0.44$ | $1.185$ | {{field-2}} |\n| $3$ | $0.66$ | $1.299$ | {{field-3}} |" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308419 1072 #uuid "bb1f0209-f72a-5477-b5b3-08ade088197b" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308419 1074 "The local truncation error (LTE) is the error made in a *single step* of a numerical method, when the step begins from an *exact value*. The LTE of a numerical method at step $n$ from $x$ with exact value ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)$ to ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{n} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-({y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)+Δy).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe find the LTE at each step, rounding to three decimal places in each case:\n\n- After the first step, we have ${x}_{\\text{new}}=0.22$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}=1.117.$ So, the LTE of the first step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{1} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(0.22)-1.117 \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1.22}-1.117 \\\\  & ≈-0.012.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- After the second step, we have ${x}_{\\text{new}}=0.44$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}=1.185.$ So, the LTE of the second step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{2} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(0.44)-1.185 \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1.44}-1.185 \\\\  & =0.015.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- After the third step, we have ${x}_{\\text{new}}=0.66$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}=1.299.$ So, the LTE of the third step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{3} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(0.66)-1.299 \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1.66}-1.299 \\\\ = & ≈-0.011.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed table is as given below.\n\n| $n$ | $x$ | $y$ | ${τ}_{n}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1.000$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $0.22$ | $1.117$ | $-0.012$ |\n| $2$ | $0.44$ | $1.185$ | $0.015$ |\n| $3$ | $0.66$ | $1.299$ | $-0.011$ |" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308420 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307804 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308420 1069 "q-333236" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308420 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308420 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x+2,\\,y(0)=0\n$$\n\nSuppose we wish to approximate the solution using the following numerical method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =(x+{y}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the exact solution is ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+2x,$ compute the local truncation error, ${τ}_{1},$ for the first step starting at $x=0$ with step size $Δx=0.3.$\n\n${τ}_{1}=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308420 1072 #uuid "2be4197e-c256-50d1-bbe2-b8cee010b8ce" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308420 1074 "The local truncation error (LTE) is the error made in a *single step* of a numerical method, when the step begins from an *exact value*. The LTE of a numerical method at step $n$ from $x$ with exact value ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)$ to ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{n} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-({y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)+Δy).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that the exact $y$-value at the start of the step $x=0$ is $y(0)=0.$ Now, let's find the \n$y$-increment given by the numerical method.\n\nFirst, the slope at the start of the step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{exact}}^{′}(0) & =0+2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the increment at this step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =(x+{y}_{\\text{exact}}^{′}(0))\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(0+2)\\cdot 0.3 \\\\  & =0.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the exact value at ${x}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}}) & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(x+Δx) \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(0+0.3) \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(0.3) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(0.3)^{2}+2(0.3) \\\\  & =0.045+0.6 \\\\  & =0.645.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the LTE for the first step starting at $x=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{1} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-(y(0)+Δy) \\\\  & =0.645-(0+0.6) \\\\  & =0.045.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308421 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307654 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308421 1069 "q-333250" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308421 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308421 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=xy-{y}^{2},\\,y(0.5)=0.7\n$$\n\nUse the implicit Euler method with one step and Newton's method to approximate $y(1).$ Round your answer to two decimal places.\n\n$y(1)≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308421 1072 #uuid "e04ee226-3766-5dd1-98e0-8ecdd9d4ed8a" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308421 1074 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nSince we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=1,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=1-0.5=0.5.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the implicit Euler method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =({x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2})\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =0.5{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}}-0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and move all terms to the left-hand side and define the resulting expression as $g({y}_{\\text{new}}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+0.5{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}}-0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-0.5{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-y=0.\n$$\n\nSo, the general form of the function $g$ for step size $Δx=0.5$ is\n\n$$\ng({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-0.5{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-y.\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=0.5+0.5=1.$ Then, the function $g$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({y}_{\\text{new}}) & =0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-0.5(1){y}_{\\text{new}}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-0.7 \\\\  & =0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+0.5{y}_{\\text{new}}-0.7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use a root-finding method with starting point $y=0.7$ to solve $g({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.$ We'll use Newton's method. \n\n$$\n({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n+1}=({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-\\frac{g({y}_{\\text{new}})}{{g}^{′}({y}_{\\text{new}})}\n$$\n\nNote that the derivative of $g$ is\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}({y}_{\\text{new}})=({y}_{\\text{new}})+0.5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton's method iterative scheme is as follows:\n\n$$\n({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n+1}=({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-\\frac{0.5(({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n})^{2}+0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-0.7}{({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}+0.5}\n$$\n\nApplying several iterations of Newton's method starting with $y(0.5)=0.7,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({y}_{\\text{new}})_{1} & =0.7 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{2} & ≈0.7875 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{3} & ≈0.7845 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{4} & ≈0.7845\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(1)≈0.78,$ rounded to two decimal places." 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308422 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308192 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308422 1069 "q-333251" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308422 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308422 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{3}-xy,\\,y(0.5)=1.1\n$$\n\nUse the implicit Euler method with one step and Newton's method to approximate $y(1).$ Round your answer to two decimal places.\n\n$y(1)≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308422 1072 #uuid "925ef6f6-0f68-50ae-9f97-ae9f2f13c53e" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308422 1074 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nSince we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=1,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=1-0.5=0.5.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the implicit Euler method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(({x}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}})\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =0.5({x}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-0.5{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and move all terms to the left-hand side and define the resulting expression as $g({y}_{\\text{new}}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+0.5({x}_{\\text{new}})^{3}-0.5{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n0.5{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-y-0.5({x}_{\\text{new}})^{3}=0.\n$$\n\nSo, the general form of the function $g$ for step size $Δx=0.5$ is\n\n$$\ng({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.5{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-y-0.5({x}_{\\text{new}})^{3}.\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=0.5+0.5=1.$ Then, the function $g$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({y}_{\\text{new}}) & =0.5(1){y}_{\\text{new}}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-1.1-0.5(1)^{3} \\\\  & =1.5{y}_{\\text{new}}-1.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use a root-finding method with starting point $y=1.1$ to solve $g({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.$ We'll use Newton's method. \n\n$$\n({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n+1}=({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-\\frac{g({y}_{\\text{new}})}{{g}^{′}({y}_{\\text{new}})}\n$$\n\nNote that the derivative of $g$ is\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}({y}_{\\text{new}})=1.5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton's method iterative scheme is as follows:\n\n$$\n({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n+1}=({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-\\frac{1.5({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-1.6}{1.5}\n$$\n\nApplying several iterations of Newton's method starting with $y(0.5)=1.1,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({y}_{\\text{new}})_{1} & =1.1 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{2} & ≈1.066667 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{3} & ≈1.066667\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(1)≈1.07,$ rounded to two decimal places." 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308423 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307766 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308423 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308064 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308423 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308260 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308423 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308267 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308423 1069 "q-333253" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308423 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308423 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=3{x}^{2}-2y,\\,y(0)=0\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using the $2$-Step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method. Given that the step size is $Δx=1,$ and that one preliminary step has already been computed using the Runge-Kutta method, complete the next row of the table below.\n\n*Hint: The corrector equation of the two-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method is defined as*\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\n*where ${y}_{p}$ denotes the predictor at the current step, and ${y}_{-1}^{′}$ is the estimated slope at the previous point.* \n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $\\frac{3}{4}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{3}{4}$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $\\frac{9}{4}$ | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} |\n\nTherefore, $y(2)≈$ {{field-4}}." 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308423 1072 #uuid "c772074b-831b-5d42-b11f-0bacd35780bb" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308423 1074 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe $2$-Step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton (ABM2) Method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{-1}^{′}=f({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point (${x}_{-1},{y}_{-1}),$ and\n\n- ${y}_{p}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})$ is the estimated slope at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})=(x+Δx,y+Δ{y}_{p}).$\n\nFrom the second row of the partially filled table, we have that $y(1)≈\\frac{3}{4},$ where the predicted $y$-increment is $Δ