 & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=(-1)\\cdot 1=-1, \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=3\\cdot 1=3, \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2, \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-1+(-1)=-2, \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=1+3=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $3$ | $3$ |\n| $2$ | $-2$ |  |  | $4$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nWe compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy,$ ${z}^{′}$ and $Δz$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the second row), and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}},$ ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =1-x+2y+z=1-2+2\\cdot (-2)+4=-1, \\\\ {z}^{′} & =x-y+{z}^{2}=2-(-2)+{4}^{2}=20, \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=(-1)\\cdot 1=-1, \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=20\\cdot 1=20, \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=2+1=3, \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-2+(-1)=-3, \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=4+20=24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $3$ | $3$ |\n| $2$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $-1$ | $4$ | $20$ | $20$ |\n| $3$ | $-3$ |  |  | $24$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(3)≈-3$\nand\n$z(3)≈24.$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308434 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308133 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308434 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308305 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308434 1069 "q-333280" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308434 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308434 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem for the functions $y=y(x),z=z(x):$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=x+{y}^{2}-z,\\, & y(-2)=0 \\\\ {z}^{′}=2-y+2z,\\, & z(-2)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with two steps of equal size to approximate $y(0)$ and $z(0).$\n\n$y(0)≈$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,z(0)≈$\n{{field-2}}" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308434 1072 #uuid "ab03030b-1a1b-57e8-869b-9bee960f6e75" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308434 1074 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=f(x,y,z),\\, & y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′}=g(x,y,z),\\, & z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEuler's method approximates the solution by applying the update rule component-wise to each equation. More precisely, the increments on $y$ and $z$ are\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}Δy={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx, \\\\ Δz={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe new values are then given by ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}=z+Δz.$\n\nFirst, note that we are given the values of $y$ and $z$ at $x=-2$ and asked to find their values at $x=0.$ The distance from $x=-2$ to $x=0$ is $2,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{0-(-2)}{2}=\\frac{2}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(-2)=0$ and $z(-2)=1.$\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-2$ | $0$ |  |  | $1$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nWe compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy,$ ${z}^{′}$ and $Δz$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the first row), and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}},$ ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x+{y}^{2}-z=-2+{0}^{2}-1=-3, \\\\ {z}^{′} & =2-y+2z=2-0+2\\cdot 1=4, \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=(-3)\\cdot 1=-3, \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=4\\cdot 1=4, \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=-2+1=-1, \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=0+(-3)=-3, \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=1+4=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-2$ | $0$ | $-3$ | $-3$ | $1$ | $4$ | $4$ |\n| $-1$ | $-3$ |  |  | $5$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nWe compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy,$ ${z}^{′}$ and $Δz$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the second row), and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}},$ ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x+{y}^{2}-z=-1+(-3)^{2}-5=3, \\\\ {z}^{′} & =2-y+2z=2-(-3)+2\\cdot 5=15, \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=3\\cdot 1=3, \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=15\\cdot 1=15, \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=-1+1=0, \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-3+3=0, \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=5+15=20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-2$ | $0$ | $-3$ | $-3$ | $1$ | $4$ | $4$ |\n| $-1$ | $-3$ | $3$ | $3$ | $5$ | $15$ | $15$ |\n| $0$ | $0$ |  |  | $20$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(0)≈0$\nand\n$z(0)≈20.$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308435 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308140 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308435 1069 "q-333282" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308435 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308435 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=3x-{y}^{2},\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nUse the implicit Euler method with one step and Newton's method to approximate $y(0.4).$ Round your answer to two decimal places.\n\n$y(0.4)≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308435 1072 #uuid "b47cc8de-c816-506f-bb22-03bfcc2cbb51" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308435 1074 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nSince we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=0.4,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=0.4-0=0.4.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the implicit Euler method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(3({x}_{\\text{new}})-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2})\\cdot 0.4 \\\\  & =1.2({x}_{\\text{new}})-0.4({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and move all terms to the left-hand side and define the resulting expression as $g({y}_{\\text{new}}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+1.2({x}_{\\text{new}})-0.4({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n0.4({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-y-1.2({x}_{\\text{new}})=0.\n$$\n\nSo, the general form of the function $g$ for step size $Δx=0.4$ is\n\n$$\ng({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.4({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-y-1.2({x}_{\\text{new}}).\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=0+0.4=0.4.$ Then, the function $g$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({y}_{\\text{new}}) & =0.4({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-1-1.2(0.4) \\\\  & =0.4({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-1.48.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use a root-finding method with starting point $y=1$ to solve $g({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.$ We'll use Newton's method. \n\n$$\n({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n+1}=({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-\\frac{g({y}_{\\text{new}})}{{g}^{′}({y}_{\\text{new}})}\n$$\n\nNote that the derivative of $g$ is\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.8({y}_{\\text{new}})+1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton's method iterative scheme is as follows:\n\n$$\n({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n+1}=({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-\\frac{0.4(({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n})^{2}+({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-1.48}{0.8(({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n})+1}\n$$\n\nApplying several iterations of Newton's method starting with $y(0)=1,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({y}_{\\text{new}})_{1} & =1 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{2} & =1.044444 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{3} & ≈1.044014 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{4} & ≈1.044014\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(0.4)≈1.04,$ rounded to two decimal places." 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308436 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308165 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308436 1069 "q-333286" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308436 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308436 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2x-{y}^{2},\\,y(0.4)=1.2\n$$\n\nUse the implicit Euler method with one step and Newton's method to approximate $y(0.9).$ Round your answer to two decimal places.\n\n$y(0.9)≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308436 1072 #uuid "faf897e3-cf50-5b4e-b765-c864f4378034" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308436 1074 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nSince we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=0.9,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=0.9-0.4=0.5.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the implicit Euler method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(2({x}_{\\text{new}})-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2})\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =({x}_{\\text{new}})-0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and move all terms to the left-hand side and define the resulting expression as $g({y}_{\\text{new}}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+({x}_{\\text{new}})-0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-y-({x}_{\\text{new}})=0.\n$$\n\nSo, the general form of the function $g$ for step size $Δx=0.5$ is\n\n$$\ng({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-y-({x}_{\\text{new}}).\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=0.4+0.5=0.9.$ Then, the function $g$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({y}_{\\text{new}}) & =0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-1.2-(0.9) \\\\  & =0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-2.1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use a root-finding method with starting point $y=1.2$ to solve $g({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.$ We'll use Newton's method. \n\n$$\n({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n+1}=({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-\\frac{g({y}_{\\text{new}})}{{g}^{′}({y}_{\\text{new}})}\n$$\n\nNote that the derivative of $g$ is\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}+1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton's method iterative scheme is as follows:\n\n$$\n({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n+1}=({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-\\frac{0.5(({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n})^{2}+({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-2.1}{({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}+1}\n$$\n\nApplying several iterations of Newton's method starting with $y(0.4)=1.2,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({y}_{\\text{new}})_{1} & =1.2 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{2} & =1.281818 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{3} & ≈1.280351 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{4} & ≈1.280351\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(0.9)≈1.28,$ rounded to two decimal places." 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308437 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307681 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308437 1069 "q-333289" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308437 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308437 1071 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308437 1072 #uuid "6ecc3793-6a18-51b0-b6dd-b12969e4e994" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308437 1074 "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=-1$ with the corresponding eigenvectors $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{-t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{-t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]={e}^{-t}[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNotice that for each vector $[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}],$ we get the straight-line solutions of the form\n${e}^{-t}[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$\nthat represent the line passing through the origin and parallel to the vector $[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince ${e}^{-t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ \n\nAmong the given options, the only phase portrait satisfying these conditions is the following:\n\n![](images/6b/6bdaece2579041f23a73323c4317751dfd8266364633bd2af258437b53d4a6f9.png)" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308438 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308230 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308438 1069 "q-333293" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308438 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308438 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={x}^{2}-{y}^{2},\\,y(1.5)=1\n$$\n\nUse the implicit Euler method with one step and Newton's method to approximate $y(2).$ Round your answer to two decimal places.\n\n$y(2)≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308438 1072 #uuid "19fa4226-3319-598c-833e-cdfb832c691d" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308438 1074 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nSince we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=2,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=2-1.5=0.5.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the implicit Euler method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2})\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =0.5({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and move all terms to the left-hand side and define the resulting expression as $g({y}_{\\text{new}}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+0.5({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-y-0.5({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}=0.\n$$\n\nSo, the general form of the function $g$ for step size $Δx=0.5$ is\n\n$$\ng({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-y-0.5({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=1.5+0.5=2.$ Then, the function $g$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({y}_{\\text{new}}) & =0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-1-0.5(2)^{2} \\\\  & =0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use a root-finding method with starting point $y=1$ to solve $g({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.$ We'll use Newton's method. \n\n$$\n({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n+1}=({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-\\frac{g({y}_{\\text{new}})}{{g}^{′}({y}_{\\text{new}})}\n$$\n\nNote that the derivative of $g$ is\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}({y}_{\\text{new}})=({y}_{\\text{new}})+1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton's method iterative scheme is as follows:\n\n$$\n({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n+1}=({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-\\frac{0.5(({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n})^{2}+({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-3}{(({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n})+1}\n$$\n\nApplying several iterations of Newton's method starting with $y(1.5)=1,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({y}_{\\text{new}})_{1} & =1 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{2} & =1.750000 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{3} & ≈1.647727 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{4} & ≈1.645752\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(2)≈1.65,$ rounded to two decimal places." 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308439 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307710 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308439 1069 "q-333295" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308439 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308439 1071 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-5x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-5y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308439 1072 #uuid "e759507e-a8b9-5e12-8e59-4730d3a76a6f" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308439 1074 "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=-5$ with the corresponding eigenvectors $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{-5t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{-5t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]={e}^{-5t}[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNotice that for each vector $[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}],$ we get the straight-line solutions of the form\n${e}^{-5t}[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$\nthat represent the line passing through the origin and parallel to the vector $[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince ${e}^{-5t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, the only phase portrait satisfying these conditions is the following:\n\n![](images/7b/7be65c63a4e5dcbe73fbc63faf3f061476ecd478cef8d58776bb843bfe84df2c.png)" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308440 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308087 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308440 1069 "q-333298" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308440 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308440 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={y}^{2}-3x,\\,y(1.5)=-2.5\n$$\n\nUse the implicit Euler method with one step and Newton's method to approximate $y(2).$ Round your answer to two decimal places.\n\n$y(2)≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308440 1072 #uuid "58a4758b-1f60-58d7-b78d-eae37f012256" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308440 1074 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nSince we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=2,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=2-1.5=0.5.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the implicit Euler method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-3{x}_{\\text{new}})\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-1.5{x}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and move all terms to the left-hand side and define the resulting expression as $g({y}_{\\text{new}}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-1.5{x}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-{y}_{\\text{new}}+y-1.5{x}_{\\text{new}}=0.\n$$\n\nSo, the general form of the function $g$ for step size $Δx=0.5$ is\n\n$$\ng({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-{y}_{\\text{new}}+y-1.5{x}_{\\text{new}}.\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=1.5+0.5=2.$ Then, the function $g$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({y}_{\\text{new}}) & =0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-{y}_{\\text{new}}-2.5-1.5(2) \\\\  & =0.5({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-{y}_{\\text{new}}-5.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use a root-finding method with starting point $y=-2.5$ to solve $g({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.$ We'll use Newton's method. \n\n$$\n({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n+1}=({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-\\frac{g({y}_{\\text{new}})}{{g}^{′}({y}_{\\text{new}})}\n$$\n\nNote that the derivative of $g$ is\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}({y}_{\\text{new}})=({y}_{\\text{new}})-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton's method iterative scheme is as follows:\n\n$$\n({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n+1}=({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-\\frac{0.5(({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n})^{2}-({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-5.5}{(({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n})-1}\n$$\n\nApplying several iterations of Newton's method starting with $y(1.5)=-2.5,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({y}_{\\text{new}})_{1} & =-2.5 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{2} & =-2.464286 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{3} & ≈-2.464102 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{4} & ≈-2.464102\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(2)≈-2.46,$ rounded to two decimal places." 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308441 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307633 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308441 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307784 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308441 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307857 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308441 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308000 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308441 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308020 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308441 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308063 1