455 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308455 1071 "Consider the initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-y,\\,y(0)=3\n$$\n\nSuppose we approximate the solution using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the exact solution is ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)=3{e}^{-x},$ compute the local truncation error ${τ}_{6}$ for the step starting at $x=1$ with step size $Δx=0.2.$ Round your answer to three decimal places.\n\n${τ}_{6}≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308455 1072 #uuid "5bd8b593-3906-5326-a083-7655db534bde" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308455 1074 "The local truncation error (LTE) is the error made in a single step of a numerical method, when the step begins from an exact value. The LTE of a numerical method at step $n$ from $x$ with exact value ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)$ to ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{n} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-({y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)+Δy).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that the exact solution ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)=3{e}^{-x}.$ So, the $y$-value at the start of the step $x=1$ is ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(1)=3{e}^{-1}.$\n\nNow, let's find the $y$-increment given by the numerical method.\n\nFirst, the slope at the start of the step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{exact}}^{′}(1) & =-{y}_{\\text{exact}}(1) \\\\  & =-3{e}^{-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the increment at this step is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}^{′}(1)\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =-3{e}^{-1}\\cdot 0.2 \\\\  & =-0.6{e}^{-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the exact value at ${x}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}}) & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(x+Δx) \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(1+0.2) \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(1.2) \\\\  & =3{e}^{-1.2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the LTE for the step starting at $x=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{6} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(1.2)-({y}_{\\text{exact}}(1)+Δy) \\\\  & =3{e}^{-1.2}-(3{e}^{-1}-0.6{e}^{-1}) \\\\  & =3{e}^{-1.2}-2.4{e}^{-1} \\\\  & ≈0.021\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308456 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307638 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308456 1069 "q-333388" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308456 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308456 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-2y=13\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x),\\,y(0)=2\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-2y=13\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{2x},$ solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308456 1072 #uuid "27ab4606-cbf1-51bb-a168-d3d147fc0481" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308456 1074 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-2{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $13\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x).$ So, we assume the particular solution to be \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}=-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n(-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))-2(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))=13\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(3\\beta -2\\alpha )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+(-3\\alpha -2\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)=13\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}3\\beta -2\\alpha =13\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)) \\\\ -3\\alpha -2\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-2,\\beta =3.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{2x}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=2$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{2\\cdot 0}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot 0)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot 0) & =2 \\\\ A-2-0 & =2 \\\\ A & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=4{e}^{2x}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308457 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307934 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308457 1069 "q-333396" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308457 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308457 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+4y=25\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x),\\,y(0)=2\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+4y=25\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{-4x},$ solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308457 1072 #uuid "f099c9b2-7941-5737-8989-a92879efe9bc" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308457 1074 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+4{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-4x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $25\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).$ So, we assume the particular solution to be \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}=-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n(-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))+4(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))=25\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(3\\beta +4\\alpha )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+(-3\\alpha +4\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)=25\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}3\\beta +4\\alpha =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)) \\\\ -3\\alpha +4\\beta =25\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-3,\\beta =4.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-4x}-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=2$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{-4\\cdot 0}-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot 0)+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot 0) & =2 \\\\ A-3-0 & =2 \\\\ A & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=5{e}^{-4x}-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308458 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308303 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308458 1069 "q-333398" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308458 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308458 1071 "Which of the following statements are true when testing the stability of Euler's method on the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),\\,y(0)=6,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda =\\frac{8}{3},$ and step size $Δx?$  \n\n- The update formula can be written as ${y}_{n+1}={(1-\\frac{8}{3}Δx)}^{n}{y}_{n}.$\n- The method is stable for $|1-\\frac{8}{3}Δx|<1.$\n- The method is stable for step size $Δx=0.25.$\n- The method is **unstable** for step size $Δx=0.8.$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308458 1072 #uuid "4be1bdfb-f8fa-566e-b0ec-d9b21f97e58a" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308458 1074 "A numerical method ${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$ is stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=6,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nNow, let's check the stability of Euler's Method by applying it to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\frac{8}{3}y,\\,y(0)=6.\n$$\n\nFirst, for an initial value problem ${y}^{′}=f(x,y),y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},$ Euler's method is given by the update formula\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})\\cdot Δx.\n$$\n\nNow, let's check our statements:\n\n- Applying Euler's method to the test equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+(-\\frac{8}{3}{y}_{n})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}-\\frac{8}{3}{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(1-\\frac{8}{3}Δx){y}_{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, statement I is false.\n\n- For stability, we require ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty .$ Here, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n} & =(1-\\frac{8}{3}Δx){y}_{n-1} \\\\  & =(1-\\frac{8}{3}Δx)((1-\\frac{8}{3}Δx){y}_{n-2}) \\\\  & ={(1-\\frac{8}{3}Δx)}^{2}{y}_{n-2} \\\\  & \\,\\,⋮\\,\\,⋮ \\\\  & ={(1-\\frac{8}{3}Δx)}^{n}{y}_{0} \\\\  & =6{(1-\\frac{8}{3}Δx)}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${y}_{n}$ converges to $0$ as $n\\to \\infty $ if and only if $|1-\\frac{8}{3}Δx|<1,$ as stated. Hence, statement II is true.\n\n- Expanding the inequality $|1-\\frac{8}{3}Δx|<1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & <1-\\frac{8}{3}Δx<1 \\\\ -2 & <-\\frac{8}{3}Δx<0 \\\\ 0 & <\\frac{8}{3}Δx<2 \\\\ 0 & <Δx<\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine stability for the given step sizes:\n\n- Since $Δx=0.25$ satisfies the above inequality, Euler's method is stable for this value. Hence, statement III is true.\n\n- Since $Δx=0.8$ does not satisfy the above inequality, Euler's method is unstable for this value. Hence, statement IV is true.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II, III, and IV only\"." 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308459 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307748 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308459 1069 "q-333415" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308459 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308459 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2x+3xy,\\,y(-2)=-1\n$$\n\nThe **implicit** Euler method is applied with step size $Δx=\\frac{1}{3}.$ \nFind the general formula for the new $y$ value, ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ in terms of the new value ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and the current value $y.$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308459 1072 #uuid "38a8fb1c-bb08-5c09-bfb9-bbf72e76e47a" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308459 1074 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nIn general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(2{x}_{\\text{new}}+3{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}})\\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{\\text{new}}+{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{\\text{new}}+{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}}-{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}}(1-{x}_{\\text{new}}) & =y+\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ 3{y}_{\\text{new}}(1-{x}_{\\text{new}}) & =3y+2{x}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}}(3-3{x}_{\\text{new}}) & =3y+2{x}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{3y+2{x}_{\\text{new}}}{3-3{x}_{\\text{new}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308460 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308223 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308460 1069 "q-333421" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308460 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308460 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2x-{x}^{2}+3xy,\\,y(0)=2\n$$\n\nThe **implicit** Euler method is applied with step size $Δx=1.$ \nFind the general formula for the new $y$ value, ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ in terms of the new value ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and the current value $y.$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308460 1072 #uuid "aa45ca56-c957-54d9-a390-a0ee7bab7259" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308460 1074 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nIn general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(2{x}_{\\text{new}}-{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}+3{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}})\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2{x}_{\\text{new}}-{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}+3{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+2{x}_{\\text{new}}-{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}+3{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}}-3{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+2{x}_{\\text{new}}-{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}}(1-3{x}_{\\text{new}}) & =y+2{x}_{\\text{new}}-{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{y+2{x}_{\\text{new}}-{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}}{1-3{x}_{\\text{new}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308461 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308243 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308461 1069 "q-333423" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308461 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308461 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=4{x}^{2}-5{x}^{2}y,\\,y(1)=0\n$$\n\nThe **implicit** Euler method is applied with step size $Δx=\\frac{1}{5}.$ \nFind the general formula for the new $y$ value, ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ in terms of the new value ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and the current value $y.$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308461 1072 #uuid "52f06dd5-43de-58de-9feb-624aff1688ef" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308461 1074 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nIn general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(4{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}-5{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}{y}_{\\text{new}})\\cdot \\frac{1}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}-{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{4}{5}{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}-{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}}+{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{4}{5}{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}}(1+{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}) & =y+\\frac{4}{5}{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2} \\\\ 5{y}_{\\text{new}}(1+{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}) & =5y+4{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}}(5+5{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}) & =5y+4{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{5y+4{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}}{5+5{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308462 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308311 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308462 1069 "q-333495" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308462 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308462 1071 "Suppose we wish to approximate the solution to an initial value problem using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf the exact solution is ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)=5x,$ find an expression for the local truncation error for the step from $x$ to ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx.$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308462 1072 #uuid "bdd54755-b7fd-5cb1-b947-e81fa8b77e0e" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308462 1074 "The local truncation error (LTE) is the error made in a single step of a numerical method, when the step begins from an exact value. The LTE of a numerical method at step $n$ from $x$ with exact value ${y}_{\\text{exact}}$ to ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{n} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-({y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)+Δy).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that the exact solution of the initial value problem is ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)=5x.$ Now, let's find the $y$-increment given by the numerical method.\n\nFirst, the slope at a step starting at $x$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{exact}}^{′}(x)=(5x)^{′}=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the increment given by the numerical method is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}^{′}(x)\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =5\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the exact value at ${x}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}}) & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(x+Δx) \\\\  & =5(x+Δx) \\\\  & =5x+5\\cdot Δx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the LTE for step $n$ starting at $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{n} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-({y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)+Δy) \\\\  & =[5x+5\\cdot Δx]-[5x+5\\cdot Δx] \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308463 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308120 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308463 1069 "q-333497" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308463 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308463 1071 "Suppose we wish to approximate the solution to an initial value problem using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf the exact solution is ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)=3{x}^{2}+5x,$ find an expression for the local truncation error for the step from $x$ to ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx.$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308463 1072 #uuid "70483166-7a2c-55a8-b391-a77c951f9240" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308463 1074 "The local truncation error (LTE) is the error made in a single step of a numerical method, when the step begins from an exact value. The LTE of a numerical method at step $n$ from $x$ with exact value ${y}_{\\text{exact}}$ to ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{n} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-({y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)+Δy).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that the exact solution of the initial value problem is ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)=3{x}^{2}+5x.$ Now, let's find the $y$-increment given by the numerical method.\n\nFirst, the slope at a step starting at $x$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{exact}}^{′}(x)=(3{x}^{2}+5x)^{′}=6x+5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the increment given by the numerical method is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}^{′}(x)\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(6x+5)\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the exact value at ${x}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}}) & =({y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)+Δx) \\\\  & =3(x+Δx)^{2}+5(x+Δx) \\\\  & =3({x}^{2}+2x\\cdot Δx+(Δx)^{2})+5x+5\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}+6x\\cdot Δx+3\\cdot (Δx)^{2}+5x+5\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}+(6x+5)\\cdot Δx+3\\cdot (Δx)^{2}+5x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the LTE for step $n$ starting at $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{n} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-({y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)+Δy) \\\\  & =[3{x}^{2}+(6x+5)\\cdot Δx+3\\cdot (Δx)^{2}+5x]-[3{x}^{2}+5x+(6x+5)\\cdot Δx] \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}-3{x}^{2}+((6x+5)-(6x+5))\\cdot Δx+5x-5x+3\\cdot (Δx)^{2} \\\\  & =3\\cdot (Δx)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308464 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308139 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308464 1069 "q-333498" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308464 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308464 1071 "Suppose we wish to approximate the solution to an initial value problem using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf the exact solution is ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)=\\frac{1}{x},$ find an expression for the local truncation error for the step from $x$ to ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx.$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308464 1072 #uuid "10354088-c0e4-5ab7-aa11-decc50fd2a44" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308464 1074 "The local truncation error (LTE) is the error made in a single step of a numerical method, when the step begins from an exact value. The LTE of a numerical method at step $n$ from $x$ with exact value ${y}_{\\text{exact}}$ to ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{n} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-({y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)+Δy).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that the exact solution of the initial value problem is ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)=\\frac{1}{x}.$ Now, let's find the $y$-increment given by the numerical method.\n\nFirst, the slope at a step starting at $x$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{exact}}^{′}(x)={(\\frac{1}{x})}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the increment given by the numerical method is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}^{′}(x)\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the exact value at ${x}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}}) & =({y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)+Δx) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x+Δx}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the LTE for step $n$ starting at $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{n} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-({y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)+Δy) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x+Δx}-(\\frac{1}{x}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\cdot Δx)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308465 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307532 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308465 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307587 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308465 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307817 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308465 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307847 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308465 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307928 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308465 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307960 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308465 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308135 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308465 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308171 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308465 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308195 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308465 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308275 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308465 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308289 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308465 1069 "q-333503" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308465 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308465 1071 "![](images/e5/e5c236defa66a00f12cd338ed0012cf91e4d7c15aa4865fc2b1edf5b0e2ea1da.png)\n\nThe phase portrait for a system of linear differential equations whose matrix has two real, distinct eigenvalues is shown above.\nThe eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ${v}_{1}=[2,\\text{ }1]^{T}$ and ${v}_{2}=[-1,\\text{ }1]^{T},$ respectively. \nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nThe tangents along this line are pointing \n{{field-2}},\nmeaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nmust approach \n{{field-3}} \nas $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have \n{{field-4}}.\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny={{field-5}}.\n$$\n\nThe tangents along this line are pointing \n{{field-6}},\nmeaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nmust approach \n{{field-7}} \nas $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have \n{{field-8}}.\n\nFinally, since the solutions become parallel to the straight-line solution containing the vector {{field-9}} as they move away from the origin, the dominant eigenvalue is \n{{field-10}}.\n\nTherefore,\n{{field-11}} \n$>0.$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308465 1072 #uuid "8b5a36f6-c7fc-5f39-8a18-1389505413c3" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308465 1074 "The straight-line solutions of the system have the form\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{and}\\,{c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{2}x,\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/97/97f7eb1fbddcd9d927447978efd4c42e705d6835bf0cc74525d42ded8c35346b.png)\n\nThe tangents along this line are pointing $\\text{away from the origin},$ meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nmust approach $\\text{infinity}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{1}>0.$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=-x,\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/ed/edc697ab38ae9a56d46181e0a45982b7045224de0cc3c4a786ddbc515f22196c.png)\n\nThe tangents along this line are pointing $\\text{away from the origin},$ meaning that the solutions of the form \n${c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nmust approach $\\text{infinity}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{2}>0.$\n\nSo, for this particular system, both eigenvalues are positive. The dominant eigenvalue is the one with the greatest magnitude.\n\nNow, since the solutions become parallel to the straight-line solution containing the vector ${v}_{1}$ as they move away from the origin, the dominant eigenvalue is ${\\lambda }_{1}.$\n\nTherefore, ${\\lambda }_{1}>{\\lambda }_{2}>0.$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308466 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307546 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308466 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307715 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308466 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308143 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308466 1069 "q-333506" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308466 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308466 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\nx{y}^{″}+3y{y}^{′}=4x,\\,y(1)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=1\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method by introducing $z={y}^{′}$ and reducing the equation to a system of first-order ODEs. What entries should be placed in the columns for $Δy$ and $Δz$ in the table below if the step size is $Δx=1?$\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ |  |  | {{field-1}} |  |  | {{field-2}} |\n\nTherefore, $y(2)≈$\n{{field-3}}." 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308466 1072 #uuid "8243f1c6-90ba-550c-8b86-bedf747a8525" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308466 1074 "For a second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=f(x,y,{y}^{′}),\\,y(a)=c,\\,{y}^{′}(a)=d,\n$$\n\nwe can use Euler's method to approximate a solution by first reducing the second-order ODE to a system of first-order ODEs,\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′}=f(x,y,z),\\, & z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nbefore applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nTo apply Euler’s method, we first isolate ${y}^{″}$ so it can serve as the derivative of a new variable $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{y}^{″}+3y{y}^{′} & =4x \\\\ x{y}^{″} & =4x-3y{y}^{′} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =\\frac{4x-3y{y}^{′}}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variable $z={y}^{′},{z}^{′}={y}^{″}$ in the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}=\\frac{4x-3yz}{x}.\n$$\n\nNote that the initial value of $z$ is $z(1)={y}^{′}(1)=1.$ So, the initial value problem reduces to the following system of first-order ODEs:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(1)=2, \\\\ {z}^{′}=\\frac{4x-3yz}{x},\\, & z(1)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed with Euler's method by applying the update rule component-wise.\n\nFirst, because the initial conditions are $y(1)=2$ and $z(1)=1,$ we place $y=2$ and $z=1$ in the table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ |  |  | $1$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute both ${y}^{′}$ and ${z}^{′}$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=1 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =\\frac{4x-3yz}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\cdot 1-3\\cdot 2\\cdot 1}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4-6}{1} \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $1$ |  | $1$ | $-2$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ and $Δz$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-2$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x,$ $Δy$ to $y,$ and $Δz$ to $z,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=2+1=3 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=1+(-2)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-2$ |\n| $2$ | $3$ |  |  | $-1$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(2)≈3.$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308467 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307688 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308467 1069 "q-333546" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308467 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308467 1071 "Which of the following statements are true when testing the stability of the implicit Euler method on the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),y(0)=1,x\\ge 0,\\lambda =6,$ and step size $Δx?$  \n\n- The stability function is $R(6Δx)=\\frac{1}{1-6Δx}.$\n- The stability region is $|\\frac{1}{1+6Δx}|<1.$\n- The method is absolutely stable.\n- The method is **unstable** for step size $Δx=0.10.$\n\n*Hint:* The stability function $R$ is given by ${y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n}.$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308467 1072 #uuid "ed443d52-d157-5213-99c2-1ffa8b393121" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308467 1074 "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda Δx)$ is the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda Δx)|<1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nA method that is stable for all possible values $\\lambda Δx>0$ is called absolutely stable.\n\nNow, let's check the stability of the implicit Euler method by applying it to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-6y,\\,y(0)=1.\n$$\n\nFirst, for an initial value problem ${y}^{′}=f(x,y),y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},$ the implicit Euler method is given by the update formula\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+f({x}_{n+1},{y}_{n+1})\\cdot Δx.\n$$\n\nWe now examine our statements:\n\n- Applying the implicit Euler method to the test equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+(-6{y}_{n+1})\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}_{n+1}+6{y}_{n+1}\\cdot Δx & ={y}_{n} \\\\ {y}_{n+1}(1+6Δx) & ={y}_{n} \\\\ {y}_{n+1} & =\\frac{1}{1+6Δx}\\cdot {y}_{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the stability function is \n\n$$\nR(6Δx)=\\frac{1}{1+6Δx}.\n$$\n\nThis shows that the statement I is false.\n\n- The method is stable when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(6Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |\\frac{1}{1+6Δx}| & <1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${y}_{n}$ converges to $0$ as $n\\to \\infty $ if and only if\n$|\\frac{1}{1+6Δx}|<1.$\nHence, statement II is true.\n\n- Now, since $1+6Δx>1$ for any positive step size $Δx,$ we have\n\n$$\n|\\frac{1}{1+6Δx}|<1\n$$\n\nfor all $Δx>0.$ So, the implicit Euler method is absolutely stable, and statement III is true.\n\n- Finally, because the implicit Euler method is absolutely stable, it is stable for step size $Δx=0.10.$ Hence, statement IV is false.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"." 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308468 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307683 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308468 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307872 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308468 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308186 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308468 1069 "q-333548" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308468 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308468 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-x{y}^{′}+6y={x}^{2},\\,y(2)=-3,\\,{y}^{′}(2)=2\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method by introducing $z={y}^{′}$ and reducing the equation to a system of first-order ODEs. What entries should be placed in the columns for $Δy$ and $Δz$ in the table below if the step size is $Δx=1?$\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ |  |  | {{field-1}} |  |  | {{field-2}} |\n\nTherefore, $y(3)≈$\n{{field-3}}." 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308468 1072 #uuid "b486cf66-a5b3-57fb-929d-d0ddb031084d" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308468 1074 "For a second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=f(x,y,{y}^{′}),\\,y(a)=c,\\,{y}^{′}(a)=d,\n$$\n\nwe can use Euler's method to approximate a solution by first reducing the second-order ODE to a system of first-order ODEs,\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′}=f(x,y,z),\\, & z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nbefore applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nTo apply Euler’s method, we first isolate ${y}^{″}$ so it can serve as the derivative of a new variable $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}-x{y}^{′}+6y & ={x}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =x{y}^{′}-6y+{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variable $z={y}^{′}$ and ${z}^{′}={y}^{″}$ in the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}=xz-6y+{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that the initial value of $z$ is $z(2)={y}^{′}(2)=2.$ So, the initial value problem reduces to the following system of first-order ODEs:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(2)=-3, \\\\ {z}^{′}=xz-6y+{x}^{2},\\, & z(2)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed with Euler's method by applying the update rule component-wise.\n\nFirst, because the initial conditions are $y(2)=-3$ and $z(2)=2,$ we place $y=-3$ and $z=2$ in the table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $-3$ |  |  | $2$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute both ${y}^{′}$ and ${z}^{′}$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=2 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =xz-6y+{x}^{2} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2-6\\cdot (-3)+{2}^{2} \\\\  & =4+18+4 \\\\  & =26\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $-3$ | $2$ |  | $2$ | $26$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ and $Δz$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =26\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =26\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $-3$ | $2$ | $2$ | $2$ | $26$ | $26$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x,$ $Δy$ to $y,$ and $Δz$ to $z,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=2+1=3 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-3+2=-1 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=2+26=28\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $-3$ | $2$ | $2$ | $2$ | $26$ | $26$ |\n| $3$ | $-1$ |  |  | $28$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(3)≈-1.$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308469 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307761 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308469 1069 "q-333563" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308469 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308469 1071 "Find ${g}^{′}(x)$ if $g(x)={\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{3}+1}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t.$\n\n${g}^{′}(x)=$ \n{{field-1}} ${e}^{{x}^{3}+1}$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308469 1072 #uuid "5e7ef1dc-79fa-5798-a4dc-fff368523998" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308469 1074 "To compute ${g}^{′}(x),$ we first make the substitution $u={x}^{3}+1$ and then we apply the chain rule, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{3}+1}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}{\\int }_{0}^{u}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe integrand is continuous on $[0,u].$ Therefore, by FTC II, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}{\\int }_{0}^{u}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot {e}^{u} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3}+1)\\cdot {e}^{{x}^{3}+1} \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}\\cdot {e}^{{x}^{3}+1} \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}\\,{e}^{{x}^{3}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308470 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307743 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308470 1069 "q-333572" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308470 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308470 1071 "Which of the following statements are true when testing the stability of the trapezoidal method on the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),y(0)=2,x\\ge 0,\\lambda =5,$ and step size $Δx?$  \n\n- The stability function is $R(5Δx)=\\frac{1-5Δx/2}{1+5Δx/2}.$\n- The stability region is $|\\frac{2-5Δx}{2+5Δx}|<1.$\n- The method is absolutely stable.\n- The method is **unstable** for step size $Δx=1.$\n\n*Hint:* The stability function $R$ is given by ${y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n}.$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308470 1072 #uuid "6a2ae136-3885-5a0c-a306-c58f92aeffc9" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308470 1074 "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=2,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda Δx)$ is the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda Δx)|<1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nA method that is stable for all possible values $\\lambda Δx>0$ is called absolutely stable.\n\nNow, let's check the stability of the trapezoidal method by applying it to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-5y,\\,y(0)=2.\n$$\n\nFirst, for an initial value problem ${y}^{′}=f(x,y),y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},$ the trapezoidal method is given by the update formula\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})+f({x}_{n+1},{y}_{n+1}))\\cdot Δx.\n$$\n\nWe now examine our statements:\n\n- Applying the trapezoidal method to this test equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})+f({x}_{n+1},{y}_{n+1}))\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(-5{y}_{n}+(-5{y}_{n+1}))\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}_{n+1}+\\frac{5}{2}{y}_{n+1}\\cdot Δx & ={y}_{n}-\\frac{5}{2}{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx \\\\ (1+\\frac{5}{2}Δx){y}_{n+1} & =(1-\\frac{5}{2}Δx){y}_{n} \\\\ {y}_{n+1} & =\\frac{1-5Δx/2}{1+5Δx/2}\\cdot {y}_{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the stability function is $R(5Δx)=\\frac{1-5Δx/2}{1+5Δx/2}.$ Hence, statement I is true.\n\n- The stability region of the method is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(5Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |\\frac{1-5Δx/2}{1+5Δx/2}| & <1 \\\\ |\\frac{2-5Δx}{2+5Δx}| & <1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, statement II is true.\n\n- Rearranging, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{|2-5Δx|}{|2+5Δx|} & <1 \\\\ |2-5Δx| & <|2+5Δx| \\\\ (2-5Δx)^{2} & <(2+5Δx)^{2} \\\\ 4-20Δx+25(Δx)^{2} & <4+20Δx+25(Δx)^{2} \\\\ -20Δx & <20Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Δx>0,$ this inequality is always true. Thus, the trapezoidal method is absolutely stable, and statement III is true.\n\n- Finally, since it is absolutely stable, the trapezoidal method is stable for step size $Δx=1,$ and hence statement IV is false.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only\"." 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308471 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307777 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308471 1069 "q-333573" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308471 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308471 1071 "Find ${g}^{′}(x)$ if $g(x)={\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{3}}({t}^{3}-2t)\\,\\text{d}t.$\n\n${g}^{′}(x)=$ \n{{field-1}}" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308471 1072 #uuid "407d28b6-654e-53f4-ad14-142f1a8c32eb" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308471 1074 "To compute ${g}^{′}(x),$ we first make the substitution $u={x}^{3}$ and then we apply the chain rule, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{3}}({t}^{3}-2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}{\\int }_{0}^{u}({t}^{3}-2t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe integrand is continuous on $[0,u].$ Therefore, by FTC II, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}{\\int }_{0}^{u}({t}^{3}-2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot ({u}^{3}-2