17592186045585 true] [17592186308481 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307807 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308481 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307826 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308481 1069 "q-333616" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308481 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308481 1071 "The function ${y}_{0}(x)={x}^{2}$ is a particular solution of the Riccati differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x{y}^{2}-2x({x}^{2}+1)y+x({x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+2).\n$$\n\nExpress this equation in the Bernoulli form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x){z}^{n}\n$$\n\nand state the functions $P(x)$ and $Q(x),$ and the value of $n.$\n\n$P(x)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$,\\,Q(x)=$\n{{field-2}}\n$,\\,n=$\n{{field-3}}" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308481 1072 #uuid "e0a5403b-70cd-58bb-afde-12cd974de3af" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308481 1074 "A Riccati differential equation has the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=a(x){y}^{2}+b(x)y+c(x).\n$$\n\nTo reduce the given Riccati equation to a Bernoulli equation, we use the substitution\n\n$$\ny(x)=z(x)+{y}_{0}(x),\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{0}(x)$ is a known particular solution.\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)={z}^{′}(x)+{y}_{0}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nIn our case, ${y}_{0}(x)={x}^{2}.$ Since ${y}_{0}^{′}(x)=2x$, we have\n\n$$\ny=z+{x}^{2}\\,⇒\\,{y}^{′}={z}^{′}+2x.\n$$\n\nLet's substitute these expressions into our Riccati equation:\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}+2x=x(z+{x}^{2})^{2}-2x({x}^{2}+1)(z+{x}^{2})+x({x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+2).\n$$\n\nNow, we expand the right-hand side, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{′}+2x & =x(z+{x}^{2})^{2}-2x({x}^{2}+1)(z+{x}^{2})+x({x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}+2) \\\\ {z}^{′}+2x & =x{z}^{2}-2xz+2x \\\\ {z}^{′}+2xz & =x{z}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a Bernoulli equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x){z}^{n}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(x)=2x,\\,Q(x)=x,\\,n=2.\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308482 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307694 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308482 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307970 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308482 1069 "q-333619" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308482 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308482 1071 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=3x(t)+2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-2x(t)-y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above, where the system's matrix has repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ of multiplicity two. Select the correct options below regarding the given system.$\\,$ The equilibrium point (origin) is a \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}\nnode." 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308482 1072 #uuid "701d41a8-54c4-5085-a286-95e1ba1a0460" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308482 1074 "The matrix of our system is\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- Since the only (repeated) eigenvalue of $A$ is positive, we have a $\\text{unstable}$ node.\n\n- Since $A$ is not diagonal and has a repeated eigenvalue, there is only one linearly independent eigenvector. So, we have a $\\text{degenerate}$ node.\n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/21/21b115b90b81f2f1bf3ecad3c8e200dae0ccd50c84459e047297e70e22efcb6f.png)" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308483 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307563 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308483 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307679 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308483 1069 "q-333620" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308483 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308483 1071 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=3x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-x(t)+y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above, where the system's matrix has repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of multiplicity two. Select the correct options below regarding the given system.$\\,$ The equilibrium point (origin) is a \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}\nnode." 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308483 1072 #uuid "f8861ee7-e573-5d09-b7e5-a9dfbae7172d" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308483 1074 "The matrix of our system is\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- Since the only (repeated) eigenvalue of $A$ is positive, we have a $\\text{unstable}$ node.\n\n- Since $A$ is not diagonal and has a repeated eigenvalue, there is only one linearly independent eigenvector. So, we have a $\\text{degenerate}$ node.\n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/97/97a85038b240896686ef1a796d81acc6aa52d01627f3aeca4268a309badc4867.png)" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308484 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307627 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308484 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308022 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308484 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308308 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308484 1069 "q-333622" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308484 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308484 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\nx{y}^{″}+4y{y}^{′}-3y=3{x}^{2},\\,y(1)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=0\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method by introducing $z={y}^{′}$ and reducing the equation to a system of first-order ODEs. What entries should be placed in the columns for $Δy$ and $Δz$ in the table below if the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{2}?$\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ |  |  | {{field-1}} |  |  | {{field-2}} |\n\nTherefore, $y(\\frac{3}{2})≈$\n{{field-3}}." 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308484 1072 #uuid "621f0602-05e8-50ed-bc75-f491feb3bd4b" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308484 1074 "For a second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=f(x,y,{y}^{′}),\\,y(a)=c,\\,{y}^{′}(a)=d,\n$$\n\nwe can use Euler's method to approximate a solution by first reducing the second-order ODE to a system of first-order ODEs,\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′}=f(x,y,z),\\, & z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nbefore applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nTo apply Euler’s method, we first isolate ${y}^{″}$ so it can serve as the derivative of a new variable $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{y}^{″}+4y{y}^{′}-3y & =3{x}^{2} \\\\ x{y}^{″} & =3{x}^{2}-4y{y}^{′}+3y \\\\ {y}^{″} & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}-4y{y}^{′}+3y}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variable $z={y}^{′},{z}^{′}={y}^{″}$ in the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}=\\frac{3{x}^{2}-4yz+3y}{x}.\n$$\n\nNote that the initial value of $z$ is $z(1)={y}^{′}(1)=0.$ So, the initial value problem reduces to the following system of first-order ODEs:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(1)=1, \\\\ {z}^{′}=\\frac{3{x}^{2}-4yz+3y}{x},\\, & z(1)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed with Euler's method by applying the update rule component-wise.\n\nFirst, because the initial conditions are $y(1)=1$ and $z(1)=0,$ we place $y=1$ and $z=0$ in the table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ |  |  | $0$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute both ${y}^{′}$ and ${z}^{′}$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=0 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}-4yz+3y}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\cdot {1}^{2}-4\\cdot 1\\cdot 0+3\\cdot 1}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3+0+3}{1} \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $0$ |  | $0$ | $6$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ and $Δz$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =0\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =0 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $6$ | $3$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x,$ $Δy$ to $y,$ and $Δz$ to $z,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+0=1 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=0+3=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $6$ | $3$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $1$ |  |  | $3$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(\\frac{3}{2})≈1.$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308485 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307941 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308485 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308050 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308485 1069 "q-333631" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308485 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308485 1071 "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t)+[\\begin{aligned}-14\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-15\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-11\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}],\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the missing coefficients in the solution to the initial value problem, shown above.\n\n$x(t)=$ \n{{field-1}} \n$\\,\\cdot \\,[\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{-2t} \\\\ {e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}]\\,+$\n{{field-2}}\n$\\,\\cdot \\,[\\begin{aligned}(2t-1){e}^{-2t} \\\\ t{e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308485 1072 #u