)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308486 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307663 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308486 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308069 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308486 1069 "q-333636" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308486 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308486 1071 "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}]x(t)+[\\begin{aligned}7\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ -5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}],\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the missing coefficients in the solution to the initial value problem, shown above.\n\n$x(t)=$ \n{{field-1}} \n$\\,\\cdot \\,[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t){e}^{-t} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t){e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}]\\,-$\n{{field-2}}\n$\\,\\cdot \\,[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t){e}^{-t} \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t){e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308486 1072 #uuid "f64f96f7-ae58-50bb-8fb6-64c4e2209bc5" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308486 1074 "We are told that the general solution to the system is \n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t){e}^{-t} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t){e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t){e}^{-t} \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t){e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}$ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nNext, we find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot 0){e}^{-0} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot 0){e}^{-0}\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot 0){e}^{-0} \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot 0){e}^{-0}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot 0)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot 0) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot 0)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot 0)\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}+3 \\\\ -{c}_{2}+2\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}+3=7 \\\\ -{c}_{2}+2=7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get ${c}_{1}=4.$ From the second equation, we have ${c}_{2}=-5.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\nx(t)=4\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t){e}^{-t} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t){e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}]-5\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t){e}^{-t} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t){e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308487 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307557 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308487 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307884 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308487 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308097 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308487 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308272 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308487 1069 "q-333637" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308487 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308487 1071 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)+3y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=3x(t)+y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ${v}_{1}=[1,\\text{ }1]^{T}$ and ${v}_{2}=[-1,\\text{ }1]^{T},$ respectively. Select the correct options below regarding the given system.$\\,$\n{{field-1}} is {{field-2}} line.\n\n$\\,$\n$y=-x$ is {{field-3}} line.\n\n$\\,$\nThe equilibrium point (origin) is {{field-4}}." 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308487 1072 #uuid "ca7ddbc3-cff0-528a-94e0-d6fbf22a4ad5" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308487 1074 "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=x.\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line and approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, $y=x$ is $\\text{an unstable}$ line.\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=-x.\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line and approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, $y=-x$ is $\\text{a stable}$ line.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{a saddle point}.$ \n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/a0/a09d50fa36b2748fb1c65ed16e20d950afbbabc18884ff8718cf1571c4e13b0e.png)" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308488 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307757 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308488 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308187 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308488 1069 "q-333638" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308488 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308488 1071 "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t)+[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}],\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the missing coefficients in the solution to the initial value problem, shown above.\n\n$x(t)=$ \n{{field-1}} \n$\\,\\cdot \\,[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]\\,+$\n{{field-2}}\n$\\,\\cdot \\,[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308488 1072 #uuid "a94fc120-f50c-5dd4-b643-1a93a1df3e1c" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308488 1074 "We are told that the general solution to the system is \n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}$ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nNext, we find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot 0) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot 0)\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot 0) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot 0)\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot 0)+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot 0) \\\\ -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot 0)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot 0)\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}+1 \\\\ {c}_{2}-2\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}+1=4 \\\\ {c}_{2}-2=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get ${c}_{1}=3.$ From the second equation, we have ${c}_{2}=3.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\nx(t)=3\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]+3\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308489 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307544 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308489 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308057 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308489 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308134 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308489 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308334 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308489 1069 "q-333643" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308489 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308489 1071 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=2x(t)+3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2,$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ${v}_{1}=[-1,\\text{ }1]^{T}$ and ${v}_{2}=[-1,\\text{ }2]^{T},$ respectively. Select the correct options below regarding the given system.$\\,$\n$y=-x$ is {{field-1}} line.\n\n$\\,$\n{{field-2}} is {{field-3}} line.\n\n$\\,$\nThe equilibrium point (origin) is {{field-4}}." 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308489 1072 #uuid "1c833496-a809-5eea-acb1-bfc822315c03" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308489 1074 "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}{e}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=-x.\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line and approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, $y=-x$ is $\\text{an unstable}$ line.\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=-2x.\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line and approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, $y=-2x$ is $\\text{an unstable}$ line.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{an unstable node (source)}.$ \n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/45/450d43a4cbf6dad8d54edbb66fc4a6e44e491290e1dc5d1303057fb0eb7928a7.png)" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308490 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307806 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308490 1069 "q-333648" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308490 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308490 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=({x}^{2}+1)y+2x,\\,y(0)=-2\n$$\n\nThe **implicit** Euler method is applied with step size $Δx=\\frac{1}{5}.$ \nFind the general formula for the new $y$ value, ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ in terms of the new value ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and the current value $y.$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308490 1072 #uuid "bfedde66-6790-5018-90d2-508742648cd4" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308490 1074 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nIn general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}+{y}_{\\text{new}}+2{x}_{\\text{new}})\\cdot \\frac{1}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}+\\frac{1}{5}{y}_{\\text{new}}+\\frac{2}{5}{x}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{5}({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}+\\frac{1}{5}{y}_{\\text{new}}+\\frac{2}{5}{x}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}}-\\frac{1}{5}({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}-\\frac{1}{5}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{2}{5}{x}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}}(1-\\frac{1}{5}({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-\\frac{1}{5}) & =y+\\frac{2}{5}{x}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ 5{y}_{\\text{new}}(\\frac{4}{5}-\\frac{1}{5}({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}) & =5y+2{x}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}}(4-({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}) & =5y+2{x}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{5y+2{x}_{\\text{new}}}{4-({x}_{\\text{new}})^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308491 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307670 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308491 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307750 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308491 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307770 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308491 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307902 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308491 1069 "q-333652" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308491 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308491 1071 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-4x(t)+2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=x(t)-5y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-6,$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ${v}_{1}=[2,\\text{ }1]^{T}$ and ${v}_{2}=[-1,\\text{ }1]^{T},$ respectively. Select the correct options below regarding the given system.$\\,$\n$y=\\frac{1}{2}x$ is {{field-1}} line.\n\n$\\,$\n{{field-2}} is {{field-3}} line.\n\n$\\,$\nThe equilibrium point (origin) is {{field-4}}." 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308491 1072 #uuid "2eca828e-dfcc-5f55-ae88-5c22040f1e5a" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308491 1074 "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{-6t}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{2}x.\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line and approach the origin as as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, $y=\\frac{1}{2}x$ is $\\text{a stable}$ line.\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=-x.\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{-6t}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line and approach the origin as as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, $y=-x$ is $\\text{a stable}$ line.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{a stable node (sink)}.$ \n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/a2/a21b978aaab9569ae4dcae346116e51b5248fdf8f3cff461411a2140f70a6a4c.png)" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308492 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307802 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308492 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308001 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308492 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308081 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308492 1069 "q-333653" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308492 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308492 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2x{y}^{′}+3{y}^{2}={x}^{2},\\,y(1)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=2\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method by introducing $z={y}^{′}$ and reducing the equation to a system of first-order ODEs. What entries should be placed in the columns for $Δy$ and $Δz$ in the table below if the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{2}?$\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ |  |  | {{field-1}} |  |  | {{field-2}} |\n\nTherefore, $y(\\frac{3}{2})≈$\n{{field-3}}." 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308492 1072 #uuid "650988e1-bb3f-55e1-a363-a37c84fed3d8" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308492 1074 "For a second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=f(x,y,{y}^{′}),\\,y(a)=c,\\,{y}^{′}(a)=d,\n$$\n\nwe can use Euler's method to approximate a solution by first reducing the second-order ODE to a system of first-order ODEs,\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′}=f(x,y,z),\\, & z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nbefore applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nTo apply Euler’s method, we first isolate ${y}^{″}$ so it can serve as the derivative of a new variable $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}+2x{y}^{′}+3{y}^{2} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{″} & ={x}^{2}-2x{y}^{′}-3{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variable $z={y}^{′}$ and ${z}^{′}={y}^{″}$ in the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}={x}^{2}-2xz-3{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that the initial value of $z$ is $z(1)={y}^{′}(1)=2.$\nSo, the initial value problem reduces to the following system of first-order ODEs:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(1)=1, \\\\ {z}^{′}={x}^{2}-2xz-3{y}^{2},\\, & z(1)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed with Euler's method by applying the update rule component-wise.\n\nFirst, because the initial conditions are $y(1)=1$ and $z(1)=2,$ we place $y=1$ and $z=2$ in the table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ |  |  | $2$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute both ${y}^{′}$ and ${z}^{′}$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=2 \\\\ {z}^{′} & ={x}^{2}-2xz-3{y}^{2} \\\\  & ={1}^{2}-2\\cdot 1\\cdot 2-3\\cdot {1}^{2} \\\\  & =1-4-3 \\\\  & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $2$ |  | $2$ | $-6$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ and $Δz$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =1 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(-6)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $2$ | $-6$ | $-3$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x,$ $Δy$ to $y,$ and $Δz$ to $z,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+1=2 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=2+(-3)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $2$ | $-6$ | $-3$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $2$ |  |  | $-1$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that \n$y(\\frac{3}{2})≈2.$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308493 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307668 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308493 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307722 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308493 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307965 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308493 1069 "q-333654" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308493 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308493 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-x{y}^{′}+{y}^{3}={x}^{3},\\,y(0)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=1\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method by introducing $z={y}^{′}$ and reducing the equation to a system of first-order ODEs. What entries should be placed in the columns for $Δy$ and $Δz$ in the table below if the step size is $Δx=\\frac{1}{2}?$\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ |  |  | {{field-1}} |  |  | {{field-2}} |\n\nTherefore, $y(\\frac{1}{2})≈$\n{{field-3}}." 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308493 1072 #uuid "761efed4-6b1b-5741-bad4-ad0cca8f5a8f" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308493 1074 "For a second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=f(x,y,{y}^{′}),\\,y(a)=c,\\,{y}^{′}(a)=d,\n$$\n\nwe can use Euler's method to approximate a solution by first reducing the second-order ODE to a system of first-order ODEs,\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′}=f(x,y,z),\\, & z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nbefore applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nTo apply Euler’s method, we first isolate ${y}^{″}$ so it can serve as the derivative of a new variable $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}-x{y}^{′}+{y}^{3} & ={x}^{3} \\\\ {y}^{″} & ={x}^{3}+x{y}^{′}-{y}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variable $z={y}^{′},{z}^{′}={y}^{″}$ in the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}={x}^{3}+xz-{y}^{3}.\n$$\n\nNote that the initial value of $z$ is $z(0)={y}^{′}(0)=1.$\nSo, the initial value problem reduces to the following system of first-order ODEs:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(0)=1, \\\\ {z}^{′}={x}^{3}+xz-{y}^{3},\\, & z(0)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed with Euler's method by applying the update rule component-wise.\n\nFirst, because the initial conditions are $y(0)=1$ and $z(0)=1,$ we place $y=1$ and $z=1$ in the table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ |  |  | $1$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute both ${y}^{′}$ and ${z}^{′}$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=1 \\\\ {z}^{′} & ={x}^{3}+xz-{y}^{3} \\\\  & ={0}^{3}+0\\cdot 1-{1}^{3} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ |  | $1$ | $-1$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ and $Δz$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x,$ $Δy$ to $y,$ and $Δz$ to $z,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=1+(-\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ |  |  | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny(\\frac{1}{2})≈\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308494 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308077 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308494 1069 "q-333680" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308494 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308494 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2x-2y-3,\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nTo approximate the solution, one step of the Runge-Kutta method has already been computed, resulting in the following estimate:\n\n$$\ny(1)≈0\n$$\n\nUse the $2$-Step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton Method with one step to approximate $y(2).$\n\n*Hint: The corrector equation of the two-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method is defined as*\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\n*where ${y}_{p}$ denotes the predictor at the current step, and ${y}_{-1}^{′}$ is the estimated slope at the previous point.*" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308494 1072 #uuid "862cb215-e1c8-5e4c-a0a2-cda6cb44f797" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308494 1074 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe $2$-Step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton (ABM $2$-step) Method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{-1}^{′}=f({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point (${x}_{-1},{y}_{-1}),$ and\n\n- ${y}_{p}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})$ is the estimated slope at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})=(x+Δx,y+Δ{y}_{p}).$\n\nBefore we start, let's add the initial condition data and Runge-Kutta step data to a table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ |  | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $0$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nNow, since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=2,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=2-1=1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the values of ${y}^{′}$ at each point according to the given rule:\n\n- The slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})=(0,1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{-1}^{′} & =2{x}_{-1}-2{y}_{-1}-3 \\\\  & =2\\cdot 0-2\\cdot 1-3 \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The slope at the current point $(x,y)=(1,0)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2x-2y-3 \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1-2\\cdot 0-3 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-5$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $-1$ |  |  |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δ{y}_{p}$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot (-1)-(-5))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-5$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $1$ |  |  |  |\n\nSo, the coordinates of the predicted new point are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2, \\\\ {y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p}=0+1=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add the second value to the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-5$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $1$ |  |  |\n\nHence, the slope at this predicted point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =2{x}_{\\text{new}}-2{y}_{p}-3 \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2-2\\cdot 1-3 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-5$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $-1$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(5\\cdot (-1)+8\\cdot (-1)-(-5))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot (-8) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-5$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-\\frac{2}{3}$ |\n\nThe value of $x$ in the next row is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=2.$ To get the value of $y$ in the next row, we add $Δy$ to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =0+(-\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-5$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-\\frac{2}{3}$ |\n| $2$ | $-\\frac{2}{3}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(2)≈-\\frac{2}{3}.$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308495 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307528 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308495 1069 "q-333682" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308495 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308495 1071 "A linear system of ODEs has the equilibrium at ${x}^{∗}=[1,\\,-1]^{T}.$ Two linearly independent eigenvectors of the system's matrix are ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}.$ After shifting using $u=x-{x}^{∗},$ the system becomes ${u}^{′}=Au.$ Which of the following is **guaranteed** to be an eigenvector of the matrix $A?$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308495 1072 #uuid "dc707ae3-5d07-512f-ba6d-4a6cde416b9f" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308495 1074 "After shifting a linear system, the eigenvalues and the corresponding eigenvectors remain unchanged.\n\nThus, the matrix $A$ in the shifted system has the same eigenvectors as the system's matrix in the original coordinates. These eigenvectors are ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2},$ or any nonzero vectors that are parallel to them.\n\nAmong the given options, the only vector that is nonzero and is guaranteed to be parallel to either ${v}_{1}$ or ${v}_{2}$ is the vector $-{v}_{2}.$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308496 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308224 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308496 1069 "q-333686" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308496 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308496 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=6x-2y,\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nTo approximate the solution, one step of the Runge-Kutta method has already been computed, resulting in the following estimate:\n\n$$\ny(1)≈\\frac{7}{3}\n$$\n\nUse the $2$-Step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton Method with one step to approximate $y(2).$\n\n*Hint: The corrector equation of the two-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method is defined as*\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{