_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot (-\\frac{11}{2})-(-\\frac{1}{2}))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-\\frac{11}{2}$ | $-8$ |  |  |  |\n\nSo, the coordinates of the predicted new point are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2, \\\\ {y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p}=2+(-8)=-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add the second value to the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-\\frac{11}{2}$ | $-8$ | $-6$ |  |  |\n\nHence, the slope at this predicted point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =3{x}_{\\text{new}}-4{y}_{p}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2-4\\cdot (-6)-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{59}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-\\frac{11}{2}$ | $-8$ | $-6$ | $\\frac{59}{2}$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(5\\cdot \\frac{59}{2}+8\\cdot (-\\frac{11}{2})-(-\\frac{1}{2}))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot 104 \\\\  & =\\frac{26}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-\\frac{11}{2}$ | $-8$ | $-6$ | $\\frac{59}{2}$ | $\\frac{26}{3}$ |\n\nThe value of $x$ in the next row is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=2.$ To get the value of $y$ in the next row, we add $Δy$ to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =2+\\frac{26}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-\\frac{11}{2}$ | $-8$ | $-6$ | $\\frac{59}{2}$ | $\\frac{26}{3}$ |\n| $2$ | $\\frac{32}{3}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(2)≈\\frac{32}{3}.$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308499 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307967 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308499 1069 "q-333693" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308499 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308499 1071 "Which of the following expressions for the global truncation error ($e$) or local truncation error ($τ$) represent a third-order numerical method?\n\n- $τ(Δx)=2(Δx)^{2}$\n- $e(Δx)=3(Δx)^{3}-3(Δx)^{2}$\n- $τ(Δx)=(Δx)^{4}-2(Δx)^{5}$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308499 1072 #uuid "81f632f0-dedf-5479-9362-313bdb74c812" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308499 1074 "For a given numerical method, if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that\n\n- the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0,\n$$\n\n- the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0,\n$$\n\nthen the method is of order $p.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option in turn:\n\n- For the LTE expression $τ(Δx)=2(Δx)^{2},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $2(Δx)^{2}=2(Δx)^{1+1}.$ So, statement I corresponds to an order $1$ method.\n\n- For the GTE expression $e(Δx)=3(Δx)^{3}-3(Δx)^{2},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $-3(Δx)^{2}.$ So, statement II corresponds to an order $2$ method.\n\n- For the LTE expression $τ(Δx)=(Δx)^{4}-2(Δx)^{5},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $(Δx)^{4}=(Δx)^{3+1}.$ So, statement III corresponds to an order $3$ method.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308500 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308300 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308500 1069 "q-333696" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308500 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308500 1071 "$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{3t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5{e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe Wronskian of the vector-valued functions given above is $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=${{field-1}}." 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308500 1072 #uuid "0db7040f-f565-57af-8a26-d71dd11bd9ca" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308500 1074 "The Wronskian $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})$ of two vector-valued functions \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t)\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{22}(t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis defined as\n\n$$\nW({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=det({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11}(t) & {x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t) & {x}_{22}(t)\\end{aligned}|.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Wronskian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({x}_{1},{x}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{3t} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5{e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2{e}^{3t}\\cdot 5{e}^{-2t}-0\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =10{e}^{3t-2t} \\\\  & =10{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308501 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308221 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308501 1069 "q-333704" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308501 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308501 1071 "$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}+1 \\\\ t\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe Wronskian of the vector-valued functions given above is $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=${{field-1}}." 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308501 1072 #uuid "85f7fe3c-a4f1-5f77-bb76-b7c289145c6c" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308501 1074 "The Wronskian $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})$ of two vector-valued functions \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t)\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{22}(t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis defined as\n\n$$\nW({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=det({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11}(t) & {x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t) & {x}_{22}(t)\\end{aligned}|.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Wronskian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({x}_{1},{x}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}+1 & t \\\\ t & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =({t}^{2}+1)\\cdot 1-t\\cdot t \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+1-{t}^{2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308502 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307771 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308502 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307939 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308502 1069 "q-333710" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308502 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308502 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\nx{y}^{″}-3y{y}^{′}=2{x}^{2},\\,y(1)=-1,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=1\n$$\n\nUse Euler's Method with one step to approximate $y(\\frac{3}{2})$ and ${y}^{′}(\\frac{3}{2}).$\n\n$y(\\frac{3}{2})≈$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,{y}^{′}(\\frac{3}{2})≈$\n{{field-2}}" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308502 1072 #uuid "c6b42415-80aa-5fa1-b545-54cb2b76e654" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308502 1074 "For a second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=f(x,y,{y}^{′}),\\,y(a)=c,\\,{y}^{′}(a)=d,\n$$\n\nwe can use Euler's method to approximate a solution by first reducing the second-order ODE to a system of first-order ODEs,\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′}=f(x,y,z)\\, & z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nbefore applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nTo apply Euler’s method, we first isolate ${y}^{″}$ so it can serve as the derivative of a new variable $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{y}^{″}-3y{y}^{′} & =2{x}^{2} \\\\ x{y}^{″} & =2{x}^{2}+3y{y}^{′} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}+3y{y}^{′}}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variable $z={y}^{′},{z}^{′}={y}^{″}$ in the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}=\\frac{2{x}^{2}+3yz}{x}.\n$$\n\nNote that the initial value of $z$ is\n$z(1)={y}^{′}(1)=1.$\nSo, the initial value problem reduces to the following system of first-order ODEs:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(1)=-1, \\\\ {z}^{′}=\\frac{2{x}^{2}+3yz}{x},\\, & z(1)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed with Euler's method by applying the update rule component-wise.\n\nSince we want to find the values of $y$ and ${y}^{′}=z$ at $x=\\frac{3}{2},$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{3}{2}-1=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we place the initial condition data in the table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ |  |  | $1$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute both ${y}^{′}$ and ${z}^{′}$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=1 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}+3yz}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot {1}^{2}+3\\cdot (-1)\\cdot 1}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2-3}{1} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $1$ |  | $1$ | $-1$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δy$ and $Δz$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x,$ $Δy$ to $y,$ and $Δz$ to $z,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-1+\\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=1+(-\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ |  |  | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(\\frac{3}{2})≈-\\frac{1}{2}$\nand\n${y}^{′}(\\frac{3}{2})=z(\\frac{3}{2})≈\\frac{1}{2}.$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308503 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307559 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308503 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307893 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308503 1069 "q-333715" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308503 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308503 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear ODEs ${x}^{′}=Ax$ with the matrix $A$ shown above. Compute the **standard** fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ for this system.\n\n| ${Φ}_{0}(t)=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\,$ |  | ${e}^{2t}+{e}^{-2t}$ | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} | ${e}^{2t}+{e}^{-2t}$ |  |  |" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308503 1072 #uuid "eff0b088-3db3-5966-a1d7-4ea54d40635c" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308503 1074 "Suppose $A$ is a constant $n\\times n$ matrix and $x(t)$ is an $n\\times 1$ column vector. The standard fundamental matrix of the linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ denoted ${Φ}_{0}(t),$ is the unique matrix solution of the initial value problem \n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}^{′}(t)=A{Φ}_{0}(t),\\,{Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere ${I}_{n}$ is the identity matrix. \n\nFurthermore, it can be shown that ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}.$\n\nWe have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]t+{\\alpha }_{0}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0} & 2{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ 2{\\alpha }_{1}t & {\\alpha }_{0}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are constants to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues of $At$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=-2t,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=2t,\n$$\n\nboth of multiplicity one. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}r(-2t)={e}^{-2t} \\\\ r(2t)={e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}-2t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{-2t} \\\\ 2t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n- Subtraction the second equation from the first one, we get\n\n$$\n-4t{\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{-2t}-{e}^{2t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{4t}({e}^{-2t}-{e}^{2t}).\n$$\n\n- Adding the first and the second equations, we get\n\n$$\n2{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{-2t}+{e}^{2t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{0}=\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{-2t}+{e}^{2t}).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0} & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{-2t}+{e}^{2t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{2t}+{e}^{-2t}) \\\\ 2{\\alpha }_{1}t & =2\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{4t}({e}^{-4t}-{e}^{4t}))t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{2t}-{e}^{-2t})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t}+{e}^{-2t} & {e}^{2t}-{e}^{-2t} \\\\ {e}^{2t}-{e}^{-2t} & {e}^{2t}+{e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308504 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307652 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308504 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307672 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308504 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307680 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308504 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307900 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308504 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307906 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308504 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307988 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308504 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308070 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308504 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308175 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308504 1069 "q-333727" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308504 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308504 1071 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=25x(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1,2}=\\pm 5\\text{i},$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ${v}_{1,2}=[\\pm \\text{i},5]^{T},$ respectively. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\ 5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t) \\\\ 5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFor example, when ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we obtain the following curve:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x={{field-1}} \\\\ y={{field-2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{20}$ | $\\f