6045585 true] [17592186308508 1071 "The function ${y}_{0}(x)=1$ is a particular solution of the Riccati differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=(x+1){y}^{2}-xy-1.\n$$\n\nExpress this equation in the Bernoulli form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x){z}^{n}\n$$\n\nand state the functions $P(x)$ and $Q(x),$ and the value of $n.$\n\n$P(x)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$,\\,Q(x)=$\n{{field-2}}\n$,\\,n=$\n{{field-3}}" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308508 1072 #uuid "01643b84-3e13-51b7-9547-6888a498e8d9" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308508 1074 "A Riccati differential equation has the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=a(x){y}^{2}+b(x)y+c(x).\n$$\n\nTo reduce the given Riccati equation to a Bernoulli equation, we use the substitution\n\n$$\ny(x)=z(x)+{y}_{0}(x),\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{0}(x)$ is a known particular solution.\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)={z}^{′}(x)+{y}_{0}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nIn our case, ${y}_{0}(x)=1.$ Since ${y}_{0}^{′}(x)=0$, we have\n\n$$\ny=z+1\\,⇒\\,{y}^{′}={z}^{′}.\n$$\n\nLet's substitute these expressions into our Riccati equation:\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}=(x+1)(z+1)^{2}-x(z+1)-1.\n$$\n\nNow, we expand the right-hand side, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{′} & =(x+1)({z}^{2}+2z+1)-xz-x-1 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =(x+1){z}^{2}+(x+2)z \\\\ {z}^{′}-(x+2)z & =(x+1){z}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a Bernoulli equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x){z}^{n}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(x)=-(x+2),\\,Q(x)=x+1,\\,n=2.\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308509 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308280 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308509 1069 "q-333782" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308509 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308509 1071 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=4x(t)+2y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives all equilibrium points of the system of linear differential equations above?" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308509 1072 #uuid "85e6fc84-64ed-533c-94e3-94bfdcc415ac" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308509 1074 "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x+y=0 \\\\ 4x+2y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the second equation is a multiple of the first one:\n\n$$\n2(2x+y)=4x+2y,\n$$\n\nso we can disregard the second equation.\n\nFrom the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n2x+y=0\\,⇒\\,y=-2x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium points are given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ -2k\\end{aligned}],\\,k\\in R.\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308510 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307744 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308510 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307815 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308510 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307853 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308510 1069 "q-333790" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308510 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308510 1071 "The function ${y}_{0}(x)={e}^{-x}$ is a particular solution of the Riccati differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={e}^{-x}{y}^{2}-(x+2{e}^{-2x})y+(x{e}^{-x}-{e}^{-x}+{e}^{-3x}).\n$$\n\nExpress this equation in the Bernoulli form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x){z}^{n}\n$$\n\nand state the functions $P(x)$ and $Q(x),$ and the value of $n.$\n\n$P(x)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$,\\,Q(x)=$\n{{field-2}}\n$,\\,n=$\n{{field-3}}" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308510 1072 #uuid "fad288eb-6596-5439-b270-942379b7ff30" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308510 1074 "A Riccati differential equation has the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=a(x){y}^{2}+b(x)y+c(x).\n$$\n\nTo reduce the given Riccati equation to a Bernoulli equation, we use the substitution\n\n$$\ny(x)=z(x)+{y}_{0}(x),\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{0}(x)$ is a known particular solution.\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)={z}^{′}(x)+{y}_{0}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nIn our case, ${y}_{0}(x)={e}^{-x}.$ Since ${y}_{0}^{′}(x)=-{e}^{-x}$, we have\n\n$$\ny=z+{e}^{-x}\\,⇒\\,{y}^{′}={z}^{′}-{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nLet's substitute these expressions into our Riccati equation:\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}-{e}^{-x}={e}^{-x}(z+{e}^{-x})^{2}-(x+2{e}^{-2x})(z+{e}^{-x})+(x{e}^{-x}-{e}^{-x}+{e}^{-3x}).\n$$\n\nNow, we expand the right-hand side, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{′}-{e}^{-x} & ={e}^{-x}(z+{e}^{-x})^{2}-(x+2{e}^{-2x})(z+{e}^{-x})+(x{e}^{-x}-{e}^{-x}+{e}^{-3x}) \\\\ {z}^{′}-{e}^{-x} & ={e}^{-x}{z}^{2}-xz-{e}^{-x} \\\\ {z}^{′}+xz & ={e}^{-x}{z}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a Bernoulli equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x){z}^{n}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(x)=x,\\,Q(x)={e}^{-x},\\,n=2.\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308511 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307709 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308511 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307767 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308511 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308096 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308511 1069 "q-333792" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308511 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308511 1071 "The function ${y}_{0}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ is a particular solution of the Riccati differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=4{y}^{2}-(8\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+4)y+(4{sin}^{2}⁡x+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x).\n$$\n\nExpress this equation in the Bernoulli form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x){z}^{n}\n$$\n\nand state the functions $P(x)$ and $Q(x),$ and the value of $n.$\n\n$P(x)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$,\\,Q(x)=$\n{{field-2}}\n$,\\,n=$\n{{field-3}}" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308511 1072 #uuid "839b2059-18bf-5b84-9bf7-bf583f1251e2" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308511 1074 "A Riccati differential equation has the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=a(x){y}^{2}+b(x)y+c(x).\n$$\n\nTo reduce the given Riccati equation to a Bernoulli equation, we use the substitution\n\n$$\ny(x)=z(x)+{y}_{0}(x),\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{0}(x)$ is a known particular solution.\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)={z}^{′}(x)+{y}_{0}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nIn our case, ${y}_{0}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ Since ${y}_{0}^{′}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$, we have\n\n$$\ny=z+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,⇒\\,{y}^{′}={z}^{′}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nLet's substitute these expressions into our Riccati equation:\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=4(z+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{2}-(8\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+4)(z+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+(4{sin}^{2}⁡x+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x).\n$$\n\nNow, we expand the right-hand side, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{′}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =4(z+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{2}-(8\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+4)(z+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+(4{sin}^{2}⁡x+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\ {z}^{′}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =4{z}^{2}-4z+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {z}^{′}+4z & =4{z}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a Bernoulli equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x){z}^{n}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(x)=4,\\,Q(x)=4,\\,n=2.\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308512 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308323 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308512 1069 "q-333793" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308512 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308512 1071 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=3x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=6x(t)+2y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives an equation of the line in the phase space that contains all equilibrium points of the system above?" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308512 1072 #uuid "b011e10e-bb41-5771-a1d6-58fcc04ea1fd" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308512 1074 "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 6 & 2\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3x+y=0 \\\\ 6x+2y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the second equation is a multiple of the first one. So, we can disregard the second equation. \n\nFrom the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n3x+y=0\\,⇒\\,y=-3x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium points are given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ -3k\\end{aligned}],\\,k\\in R.\n$$\n\nThis determines the line with the equation $y=-3x.$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308513 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307911 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308513 1069 "q-333794" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308513 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308513 1071 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-x(t)+2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-2x(t)+4y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives an equation of the line in the phase space that contains all equilibrium points of the system above?" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308513 1072 #uuid "46c9474c-2ae5-534d-9ad7-6fa31cf39bbb" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308513 1074 "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-x+2y=0 \\\\ -2x+4y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the second equation is a multiple of the first one. So, we can disregard the second equation. \n\nFrom the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n-x+2y=0\\,⇒\\,2y=x\\,⇒\\,y=\\frac{x}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium points are given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ \\frac{k}{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,k\\in R.\n$$\n\nThis determines the line with the equation $y=\\frac{x}{2}.$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308514 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308149 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308514 1069 "q-333795" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308514 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308514 1071 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=4x(t)-2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=8x(t)-4y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives an equation of the line in the phase space that contains all equilibrium points of the system above?" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308514 1072 #uuid "823bb65d-36ce-5de4-8512-ee5806601897" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308514 1074 "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ 8 & -4\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4x-2y=0 \\\\ 8x-4y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the second equation is a multiple of the first one. So, we can disregard the second equation. \n\nFrom the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n4x-2y=0\\,⇒\\,2x-y=0\\,⇒\\,y=2x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium points are given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ 2k\\end{aligned}],\\,k\\in R.\n$$\n\nThis determines the line with the equation $y=2x.$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308515 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307957 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308515 1069 "q-333800" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308515 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308515 1071 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)+2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=2x(t)+4y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives an equation of the line in the phase space that contains all equilibrium points of the system above?" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308515 1072 #uuid "42732689-96ca-57a8-bbe6-329c180fcabd" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308515 1074 "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+2y=0 \\\\ 2x+4y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the second equation is a multiple of the first one. So, we can disregard the second equation. \n\nFrom first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx+2y=0\\,⇒\\,2y=-x\\,⇒\\,y=-\\frac{x}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium points are given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ -\\frac{k}{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,k\\in R.\n$$\n\nThis determines the line with the equation $y=-\\frac{x}{2}.$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308516 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307841 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308516 1069 "q-333801" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308516 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308516 1071 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-3x(t)+4y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-6x(t)+8y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives an equation of the line in the phase space that contains all equilibrium points of the system above?" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308516 1072 #uuid "9ccbbaf5-d549-5415-8348-44e0b67f6944" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308516 1074 "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 4 \\\\ -6 & 8\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-3x+4y=0 \\\\ -6x+8y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the second equation is a multiple of the first one. So, we can disregard the second equation. \n\nFrom first equation, we get\n\n$$\n-3x+4y=0\\,⇒\\,4y=3x\\,⇒\\,y=\\frac{3}{4}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium points are given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ \\frac{3}{4}k\\end{aligned}],\\,k\\in R.\n$$\n\nThis determines the line with the equation $y=\\frac{3}{4}x.$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308517 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307984 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308517 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308335 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308517 1069 "q-333817" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308517 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308517 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny{y}^{″}-4x{y}^{′}=2{x}^{2},\\,y(1)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=0\n$$\n\nUse Euler's Method with one step to approximate $y(2)$ and ${y}^{′}(2).$\n\n$y(2)≈$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,{y}^{′}(2)≈$\n{{field-2}}" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308517 1072 #uuid "efb769a3-227a-5223-907e-04a6d0f28687" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308517 1074 "For a second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=f(x,y,{y}^{′}),\\,y(a)=c,\\,{y}^{′}(a)=d,\n$$\n\nwe can use Euler's method to approximate a solution by first reducing the second-order ODE to a system of first-order ODEs,\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′}=f(x,y,z)\\, & z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nbefore applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nTo apply Euler’s method, we first isolate ${y}^{″}$ so it can serve as the derivative of a new variable $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y{y}^{″}-4x{y}^{′} & =2{x}^{2} \\\\ y{y}^{″} & =2{x}^{2}+4x{y}^{′} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}+4x{y}^{′}}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variable $z={y}^{′},{z}^{′}={y}^{″}$ in the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}=\\frac{2{x}^{2}+4xz}{y}.\n$$\n\nNote that the initial value of $z$ is\n$z(1)={y}^{′}(1)=0.$\nSo, the initial value problem reduces to the following system of first-order ODEs:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(1)=1, \\\\ {z}^{′}=\\frac{2{x}^{2}+4xz}{y},\\, & z(1)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed with Euler's method by applying the update rule component-wise.\n\nSince we want to find the values of $y$ and ${y}^{′}=z$ at $x=2,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=2-1=1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we place the initial condition data in the table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ |  |  | $0$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute both ${y}^{′}$ and ${z}^{′}$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=0 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}+4xz}{y} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot {1}^{2}+4\\cdot 1\\cdot 0}{1} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $0$ |  | $0$ | $2$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δy$ and $Δz$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =0\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =0 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $2$ | $2$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x,$ $Δy$ to $y,$ and $Δz$ to $z,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+0=1 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=0+2=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $2$ | $2$ |\n| $2$ | $1$ |  |  | $2$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(2)≈1$\nand\n${y}^{′}(2)=z(2)≈2.$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308518 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307717 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308518 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307837 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308518 1069 "q-333822" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308518 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308518 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n3y{y}^{″}-({y}^{′})^{2}=4x,\\,y(1)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=1\n$$\n\nUse Euler's Method with one step to approximate $y(2)$ and ${y}^{′}(2).$\n\n$y(2)≈$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,{y}^{′}(2)≈$\n{{field-2}}" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308518 1072 #uuid "6c80a951-167d-5473-8188-804c65b01fc9" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308518 1074 "For a second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=f(x,y,{y}^{′}),\\,y(a)=c,\\,{y}^{′}(a)=d,\n$$\n\nwe can use Euler's method to approximate a solution by first reducing the second-order ODE to a system of first-order ODEs,\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′}=f(x,y,z)\\, & z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nbefore applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nTo apply Euler’s method, we first isolate ${y}^{″}$ so it can serve as the derivative of a new variable $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3y{y}^{″}-({y}^{′})^{2} & =4x \\\\ 3y{y}^{″} & =4x+({y}^{′})^{2} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =\\frac{4x+({y}^{′})^{2}}{3y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variable $z={y}^{′},{z}^{′}={y}^{″}$ in the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}=\\frac{4x+{z}^{2}}{3y}.\n$$\n\nNote that the initial value of $z$ is\n$z(1)={y}^{′}(1)=1.$\nSo, the initial value problem reduces to the following system of first-order ODEs:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(1)=1, \\\\ {z}^{′}=\\frac{4x+{z}^{2}}{3y},\\, & z(1)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed with Euler's method by applying the update rule component-wise.\n\nSince we want to find the values of $y$ and ${y}^{′}=z$ at $x=2,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=2-1=1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we place the initial condition data in the table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ |  |  | $1$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute both ${y}^{′}$ and ${z}^{′}$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=1 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =\\frac{4x+{z}^{2}}{3y} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\cdot 1+{1}^{2}}{3\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4+1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $1$ |  | $1$ | $\\frac{5}{3}$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δy$ and $Δz$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $\\frac{5}{3}$ | $\\frac{5}{3}$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x,$ $Δy$ to $y,$ and $Δz$ to $z,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+1=2 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=1+\\frac{5}{3}=\\frac{8}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $\\frac{5}{3}$ | $\\frac{5}{3}$ |\n| $2$ | $2$ |  |  | $\\frac{8}{3}$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(2)≈2$\nand\n${y}^{′}(2)=z(2)≈\\frac{8}{3}.$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308519 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307866 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308519 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308010 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308519 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308131 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308519 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308274 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308519 1069 "q-333823" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308519 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308519 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem for the functions $y=y(x),z=z(x):$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=6x+2y+z,\\, & y(1)=-1 \\\\ {z}^{′}=2x-y+z,\\, & z(1)=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method.\nWhat entries should be placed in the columns for $Δy$ and $Δz$ in the table below if the step size is $Δx=1?$\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ |  |  | {{field-1}} |  |  | {{field-2}} |\n\nTherefore, $y(2)≈$\n{{field-3}}\nand $z(2)≈$\n{{field-4}}." 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308519 1072 #uuid "c2c65ca2-2af9-541e-bd05-a2ff48b05cfb" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308519 1074 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=f(x,y,z),\\, & y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′}=g(x,y,z),\\, & z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEuler's method approximates the solution by applying the update rule component-wise to each equation. More precisely, the increments on $y$ and $z$ are\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}Δy={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx, \\\\ Δz={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe new values are then given by ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}=z+Δz.$\n\nFirst, because the initial conditions are $y(1)=-1$ and $z(1)=3,$ we place $y=-1$ and $z=3$ in the table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ |  |  | $3$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute both ${y}^{′}$ and ${z}^{′}$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =6x+2y+z \\\\  & =6\\cdot 1+2\\cdot (-1)+3 \\\\  & =6-2+3 \\\\  & =7 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =2x-y+z \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1-(-1)+3 \\\\  & =2+1+3 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $7$ |  | $3$ | $6$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ and $Δz$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =7\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =7, \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =6\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $7$ | $7$ | $3$ | $6$ | $6$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x,$ $Δy$ to $y,$ and $Δz$ to $z,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2, \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-1+7=6, \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=3+6=9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $7$ | $7$ | $3$ | $6$ | $6$ |\n| $2$ | $6$ |  |  | $9$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(2)≈6$ and $z(2)≈9.$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308520 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307556 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308520 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307754 13194139533415 17592186045