-y+3z \\\\  & =2\\cdot 0-3+3\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =0-3-6 \\\\  & =-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $3$ | $4$ |  | $-2$ | $-9$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ and $Δz$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =4\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =8 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(-9)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $3$ | $4$ | $8$ | $-2$ | $-9$ | $-18$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x,$ $Δy$ to $y,$ and $Δz$ to $z,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+2=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=3+8=11 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=-2+(-18)=-20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $3$ | $4$ | $8$ | $-2$ | $-9$ | $-18$ |\n| $2$ | $11$ |  |  | $-20$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(2)≈11$\nand\n$z(2)≈-20.$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308521 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307915 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308521 1069 "q-333832" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308521 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308521 1071 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)-y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-3x(t)+3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=4,$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ${v}_{1}=[1,\\text{ }1]^{T}$ and ${v}_{2}=[-1,\\text{ }3]^{T},$ respectively. Which of the following could represent the phase portrait of the system?\n\n*Hint: Here, the dotted line represents the equilibrium solutions.*" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308521 1072 #uuid "7b9dc234-d83d-52ed-858a-43cf39227d57" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308521 1074 "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nLet's now sketch the phase portrait.\n\n- First, notice that ${x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ are the equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/18/18fad31b9f69e55d9f4bc414d3e1b732eaee7685bf36a183033fc302e911eb82.png)\n\n- Now, the line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n$y=-3x$\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line. Since ${e}^{4t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n![](images/2b/2bfda58fee727ccdc9f424140e2b8143bf5afe426ef0b1fa72105272d9a1b41e.png)\n\nAll other non-equilibrium solutions are parallel to the vector ${v}_{2}.$ So, the phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/f0/f0821717299fb0b9858409db9a93a610a75e0564a2b60f5bfcd6eca26d2909ec.png)" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308522 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308083 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308522 1069 "q-333837" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308522 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308522 1071 "Suppose we wish to approximate the solution to an initial value problem using the following numerical method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =(xy+{y}^{2})\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf the exact solution is ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)=3{x}^{2}+5x,$ find an expression for the local truncation error for the step from $x$ to ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx.$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308522 1072 #uuid "726f6f46-0815-5100-8694-30312d46b17e" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308522 1074 "The local truncation error (LTE) is the error made in a single step of a numerical method, when the step begins from an exact value. The LTE of a numerical method at step $n$ from $x$ with exact value ${y}_{\\text{exact}}$ to ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{n} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-({y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)+Δy).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that the exact solution of the initial value problem is ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)=3{x}^{2}+5x.$ Now, let's find the $y$-increment given by the numerical method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =(x\\cdot {y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)+{y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)^{2})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(x\\cdot (3{x}^{2}+5x)+(3{x}^{2}+5x)^{2})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(3{x}^{3}+5{x}^{2}+9{x}^{4}+30{x}^{3}+25{x}^{2})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(9{x}^{4}+33{x}^{3}+30{x}^{2})\\cdot Δx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the exact value at ${x}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}}) & =({y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)+Δx) \\\\  & =3(x+Δx)^{2}+5(x+Δx) \\\\  & =3({x}^{2}+2x\\cdot Δx+(Δx)^{2})+5x+5(Δx) \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}+6x\\cdot Δx+3(Δx)^{2}+5x+5(Δx) \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}+5x+(6x+5)\\cdot Δx+3(Δx)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the LTE for step $n$ starting at $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{n} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\  & =[3{x}^{2}+5x+(6x+5)\\cdot Δx+3(Δx)^{2}]-[3{x}^{2}+5x+(9{x}^{4}+33{x}^{3}+30{x}^{2})\\cdot Δx] \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}-3{x}^{2}+5x-5x+(6x+5)\\cdot Δx+3(Δx)^{2}-(9{x}^{4}+33{x}^{3}+30{x}^{2})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(6x+5)\\cdot Δx+3(Δx)^{2}-(9{x}^{4}+33{x}^{3}+30{x}^{2})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(-9{x}^{4}-33{x}^{3}-30{x}^{2}+6x+5)\\cdot Δx+3(Δx)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308523 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307860 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308523 1069 "q-333838" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308523 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308523 1071 "Suppose we wish to approximate the solution to an initial value problem using the following numerical method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf the exact solution is ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)={x}^{2}+1,$ find an expression for the local truncation error for the step from $x$ to ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx.$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308523 1072 #uuid "baf3125d-0d25-502b-857d-9bbe768e2a62" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308523 1074 "The local truncation error (LTE) is the error made in a single step of a numerical method, when the step begins from an exact value. The LTE of a numerical method at step $n$ from $x$ with exact value ${y}_{\\text{exact}}$ to ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{n} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-({y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)+Δy).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that the exact solution of the initial value problem is ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)={x}^{2}+1.$ Now, let's find the $y$-increment given by the numerical method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+{y}_{\\text{exact}}(x))\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+{x}^{2}+1)\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+x+1)\\cdot Δx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the exact value at ${x}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}}) & =({y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)+Δx) \\\\  & =(x+Δx)^{2}+1 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+2x\\cdot Δx+(Δx)^{2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the LTE for step $n$ starting at $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{n} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\  & =[{x}^{2}+2x\\cdot Δx+(Δx)^{2}+1]-[{x}^{2}+1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+x+1)\\cdot Δx] \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-{x}^{2}+1-1+(2x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+x+1))\\cdot Δx+(Δx)^{2} \\\\  & =(2x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+x+1))\\cdot Δx+(Δx)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308524 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307751 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308524 1069 "q-333839" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308524 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308524 1071 "Suppose we wish to approximate the solution to an initial value problem using the following numerical method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =({x}^{2}+\\sqrt{y})\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf the exact solution is ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)={x}^{2}+4x+5,$ find an expression for the local truncation error for the step from $x$ to ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx.$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308524 1072 #uuid "e32b7e1b-4c8b-59e8-9891-eb558702e446" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308524 1074 "The local truncation error (LTE) is the error made in a single step of a numerical method, when the step begins from an exact value. The LTE of a numerical method at step $n$ from $x$ with exact value ${y}_{\\text{exact}}$ to ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{n} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-({y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)+Δy).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that the exact solution of the initial value problem is ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)={x}^{2}+4x+5.$ Now, let's find the $y$-increment given by the numerical method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =({x}^{2}+\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4x+5})\\cdot Δx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the exact value at ${x}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}}) & =(x+Δx)^{2}+4(x+Δx)+5 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+2x\\cdot Δx+(Δx)^{2}+4x+4\\cdot Δx+5 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+4x+5+(2x+4)\\cdot Δx+(Δx)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the LTE for step $n$ starting at $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{n} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\  & =[{x}^{2}+4x+5+(2x+4)\\cdot Δx+(Δx)^{2}]-[{x}^{2}+4x+5+({x}^{2}+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4x+5})\\cdot Δx] \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-{x}^{2}+4x-4x+5-5+(2x+4)\\cdot Δx+(Δx)^{2}-({x}^{2}+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4x+5})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =((2x+4)-{x}^{2}-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4x+5})\\cdot Δx+(Δx)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308525 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307608 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308525 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307974 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308525 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308016 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308525 1069 "q-333843" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308525 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308525 1071 "$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ -3{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-2{e}^{t} \\\\ 6{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in R\n$$\n\nConsider the vector-valued functions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ above. Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are solutions of the same system of linear ODEs, select the correct options in the reasoning below.$\\,$ The Wronskian is $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=$ \n{{field-1}},\nwhich is\n{{field-2}}.  \n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the set ${{x}_{1}(t),{x}_{2}(t)}$ is linearly \n{{field-3}} \non $R.$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308525 1072 #uuid "5afef72e-9d34-5cfb-8e31-ea9c3eb23bfa" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308525 1074 "The Wronskian $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})$ of two vector-valued functions \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t)\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{22}(t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis defined as\n\n$$\nW({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=det({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11}(t) & {x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t) & {x}_{22}(t)\\end{aligned}|.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- If the Wronskian is not identically zero, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If the Wronskian is identically zero, and it's known that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are solutions to the same system of linear ODEs, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, our Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({x}_{1},{x}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} & -2{e}^{t} \\\\ -3{e}^{t} & 6{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =({e}^{t})\\cdot (6{e}^{t})-(-2{e}^{t})\\cdot (-3{e}^{t}) \\\\  & =6{e}^{2t}-6{e}^{2t} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is $\\text{zero for all }t\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the set ${{x}_{1}(t),{x}_{2}(t)}$ is linearly $\\text{dependent}$ on $R.$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308526 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307524 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308526 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308252 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308526 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308259 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308526 1069 "q-333869" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308526 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308526 1071 "$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in R\n$$\n\nConsider the vector-valued functions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ above. Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are solutions of the same system of linear ODEs, select the correct options in the reasoning below.$\\,$ The Wronskian is $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=$ \n{{field-1}},\n\n$\\,$ which is\n{{field-2}}.  \n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the set ${{x}_{1}(t),{x}_{2}(t)}$ is linearly \n{{field-3}} \non $R.$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308526 1072 #uuid "27b787b2-4e51-56eb-90cf-8824cc40ba92" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308526 1074 "The Wronskian $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})$ of two vector-valued functions \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t)\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{22}(t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis defined as\n\n$$\nW({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=det({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11}(t) & {x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t) & {x}_{22}(t)\\end{aligned}|.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- If the Wronskian is not identically zero, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If the Wronskian is identically zero, and it's known that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are solutions to the same system of linear ODEs, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, our Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({x}_{1},{x}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & -3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot (2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot (3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =4{cos}^{2}⁡t+9{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =4{cos}^{2}⁡t+9{sin}^{2}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is $\\text{nonzero for at least one }t\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the set ${{x}_{1}(t),{x}_{2}(t)}$ is linearly $\\text{independent}$ on $R.$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308527 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308073 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308527 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308182 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308527 1069 "q-333875" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308527 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308527 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+x{y}^{′}-2y=0,\\,y(1)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=1\n$$\n\nUse Euler's Method with two steps to approximate $y(2)$ and ${y}^{′}(2).$\n\n$$\ny(2)≈{{field-1}},\\,{y}^{′}(2)≈{{field-2}}\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308527 1072 #uuid "81336a83-b9fd-5624-b50d-8f56705ff853" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308527 1074 "For a second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=f(x,y,{y}^{′}),\\,y(a)=c,\\,{y}^{′}(a)=d,\n$$\n\nwe can use Euler's method to approximate a solution by first reducing the second-order ODE to a system of first-order ODEs,\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′}=f(x,y,z)\\, & z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nbefore applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nTo apply Euler’s method, we first isolate ${y}^{″}$ so it can serve as the derivative of a new variable $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+x{y}^{′}-2y & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =2y-x{y}^{′} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =\\frac{2y-x{y}^{′}}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variable $z={y}^{′},{z}^{′}={y}^{″}$ in the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}=\\frac{2y-xz}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nNote that the initial value of $z$ is $z(1)={y}^{′}(1)=1.$\nSo, the initial value problem reduces to the following system of first-order ODEs:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(1)=2, \\\\ {z}^{′}=\\frac{2y-xz}{{x}^{2}},\\, & z(1)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed with Euler's method by applying the update rule component-wise.\n\nFirst, note that we are given the values of $y$ and $z={y}^{′}$ at $x=1$ and asked to find their values at $x=2.$ The distance from $x=1$ to $x=2$ is $1,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{2-1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(1)=2$ and $z(1)=1.$\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ |  |  | $1$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nWe compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy,$ ${z}^{′}$ and $Δz$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the first row), and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}},$ ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=1 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =\\frac{2y-xz}{{x}^{2}}=\\frac{2\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 1}{{1}^{2}}=3 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=1\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=3\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=2+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=1+\\frac{3}{2}=\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $3$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ |  |  | $\\frac{5}{2}$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nWe compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy,$ ${z}^{′}$ and $Δz$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the second row), and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}},$ ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ {z}^{′} & =\\frac{2y-xz}{{x}^{2}}=\\frac{2\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}-\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}}{{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}}=\\frac{(\\frac{5}{4})}{(\\frac{9}{4})}=\\frac{5}{9} \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=\\frac{5}{9}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{5}{18} \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=\\frac{5}{2}+\\frac{5}{4}=\\frac{15}{4} \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=\\frac{5}{2}+\\frac{5}{18}=\\frac{25}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $3$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ | $\\frac{5}{4}$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ | $\\frac{5}{9}$ | $\\frac{5}{18}$ |\n| $2$ | $\\frac{15}{4}$ |  |  | $\\frac{25}{9}$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(2)≈\\frac{15}{4}$\nand\n${y}^{′}(2)=z(2)≈\\frac{25}{9}.$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308528 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307704 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308528 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308213 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308528 1069 "q-333880" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308528 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308528 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}-y=0,\\,y(1)=-1,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=2\n$$\n\nUse Euler's Method with two steps to approximate $y(2)$ and ${y}^{′}(2).$\n\n$$\n-\\frac{5}{4}\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308528 1072 #uuid "8b1c05ae-5800-59fe-b52e-f6754d3810af" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308528 1074 "For a second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=f(x,y,{y}^{′}),\\,y(a)=c,\\,{y}^{′}(a)=d,\n$$\n\nwe can use Euler's method to approximate a solution by first reducing the second-order ODE to a system of first-order ODEs,\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′}=f(x,y,z)\\, & z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nbefore applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nTo apply Euler’s method, we first isolate ${y}^{″}$ so it can serve as the derivative of a new variable $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}-y & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =y-4{y}^{′} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =\\frac{y-4{y}^{′}}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variable $z={y}^{′},{z}^{′}={y}^{″}$ in the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}=\\frac{y-4z}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nNote that the initial value of $z$ is $z(1)={y}^{′}(1)=2.$\nSo, the initial value problem reduces to the following system of first-order ODEs:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(1)=-1, \\\\ {z}^{′}=\\frac{y-4z}{{x}^{2}},\\, & z(1)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed with Euler's method by applying the update rule component-wise.\n\nFirst, note that we are given the values of $y$ and $z={y}^{′}$ at $x=1$ and asked to find their values at $x=2.$ The distance from $x=1$ to $x=2$ is $1,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{2-1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(1)=-1$ and $z(1)=2.$\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ |  |  | $2$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nWe compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy,$ ${z}^{′}$ and $Δz$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the first row), and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}},$ ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=2 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =\\frac{y-4z}{{x}^{2}}=\\frac{-1-4\\cdot 2}{{1}^{2}}=-9 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=1 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=-9\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{9}{2} \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-1+1=0 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=2-\\frac{9}{2}=-\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $2$ | $-9$ | $-\\frac{9}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $0$ |  |  | $-\\frac{5}{2}$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nWe compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy,$ ${z}^{′}$ and $Δz$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the second row), and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}},$ ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=-\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ {z}^{′} & =\\frac{y-4z}{{x}^{2}}=\\frac{0-4(-\\frac{5}{2})}{{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}}=\\frac{10}{(\\frac{9}{4})}=\\frac{40}{9} \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=-\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=\\frac{40}{9}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{20}{9} \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=0-\\frac{5}{4}=-\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=-\\frac{5}{2}+\\frac{20}{9}=-\\frac{5}{18}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $2$ | $-9$ | $-\\frac{9}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $0$ | $-\\frac{5}{2}$ | $-\\frac{5}{4}$ | $-\\frac{5}{2}$ | $\\frac{40}{9}$ | $\\frac{20}{9}$ |\n| $2$ | $-\\frac{5}{4}$ |  |  | $-\\frac{5}{18}$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(2)≈-\\frac{5}{4}$\nand\n${y}^{′}(2)=z(2)≈-\\frac{5}{18}.$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308529 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308117 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308529 1069 "q-333882" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308529 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308529 1071 "If $G(x)={\\int }_{x}^{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)}(1+{t}^{2})^{2}\\,\\text{d}t,$ then ${G}^{′}(0)=${{field-1}}." 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308529 1072 #uuid "d1246374-7d17-597e-bd92-6ebe36c38c74" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308529 1074 "Here, both the lower and upper limits contain a variable. However, to apply FTC II, we need the variable to be on the upper limit only. So, we can split the integral into two integrals and rewrite them so that in both cases the variable is on the upper limit. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{x}^{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)}(1+{t}^{2})^{2}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{x}^{a}(1+{t}^{2})^{2}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)}(1+{t}^{2})^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{a}^{x}(1+{t}^{2})^{2}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)}(1+{t}^{2})^{2}\\,\\text{d}t,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is any constant number between $x$ and $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x).$\n\nThe integrand $(1+{t}^{2})^{2}$ is continuous for all $t.$ Therefore, applying the FTC II, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{G}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-{\\int }_{a}^{x}(1+{t}^{2})^{2}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)}(1+{t}^{2})^{2}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\int }_{a}^{x}(1+{t}^{2})^{2}\\,\\text{d}t)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\int }_{a}^{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)}(1+{t}^{2})^{2}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\int }_{a}^{x}(1+{t}^{2})^{2}\\,\\text{d}t)+\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}({\\int }_{a}^{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)}(1+{t}^{2})^{2}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-(1+{x}^{2})^{2}+\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot {(1+{u}^{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =-(1+{x}^{2})^{2}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x))\\cdot {(1+{tan}^{2}⁡(x))}^{2} \\\\  & =-(1+{x}^{2})^{2}+{sec}^{2}⁡(x)\\cdot {(1+{tan}^{2}⁡(x))}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the substitution $u=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x).$\n\nFinally, we can compute ${G}^{′}(0).$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{G}^{′}(0) & =-(1+{0}^{2})^{2}+{sec}^{2}⁡(0)\\cdot {(1+{tan}^{2}⁡(0))}^{2} \\\\  & =-1+1\\cdot {1}^{2} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308530 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308141 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308530 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308235 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308530 1069 "q-333886" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308530 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308530 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem for the functions $y=y(x),z=z(x):$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=\\frac{x+2y-z}{(x-z)^{2}},\\, & y(0)=4 \\\\ {z}^{′}=xy+2z,\\, & z(0)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse Euler's Method with one step to approximate $y(2)$ and $z(2).$\n\n$\\,y(2)≈$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,z(2)≈$\n{{field-2}}" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308530 1072 #uuid "e6ff3494-3572-5c80-b1e1-872d45383b2d" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308530 1074 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=f(x,y,z),\\, & y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′}=g(x,y,z),\\, & z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEuler's method approximates the solution by applying the update rule component-wise to each equation. More precisely, the increments on $y$ and $z$ are\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}Δy={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx, \\\\ Δz={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe new values are then given by ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}=z+Δz.$\n\nSince we want to find the value of $y$ and $z$ at $x=2,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=2-0=2.\n$$\n\nFirst, we place the initial condition data in a table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $4$ |  |  | $2$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute both ${y}^{′}$ and ${z}^{′}$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{x+2y-z}{(x-z)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0+2\\cdot 4-2}{(0-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0+8-2}{(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {z}^{′} & =xy+2z \\\\  & =0\\cdot 4+2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =0+4 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $4$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ |  | $2$ | $4$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δy$ and $Δz$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =3 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =4\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $4$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $3$ | $2$ | $4$ | $8$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x,$ $Δy$ to $y,$ and $Δz$ to $z,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+2=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=4+3=7 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=2+8=10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $4$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $3$ | $2$ | $4$ | $8$ |\n| $2$ | $7$ |  |  | $10$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(2)≈7$\nand\n$z(2)≈10.$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308531 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307554 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308531 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308234 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308531 1069 "q-333890" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308531 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308531 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem for the functions $y=y(x),z=z(x):$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=3x-y+2z,\\, & y(1)=3 \\\\ {z}^{′}=2x+y+z,\\, & z(1)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse Euler's Method with one step to approximate $y(3)$ and $z(3).$\n\n$\\,y(3)≈$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,z(3)≈$\n{{field-2}}" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308531 1072 #uuid "ce26fc8f-23bd-5956-a946-36abed73c6df" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308531 1074 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=f(x,y,z),\\, & y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′}=g(x,y,z),\\, & z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEuler's method approximates the solution by applying the update rule component-wise to each equation. More precisely, the increments on $y$ and $z$ are\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}Δy={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx, \\\\ Δz={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe new values are then given by ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}=z+Δz.$\n\nSince we want to find the value of $y$ and $z$ at $x=3,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=3-1=2.\n$$\n\nFirst, we place the initial condition data in a table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $3$ |  |  | $1$ |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute both ${y}^{′}$ and ${z}^{′}$ according to the given rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =3x-y+2z \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1-3+2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =3-3+2 \\\\  & =2 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =2x+y+z \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1+3+1 \\\\  & =2+3+1 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $3$ | $2$ |  | $1$ | $6$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δy$ and $Δz$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =4 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =6\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $3$ | $2$ | $4$ | $1$ | $6$ | $12$ |\n\nTo get the new values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the *next* row, we add $Δx$ to $x,$ $Δy$ to $y,$ and $Δz$ to $z,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+2=3 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=3+4=7 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=1+12=13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these values to our table in a new row gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $3$ | $2$ | $4$ | $1$ | $6$ | $12$ |\n| $3$ | $7$ |  |  | $13$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(3)≈7$\nand\n$z(3)≈13.$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308532 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307555 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308532 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307634 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308532 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308052 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308532 1069 "q-333902" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308532 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308532 1071 "The function ${y}_{0}(x)=2$ is a particular solution of the Riccati differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=4x\\,{y}^{2}-(16x+3)y+(16x+6).\n$$\n\nExpress this equation in the Bernoulli form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x){z}^{n}\n$$\n\nand state the functions $P(x)$ and $Q(x),$ and the value of $n.$\n\n$P(x)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$,\\,Q(x)=$\n{{field-2}}\n$,\\,n=$\n{{field-3}}" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308532 1072 #uuid "e2af2d04-abf4-59b9-9658-a3824e1eb593" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308532 1074 "A Riccati differential equation has the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=a(x){y}^{2}+b(x)y+c(x).\n$$\n\nTo reduce the given Riccati equation to a Bernoulli equation, we use the substitution\n\n$$\ny(x)=z(x)+{y}_{0}(x),\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{0}(x)$ is a known particular solution.\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)={z}^{′}(x)+{y}_{0}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nIn our case, ${y}_{0}(x)=2.$ Since ${y}_{0}^{′}(x)=0$, we have\n\n$$\ny=z+2\\,⇒\\,{y}^{′}={z}^{′}.\n$$\n\nLet's substitute these expressions into our Riccati equation:\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}=4x(z+2)^{2}-(16x+3)(z+2)+(16x+6).\n$$\n\nNow, we expand the right-hand side, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{′} & =4x(z+2)^{2}-(16x+3)(z+2)+(16x+6) \\\\ {z}^{′} & =4x{z}^{2}-3z \\\\ {z}^{′}+3z & =4x{z}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a Bernoulli equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x){z}^{n}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(x)=3,\\,Q(x)=4x,\\,n=2.\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308533 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307961 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308533 1069 "q-333903" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308533 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308533 1071 "Given that ${y}_{0}(x)={e}^{x}$ is a particular solution of the Riccati differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={e}^{x}{y}^{2}-2{e}^{2x}y+{e}^{3x}+{e}^{x}\n$$\n\n \n\nfind the general solution of the equation." 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308533 1072 #uuid "f9ee821d-73da-51fb-959a-a38890a16abc" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308533 1074 "The equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={e}^{x}{y}^{2}-2{e}^{2x}y+{e}^{3x}+{e}^{x},\n$$\n\nis a Riccati differential equation of the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=a(x){y}^{2}+b(x)y+c(x),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\na(x)={e}^{x},\\,b(x)=-2{e}^{2x},\\,c(x)={e}^{3x}+{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nTo reduce the given Riccati equation to a Bernoulli equation, we use the substitution\n\n$$\ny(x)=z(x)+{y}_{0}(x),\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{0}(x)$ is a known particular solution.\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)={z}^{′}(x)+{y}_{0}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nIn our case, ${y}_{0}(x)={e}^{x}.$ Since ${y}_{0}^{′}(x)={e}^{x}$, we have\n\n$$\ny=z+{e}^{x}\\,⇒\\,{y}^{′}={z}^{′}+{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nLet's substitute these expressions into our Riccati equation:\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}+{e}^{x}={e}^{x}(z+{e}^{x})^{2}-2{e}^{2x}(z+{e}^{x})+{e}^{3x}+{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nNow, we expand the right-hand side, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{′}+{e}^{x} & ={e}^{x}({z}^{2}+2{e}^{x}z+{e}^{2x})-2{e}^{2x}(z+{e}^{x})+{e}^{3x}+{e}^{x} \\\\ {z}^{′}+{e}^{x} & ={e}^{x}{z}^{2}+{e}^{x} \\\\ {z}^{′} & ={e}^{x}{z}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve the obtained equation using separation of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & ={e}^{x}{z}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & ={e}^{x} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int {z}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ -\\frac{1}{z} & ={e}^{x}+C \\\\ z & =-\\frac{1}{{e}^{x}+C}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nFinally, let's substitute $z=y-{e}^{x}$ to recover the initial variable $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =-\\frac{1}{{e}^{x}+C} \\\\ y-{e}^{x} & =-\\frac{1}{{e}^{x}+C} \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{{e}^{x}+C}+{e}^{x} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}-\\frac{1}{{e}^{x}+C}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308534 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307919 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308534 1069 "q-333908" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308534 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308534 1071 "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 6 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above.\nFind the unknown function $f(t)$ in the general solution to the system, shown below.\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}f(t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n*Hint: The matrix has the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=3\\text{i}$ with the corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$*" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308534 1072 #uuid "ca67e8b6-0234-571f-a67b-fda3fdb0ac5e" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308534 1074 "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 6 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe are given the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=3\\text{i}$ and the corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{1}$ is \n${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}.$ But we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=3\\text{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3\\text{i}t}.\n$$\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}1-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3\\text{i}t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)-\\text{i}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))+\\text{i}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}]+\\text{i}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+\\text{i}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${w}_{1}$ and ${w}_{2}$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $f(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308535 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307637 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308535 1069 "q-333909" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308535 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308535 1071 "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -5 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above.\nFind the unknown function $f(t)$ in the general solution to the system, shown below.\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}f(t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n*Hint: The matrix has the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=\\text{i}$ with the corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2+\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$*" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308535 1072 #uuid "5689a8d6-4ee4-559f-9467-3ae542252695" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308535 1074 "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -5 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe are given the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=\\text{i}$ and the corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2+\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{1}$ is \n${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}.$ But we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=\\text{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2+\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\text{i}t}.\n$$\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}2+\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\text{i}t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2+\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\text{i}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+\\text{i}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]+\\text{i}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+\\text{i}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${w}_{1}$ and ${w}_{2}$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $f(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308536 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307977 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308536 1069 "q-333915" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308536 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308536 1071 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2x(t)+4y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=x(t)+2y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=4,$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ${v}_{1}=[-2,\\text{ }1]^{T}$ and ${v}_{2}=[2,\\text{ }1]^{T},$ respectively. Which of the following could represent the phase portrait of the system?\n\n*Hint: Here, the dotted line represents the equilibrium solutions.*" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308536 1072 #uuid "c4d342dc-2696-51d2-8f06-b24bb043c8fd" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308536 1074 "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nLet's now sketch the phase portrait.\n\n- First, notice that ${x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ are the equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=-\\frac{1}{2}x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/41/41fff6a9cacaf140a6f86b498ba9415aef0a4699fcb119146ee49e0c98843eeb.png)\n\n- Now, the line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n$y=\\frac{1}{2}x$\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line. Since ${e}^{4t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n![](images/34/3499c19ed90a1cbd1916878fe2b9b26401c4eedd656cb9026fbdf35e5b78acb4.png)\n\nAll other non-equilibrium solutions are parallel to the vector ${v}_{2}.$ So, the phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/12/126db28b161fa823ae5048f79f0ed4516cf3b24053f83cbcd9bc564ae11cd3ef.png)" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308537 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307621 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308537 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307772 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308537 1069 "q-333916" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308537 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308537 1071 "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 18 \\\\ -8 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above.\nFind the unknown functions $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ in the general solution to the system, shown below.\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}f(t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12t)\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}g(t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n*Hint: One of the eigenvalues of the matrix is ${\\lambda }_{1}=12\\text{i}.$*\n\n$f(t)=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,g(t)=$\n{{field-2}}" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308537 1072 #uuid "23c54f10-fdd2-5cf5-a512-18e39d6dc69a" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308537 1074 "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 18 \\\\ -8 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is ${\\lambda }_{1}=12\\text{i}.$ \n\nLet's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors. For ${\\lambda }_{1}=12\\text{i},$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=[\\begin{aligned}-12\\text{i} & 18 \\\\ -8 & -12\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3\\text{i} \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-3\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{1}$ is \n${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}.$ But we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=12\\text{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-3\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\text{12i}t}.\n$$\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}-3\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\text{12i}t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12t)) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3\\text{i}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12t)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12t)+2\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12t)-3\\text{i}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12t)+2\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12t)\\end{aligned}]+\\text{i}[\\begin{aligned}-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+\\text{i}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${w}_{1}$ and ${w}_{2}$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12t)\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12t)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\nf(t)=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12t),\\,g(t)=-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12t).\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308538 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307525 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308538 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307812 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308538 1069 "q-333918" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308538 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308538 1071 "Find ${e}^{At}$ for $A=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -6 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}].$ \n\n*Hint: The eigenvalues of $A$ are $-4$ and $0.$*\n\n| ${e}^{At}=\\,\\,\\frac{1}{2}$ |  | {{field-1}} | $-3+3{e}^{-4t}$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} | $3-{e}^{-4t}$ |  |  |" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308538 1072 #uuid "257aed5d-b769-55be-9201-2e8d67dfb051" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308538 1074 "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -6 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}]t+{\\alpha }_{0}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-6{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & -6{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ 2{\\alpha }_{1}t & 2{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues of $At$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=-4t,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=0,\n$$\n\nboth of multiplicity one. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}r(-4t)={e}^{-4t} \\\\ r(0)=1\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}-4t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{-4t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{0}=1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n- From the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{0}=1.\n$$\n\n- Substituting ${\\alpha }_{0}=1$ into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n-4t{\\alpha }_{1}+1={e}^{-4t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{4t}({e}^{-4t}-1).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =-6(-\\frac{1}{4t}({e}^{-4t}-1))t+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{4}({e}^{-4t}-1)+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}({e}^{-4t}-1)+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{-3+3{e}^{-4t}+2}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1+3{e}^{-4t}}{2} \\\\ 2{\\alpha }_{1}t & =2(-\\frac{1}{4t}({e}^{-4t}-1))t \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{4}({e}^{-4t}-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{-4t}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1-{e}^{-4t}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{e}^{At}=\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1+3{e}^{-4t} & -3+3{e}^{-4t} \\\\ 1-{e}^{-4t} & 3-{e}^{-4t}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308539 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307572 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308539 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307585 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308539 1069 "q-333921" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308539 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308539 1071 "Find ${e}^{At}$ for $A=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -6 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}].$ \n\n*Hint: The eigenvalues of $A$ are $-2$ and $1.$*\n\n| ${e}^{At}=\\,\\,$ |  | $-{e}^{t}+2{e}^{-2t}$ | $-2{e}^{t}+2{e}^{-2t}$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |  |" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308539 1072 #uuid "d259e94f-fd62-514f-881a-1710eb3fba54" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308539 1074 "Explanation:\n\nWe have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -6 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]t+{\\alpha }_{0}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & -6{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ 3{\\alpha }_{1}t & 4{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues of $At$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=-2t,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=t,\n$$\n\nboth of multiplicity one. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}r(-2t)={e}^{-2t} \\\\ r(t)={e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}-2t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{-2t} \\\\ t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n- Subtracting the second equation from the first one, we get\n\n$$\n-3t{\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{-2t}-{e}^{t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{3t}({e}^{-2t}-{e}^{t}).\n$$\n\n- Multiplying the second equation by $2$ and adding to the first one, we get\n\n$$\n3{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{-2t}+2{e}^{t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{0}=\\frac{1}{3}({e}^{-2t}+2{e}^{t}).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{\\alpha }_{1}t & =3(-\\frac{1}{3t}({e}^{-2t}-{e}^{t}))t \\\\  & =-({e}^{-2t}-{e}^{t}) \\\\  & ={e}^{t}-{e}^{-2t} \\\\ 4{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =4(-\\frac{1}{3t}({e}^{-2t}-{e}^{t}))t+\\frac{1}{3}({e}^{-2t}+2{e}^{t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}((-4{e}^{-2t}+4{e}^{t})+({e}^{-2t}+2{e}^{t})) \\\\  & =2{e}^{t}-{e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{t}+2{e}^{-2t} & -2{e}^{t}+2{e}^{-2t} \\\\ {e}^{t}-{e}^{-2t} & 2{e}^{t}-{e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308540 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307674 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308540 1069 "q-333930" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308540 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308540 1071 "For the initial value problem ${y}^{′}=f(x,y),y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},$ the modified Euler method can be expressed as the update formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{n}^{(p)} & =f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})\\cdot Δx, \\\\ {y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})+f({x}_{n+1},{y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}^{(p)}))\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true when testing the stability of the modified Euler method on the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),y(0)=1,x\\ge 0,\\lambda =4,$ and step size $Δx?$  \n\n- The stability function is $R(4Δx)=1-4Δx+8(Δx)^{2}.$\n- The stability region is $|8{(Δx-\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}|<1.$\n- The method is absolutely stable.\n- The method is stable for step size $Δx=0.5.$\n\n*Hint:* The stability function $R$ is given by ${y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n}.$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308540 1072 #uuid "9d872692-c2e4-5ebe-a433-d44857eb60bd" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308540 1074 "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda Δx)$ is the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda Δx)|<1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nA method that is stable for all possible values $\\lambda Δx>0$ is called absolutely stable.\n\nNow, let's check the stability of the modified Euler Method by applying it to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-4y,\\,y(0)=1.\n$$\n\nWe now examine our statements:\n\n- Applying the modified Euler method to this test equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}^{(p)} & ={y}_{n}+f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}-4{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})+f({x}_{n+1},{y}_{n}-4{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx))\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(-4{y}_{n}-4({y}_{n}-4{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx))\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(-8{y}_{n}+16{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx)\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}-4{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx+8{y}_{n}\\cdot (Δx)^{2} \\\\  & =[1-4Δx+8(Δx)^{2}]{y}_{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the stability function is $R(4Δx)=1-4Δx+8(Δx)^{2}.$ Hence, statement I is true.\n\n- The method is stable when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(4Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |1-4Δx+8(Δx)^{2}| & <1 \\\\ |8{(Δx-\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}| & <1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${y}_{n}$ converges to $0$ as $n\\to \\infty $ if and only if\n\n$$\n|8{(Δx-\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}|<1.\n$$\n\nHence, statement II is false.\n\n- Now, since the argument of the absolute value is always non-negative, we can remove the absolute value. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8{(Δx-\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2} & <1 \\\\ 8{(Δx-\\frac{1}{4})}^{2} & <\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {(Δx-\\frac{1}{4})}^{2} & <\\frac{1}{16} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{4}<Δx-\\frac{1}{4} & <\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 0<Δx & <\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the modified Euler method is not absolutely stable, and statement III is false.\n\n- Finally, since it does not satisfy this inequality, the modified Euler method is unstable for step size $Δx=0.5,$ and hence statement IV is false.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I only\"." 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308541 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307774 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308541 1069 "q-333952" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308541 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308541 1071 "If $G(x)={\\int }_{x}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t,$ then ${G}^{′}(0)=${{field-1}}." 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308541 1072 #uuid "12d6561d-5f82-5eff-8354-a16f5ebb4e3a" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308541 1074 "Here, both the lower and upper limits contain a variable. However, to apply FTC II, we need the variable to be on the upper limit only. So, we can split the integral into two integrals and rewrite them so that in both cases the variable is on the upper limit. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{x}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{x}^{a}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{a}^{x}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is any constant number between $x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x).$\n\nThe integrand ${e}^{t}$ is continuous for all $t.$ Therefore, applying the FTC II, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{G}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-{\\int }_{a}^{x}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{a}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\int }_{a}^{x}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\int }_{a}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\int }_{a}^{x}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t)+\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}({\\int }_{a}^{u}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-{e}^{x}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x))\\cdot {e}^{u} \\\\  & =-{e}^{x}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x))\\cdot {e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)} \\\\  & =-{e}^{x}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\cdot {e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the substitution $u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x).$\n\nFinally, we can compute ${G}^{′}(0).$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{G}^{′}(0) & =-{e}^{0}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)\\cdot {e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)} \\\\  & =-1-0 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308542 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307808 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308542 1069 "q-333968" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308542 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308542 1071 "For the initial value problem ${y}^{′}=f(x,y),y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},$ the modified Euler method can be expressed as the update formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{n}^{(p)} & =f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})\\cdot Δx, \\\\ {y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})+f({x}_{n+1},{y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}^{(p)}))\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true when testing the stability of the modified Euler method on the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),y(0)=1,x\\ge 0,\\lambda =2,$ and step size $Δx?$  \n\n- The stability function is $R(2Δx)=1-2Δx+2(Δx)^{2}.$\n- The stability region is $|2{(Δx-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}|<1.$\n- The method is absolutely stable.\n- The method is stable for step size $Δx=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n*Hint:* The stability function $R$ is given by ${y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n}.$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308542 1072 #uuid "632bafc0-a6e4-5e65-b220-22e1ed511764" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308542 1074 "A numerical method  ${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$ is stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=\\frac{1}{2},\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda Δx)$ is the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda Δx)|<1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nA method that is stable for all possible values $\\lambda Δx>0$ is called absolutely stable.\n\nNow, let’s check the stability of the modified Euler Method by applying it to the test equation  \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-2y,\\,y(0)=1.\n$$\n\nWe now examine our statements:\n\n- Applying the modified Euler method to this test equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}^{(p)} & ={y}_{n}+f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}-2{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})+f({x}_{n+1},{y}_{n}-2{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx))\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(-2{y}_{n}-2({y}_{n}-2{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx))\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}+\\frac{1}{2}(-4{y}_{n}+4{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx)\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}-2{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx+2{y}_{n}\\cdot (Δx)^{2} \\\\  & =[1-2Δx+2(Δx)^{2}]{y}_{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the stability function is $R(2Δx)=1-2Δx+2(Δx)^{2}.$ Hence, statement I is true.\n\n- The method is stable when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(2Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |1-2Δx+2(Δx)^{2}| & <1 \\\\ |2{(Δx-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}| & <1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${y}_{n}$ converges to $0$ as $n\\to \\infty $ if and only if\n\n$$\n|2{(Δx-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}|<1.\n$$\n\nHence, statement II is true.\n\n- Now, since the argument of the absolute value is always non-negative, we can remove the absolute value. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{(Δx-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2} & <1 \\\\ 2{(Δx-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & <\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {(Δx-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & <\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}<Δx-\\frac{1}{2} & <\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0<Δx & <1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the modified Euler method is not absolutely stable, and statement III is false.\n\n- Finally, since it satisfies this inequality, the modified Euler method is stable for step size $Δx=\\frac{1}{2},$ and hence statement IV is true.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I, II, and IV only\"." 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308543 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307645 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308543 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308314 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308543 1069 "q-333971" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308543 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308543 1071 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-2x(t),\\end{aligned}\\,[\\begin{aligned}x(0) \\\\ y(0)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}].$*\n\n| $[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  |" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308543 1072 #uuid "b59372b5-46f1-5738-9ccc-9025ae0786e0" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308543 1074 "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,x(0),\n$$\n\nwhere ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$ is the standard fundamental matrix of the system, and $x(0)$ is the initial condition vector.\n\nNow, using the given information, the standard fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{At}\\,x(0) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308544 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308080 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308544 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308313 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308544 1069 "q-333997" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308544 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308544 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n2x{y}^{″}+5({y}^{′})^{2}+x=0,\\,y(1)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=-1\n$$\n\nUse Euler's Method with two steps to approximate $y(3)$ and ${y}^{′}(3).$\n\n$$\ny(3)≈{{field-1}},\\,{y}^{′}(3)≈{{field-2}}\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308544 1072 #uuid "fb1abd48-adcc-5047-a986-a35aa0061fc5" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308544 1074 "For a second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=f(x,y,{y}^{′}),\\,y(a)=c,\\,{y}^{′}(a)=d,\n$$\n\nwe can use Euler's method to approximate a solution by first reducing the second-order ODE to a system of first-order ODEs,\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′}=f(x,y,z)\\, & z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nbefore applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nTo apply Euler’s method, we first isolate ${y}^{″}$ so it can serve as the derivative of a new variable $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x{y}^{″}+5({y}^{′})^{2}+x & =0 \\\\ 2x{y}^{″} & =-5({y}^{′})^{2}-x \\\\ {y}^{″} & =-\\frac{5({y}^{′})^{2}+x}{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variable $z={y}^{′},{z}^{′}={y}^{″}$ in the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}=-\\frac{5{z}^{2}+x}{2x}.\n$$\n\nNote that the initial value of $z$ is $z(1)={y}^{′}(1)=-1.$\nSo, the initial value problem reduces to the following system of first-order ODEs:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(1)=2, \\\\ {z}^{′}=-\\frac{5{z}^{2}+x}{2x},\\, & z(1)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed with Euler's method by applying the update rule component-wise.\n\nFirst, note that we are given the values of $y$ and $z={y}^{′}$ at $x=1$ and asked to find their values at $x=3.$ The distance from $x=1$ to $x=3$ is $2,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{3-1}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, we'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(1)=2$ and $z(1)=-1.$\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ |  |  | $-1$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nWe compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy,$ ${z}^{′}$ and $Δz$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the first row), and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}},$ ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=-1 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =-\\frac{5{z}^{2}+x}{2x}=-\\frac{5\\cdot (-1)^{2}+1}{2\\cdot 1}=-3 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=-1\\cdot 1=-1 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=-3\\cdot 1=-3 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=2-1=1 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=-1-3=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ | $-3$ | $-3$ |\n| $2$ | $1$ |  |  | $-4$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nWe compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy,$ ${z}^{′}$ and $Δz$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the second row), and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}},$ ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=-4 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =-\\frac{5{z}^{2}+x}{2x}=-\\frac{5\\cdot (-4)^{2}+2}{2\\cdot 2}=-\\frac{41}{2} \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=-4\\cdot 1=-4 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=-\\frac{41}{2}\\cdot 1=-\\frac{41}{2} \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=2+1=3 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1-4=-3 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=-4-\\frac{41}{2}=-\\frac{49}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $-1$ | $-1$ | $-1$ | $-3$ | $-3$ |\n| $2$ | $1$ | $-4$ | $-4$ | $-4$ | $-\\frac{41}{2}$ | $-\\frac{41}{2}$ |\n| $3$ | $-3$ |  |  | $-\\frac{49}{2}$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(3)≈-3$\nand\n${y}^{′}(3)=z(3)≈-\\frac{49}{2}.$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308545 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307856 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308545 1069 "q-334000" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308545 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308545 1071 "Which of the following equations is a Clairaut differential equation?\n\n- $y={x}^{3}{y}^{′}+x{y}^{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)$\n- $x{y}^{4}-xy{y}^{′}-{y}^{2}=0$\n- $y=x{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{{y}^{′}}$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308545 1072 #uuid "077e1927-c6a2-59ff-bf00-a9d36509bb47" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308545 1074 "A Clairaut differential equation has the form\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $g$ is a differentiable function of ${y}^{′}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is *not* a Clairaut differential equation. In fact, it cannot be written in the form $y=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′}).$\n\n- Equation II is *not* a Clairaut differential equation. Dividing the equation by $y,$ we get\n\n$$\nx{y}^{3}-x{y}^{′}-y=0.\n$$\n\nAgain, this equation cannot be written in the form $y=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′}).$\n\n- Equation III is a Clairaut differential equation of the form $y=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′})$ with\n\n$$\ng({y}^{′})=\\frac{1}{{y}^{′}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308546 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307631 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308546 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308331 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308546 1069 "q-334003" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308546 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308546 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny{y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}-x=0,\\,y(-1)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(-1)=-2\n$$\n\nUse Euler's Method with two steps to approximate $y(1)$ and ${y}^{′}(1).$\n\n$$\ny(1)≈{{field-1}},\\,{y}^{′}(1)≈{{field-2}}\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308546 1072 #uuid "11300967-9935-5878-8815-775bcb4f9714" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308546 1074 "For a second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=f(x,y,{y}^{′}),\\,y(a)=c,\\,{y}^{′}(a)=d,\n$$\n\nwe can use Euler's method to approximate a solution by first reducing the second-order ODE to a system of first-order ODEs,\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′}=f(x,y,z)\\, & z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nbefore applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nTo apply Euler’s method, we first isolate ${y}^{″}$ so it can serve as the derivative of a new variable $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y{y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}-x & =0 \\\\ y{y}^{″} & =x-3{y}^{′} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =\\frac{x-3{y}^{′}}{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variable $z={y}^{′},{z}^{′}={y}^{″}$ in the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}=\\frac{x-3z}{y}.\n$$\n\nNote that the initial value of $z$ is $z(-1)={y}^{′}(-1)=-2.$\nSo, the initial value problem reduces to the following system of first-order ODEs:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=z,\\, & y(-1)=1, \\\\ {z}^{′}=\\frac{x-3z}{y},\\, & z(-1)=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed with Euler's method by applying the update rule component-wise.\n\nFirst, note that we are given the values of $y$ and $z={y}^{′}$ at $x=-1$ and asked to find their values at $x=1.$ The distance from $x=-1$ to $x=1$ is $2,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{1-(-1)}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, we'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(-1)=1$ and $z(-1)=-2.$\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ |  |  | $-2$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nWe compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy,$ ${z}^{′}$ and $Δz$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the first row), and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}},$ ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=-2 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =\\frac{x-3z}{y}=\\frac{-1-3(-2)}{1}=5 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=-2\\cdot 1=-2 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=5\\cdot 1=5 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=-1+1=0 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1-2=-1 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=-2+5=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-2$ | $-2$ | $5$ | $5$ |\n| $0$ | $-1$ |  |  | $3$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nWe compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy,$ ${z}^{′}$ and $Δz$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the second row), and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}},$ ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=3 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =\\frac{x-3z}{y}=\\frac{0-3\\cdot 3}{(-1)}=9 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=3\\cdot 1=3 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=9\\cdot 1=9 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+1=1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-1+3=2 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=3+9=12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-2$ | $-2$ | $5$ | $5$ |\n| $0$ | $-1$ | $3$ | $3$ | $3$ | $9$ | $9$ |\n| $1$ | $2$ |  |  | $12$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(1)≈2$\nand\n${y}^{′}(1)=z(1)≈12.$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308547 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307811 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308547 1069 "q-334008" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308547 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308547 1071 "For which of the following step sizes is Euler's Method **stable**, given that $\\lambda $ is the parameter of the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),y(0)=1,x\\ge 0,\\lambda >0,$ and step size $Δx?$\n\n- $Δx=\\frac{0.25}{\\lambda }$\n- $Δx=\\frac{3.5}{\\lambda }$\n- $Δx=\\frac{2.2}{\\lambda }$\n- $Δx=\\frac{1.5}{\\lambda }$\n\n*Hint: The stability function for Euler's method is given by* $R(\\lambda Δx)=1-\\lambda Δx.$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308547 1072 #uuid "3f7955cf-0d1a-5a59-88f5-cf30308c87f2" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308547 1074 "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nFor stability, we require that the following condition holds:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(\\lambda Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |1-\\lambda Δx| & <1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this gives\n\n$$\n0<\\lambda Δx<2.\n$$\n\nThis means that Euler's method is stable when applied to the test equation for values in the region $0<\\lambda Δx<2.$\n\nNow, we consider each step size in turn.\n\n- For $Δx=\\frac{0.25}{\\lambda },$ we have $\\lambda Δx=0.25.$ Since this value falls *within* the given region,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0<\\lambda Δx & <2 \\\\ 0<0.25 & <2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEuler's method *must be* stable for this step size.\n\n- For $Δx=\\frac{3.5}{\\lambda },$ we have $\\lambda Δx=3.5\\ge 2.$ Since this value falls *outside* the given region, Euler's method is *not* stable for this step size.\n\n- For $Δx=\\frac{2.2}{\\lambda },$ we have $\\lambda Δx=2.2\\ge 2.$ Since this value falls *outside* the given region, Euler's method is *not* stable for this step size.\n\n- For $Δx=\\frac{1.5}{\\lambda },$ we have $\\lambda Δx=1.5.$ Since this value falls *within* the given region,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0<\\lambda Δx & <2 \\\\ 0<1.5 & <2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEuler's method *must be* stable for this step size.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and IV only\"." 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308548 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307619 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308548 1069 "q-334009" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308548 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308548 1071 "For which of the following step sizes is Euler's Method **stable**, given that $\\lambda $ is the parameter of the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),y(0)=1,x\\ge 0,\\lambda >0,$ and step size $Δx?$\n\n- $Δx=\\frac{1.4}{\\lambda }$\n- $Δx=\\frac{1.25}{\\lambda }$\n- $Δx=\\frac{2.5}{\\lambda }$\n- $Δx=\\frac{0.75}{\\lambda }$\n\n*Hint: The stability function for Euler's method is given by* $R(\\lambda Δx)=1-\\lambda Δx.$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308548 1072 #uuid "f21f7e32-ddad-5b51-8839-e9c4ba026875" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308548 1074 "A numerical method  ${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$  is stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nFor stability, we require that the following condition holds:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(\\lambda Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |1-\\lambda Δx| & <1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this gives\n\n$$\n0<\\lambda Δx<2.\n$$\n\nThis means that Euler's method is stable when applied to the test equation for values in the region $0<\\lambda Δx<2.$\n\nNow, we consider each step size in turn.\n\n- For $Δx=\\frac{1.4}{\\lambda },$ we have $\\lambda Δx=1.4.$ Since this value falls *within* the given region,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0<\\lambda Δx & <2 \\\\ 0<1.4 & <2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEuler's method *must be* stable for this step size.\n\n- For $Δx=\\frac{1.25}{\\lambda },$ we have $\\lambda Δx=1.25.$ Since this value falls *within* the given region,\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0<\\lambda Δx & <2 \\\\ 0<1.25 & <2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEuler's method *must be* stable for this step size.\n\n- For $Δx=\\frac{2.5}{\\lambda },$ we have $\\lambda Δx=2.5\\ge 2.$ Since this value falls *outside* the given region, Euler's method is *not* stable for this step size.\n\n- For $Δx=\\frac{0.75}{\\lambda },$ we have $\\lambda Δx=0.75.$ Since this value falls *within* the given region,\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0<\\lambda Δx & <2 \\\\ 0<0.75 & <2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEuler's method *must be* stable for this step size.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and IV only\"." 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308549 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307810 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308549 1069 "q-334014" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308549 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308549 1071 "For which of the following step sizes is Euler's Method **unstable**, given that $\\lambda $ is the parameter of the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),y(0)=1,x\\ge 0,\\lambda >0,$ and step size $Δx?$\n\n- $Δx=\\frac{2.2}{\\lambda }$\n- $Δx=\\frac{0.4}{\\lambda }$\n- $Δx=\\frac{2}{\\lambda }$\n- $Δx=\\frac{5}{\\lambda }$\n\n*Hint: The stability function for Euler's method is given by* $R(\\lambda Δx)=1-\\lambda Δx.$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308549 1072 #uuid "7c9db1fa-3df6-5592-9112-986f10641d50" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308549 1074 "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nFor stability, we require that the following condition holds:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(\\lambda Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |1-\\lambda Δx| & <1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this gives\n\n$$\n0<\\lambda Δx<2.\n$$\n\nThis means that Euler's method is stable when applied to the test equation for values in the region $0<\\lambda Δx<2.$\n\nNow, we consider each step size in turn.\n\n- For $Δx=\\frac{2.2}{\\lambda },$ we have $\\lambda Δx=2.2\\ge 2.$ Since this value falls outside the given region, Euler's method is not stable (or unstable) for this step size.\n\n- For $Δx=\\frac{0.4}{\\lambda },$ we have $\\lambda Δx=0.4.$ Since this value falls within the given region,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & <\\lambda Δx<2 \\\\ 0 & <0.4<2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEuler's method must be stable for this step size.\n\n- For $Δx=\\frac{2}{\\lambda },$ we have $\\lambda Δx=2\\ge 2.$ Since this value falls outside the given region, Euler's method is not stable (or unstable) for this step size.\n\n- For $Δx=\\frac{5}{\\lambda },$ we have $\\lambda Δx=5\\ge 2.$ Since this value falls outside the given region, Euler's method is not stable (or unstable) for this step size.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, III, and IV only\"." 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308550 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307677 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308550 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307849 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308550 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308190 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308550 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308219 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308550 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308282 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308550 1069 "q-334021" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308550 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308550 1071 "Given the set of functions ${\\,7\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\\,5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}$ defined on $x\\in R,$ select the correct options in the following reasoning.For all $x\\in R,$  we have that\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}\\cdot 7\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,+\\,{{field-2}}\\cdot 5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,+\\,{{field-3}}\\cdot (-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)=0.\n$$\n\nSince \n{{field-4}} \nthe scalar coefficients in the linear combination are zero, the given set of functions is linearly \n{{field-5}}." 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308550 1072 #uuid "db530145-a259-5aa0-aa5d-dbfcaff7cc5f" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308550 1074 "Recall that the set of functions ${{f}_{1}(x),{f}_{2}(x),…,{f}_{n}(x)},$ which are defined on some interval $I,$ is *linearly dependent* if there exist real scalars ${k}_{1},{k}_{2},…,{k}_{n}$ not all zero such that the linear combination ${k}_{1}\\cdot {f}_{1}+{k}_{2}\\cdot {f}_{2}+…+{k}_{n}\\cdot {f}_{n}$ is identically zero, i.e, \n\n$$\n{k}_{1}\\cdot {f}_{1}(x)+{k}_{2}\\cdot {f}_{2}(x)+…+{k}_{n}\\cdot {f}_{n}(x)=0\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in I.$ Otherwise, if ${k}_{1}={k}_{2}=⋯={k}_{n}=0$ is the only solution, the set is *linearly independent*.\n\nNow, for all $x\\in R,$ we have that \n\n$$\n0\\cdot 7\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,+\\,4\\cdot 5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,+\\,4\\cdot (-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $\\text{not all}$ the scalar coefficients in the linear combination are zero, the given set of functions is linearly $\\text{dependent}.$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308551 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307703 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308551 1069 "q-334054" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308551 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308551 1071 "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}]x(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n*Hint: The matrix has the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=2\\text{i}$ with the corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$*" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308551 1072 #uuid "ec6ae686-d666-58a4-bc66-27db653b0dac" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308551 1074 "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\nWe are given the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=2\\text{i}$ and the corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{1}$ is \n${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}.$ But we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=2\\text{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2\\text{i}t}.\n$$\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}1-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2\\text{i}t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}](\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)-\\text{i}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]+\\text{i}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+\\text{i}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${w}_{1}$ and ${w}_{2}$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}-{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & =3[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]+3[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308552 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307641 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308552 1069 "q-334059" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308552 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308552 1071 "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n