}_{1}=4+6\\text{i}$ and the corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1+2\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{1}$ is \n${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}.$ But we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=4+6\\text{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1+2\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{(4+6\\text{i})t}.\n$$\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}1+2\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{(4+6\\text{i})t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1+2\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)+\\text{i}{e}^{4t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)+2\\text{i}{e}^{4t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)-2{e}^{4t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t) \\\\ {e}^{4t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)+\\text{i}{e}^{4t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t))+\\text{i}{e}^{4t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)) \\\\ {e}^{4t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)+\\text{i}{e}^{4t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}+\\text{i}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t} \\\\  & ={\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+\\text{i}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${w}_{1}$ and ${w}_{2}$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $f(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t).$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308571 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308103 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308571 1069 "q-334132" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308571 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308571 1071 "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}7 & -10 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above.\nFind the unknown function $f(t)$ in the general solution to the system, shown below.\n\n$$\n\\text{x}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}]{e}^{6t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}f(t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}]{e}^{6t}\n$$\n\n*Hint: The matrix has the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=6+3\\text{i}$ with the corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1+3\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$*" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308571 1072 #uuid "5c4141c9-f4bb-59de-ba8c-565405b181f6" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308571 1074 "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}7 & -10 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe are given the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=6+3\\text{i}$ and the corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1+3\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{1}$ is \n${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}.$ But we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=6+3\\text{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1+3\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{(6+3\\text{i})t}.\n$$\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}1+3\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{(6+3\\text{i})t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1+3\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{6t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{6t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\text{i}{e}^{6t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\text{i}{e}^{6t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-3{e}^{6t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ {e}^{6t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\text{i}{e}^{6t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{6t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))+\\text{i}{e}^{6t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) \\\\ {e}^{6t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\text{i}{e}^{6t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}]{e}^{6t}+\\text{i}[\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}]{e}^{6t} \\\\  & ={\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+\\text{i}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${w}_{1}$ and ${w}_{2}$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}]{e}^{6t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}]{e}^{6t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $f(t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308572 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308137 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308572 1069 "q-334133" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308572 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308572 1071 "Which of the following equations is a Clairaut differential equation?\n\n- $y+2=x{y}^{′}+{y}^{′}$\n- ${y}^{′}-y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x{y}^{′})$\n- $x{y}^{′}+{y}^{′}-y=0$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308572 1072 #uuid "cf963632-b648-5646-b884-a63de120bfff" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308572 1074 "A Clairaut differential equation has the form\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $g$ is a differentiable function of ${y}^{′}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is a Clairaut differential equation. Isolating the variable $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+{y}^{′}-2,\n$$\n\nwhich is a Clairaut differential equation of the form $y=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′})$ with\n\n$$\ng({y}^{′})={y}^{′}-2.\n$$\n\n- Equation II is *not* a Clairaut differential equation. Isolating the variable $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny={y}^{′}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x{y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nThis equation cannot be written in the form $y=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′}).$\n\n- Equation III is a Clairaut differential equation. Isolating the variable $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+{y}^{′},\n$$\n\nwhich is a Clairaut differential equation of the form $y=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′})$ with\n\n$$\ng({y}^{′})={y}^{′}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308573 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307552 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308573 1069 "q-334135" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308573 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308573 1071 "Which of the following equations is a Clairaut differential equation?\n\n- $y=x{y}^{′}+{y}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x{y}^{2})$\n- $y=x{y}^{′}+{2}^{{y}^{′}}$\n- ${y}^{2}=x{y}^{′}+\\frac{2}{x}$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308573 1072 #uuid "feda7358-5560-5f92-8b57-984ba0bdd378" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308573 1074 "A Clairaut differential equation has the form\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $g$ is a differentiable function of ${y}^{′}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is *not* a Clairaut differential equation. In fact, it cannot be written in the form $y=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′}).$\n\n- Equation II is a Clairaut differential equation of the form $y=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′})$ with \n\n$$\ng({y}^{′})={2}^{{y}^{′}}.\n$$\n\n- Equation III is *not* a Clairaut differential equation. It cannot be written in the form $y=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′}).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308574 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307835 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308574 1069 "q-334137" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308574 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308574 1071 "Given that ${y}_{0}(x)=x+7$ is a particular solution of the Riccati differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=8{y}^{2}-16(x+7)y+8(x+7)^{2}+1\n$$\n\nfind the general solution of the equation." 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308574 1072 #uuid "5834adff-aeeb-5b25-89cd-fb7a35edb449" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308574 1074 "The equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=8{y}^{2}-16(x+7)y+8(x+7)^{2}+1,\n$$\n\nis a Riccati differential equation of the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=a(x){y}^{2}+b(x)y+c(x),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\na(x)=8,\\,b(x)=-16(x+7),\\,c(x)=8(x+7)^{2}+1.\n$$\n\nTo reduce the given Riccati equation to a Bernoulli equation, we use the substitution\n\n$$\ny(x)=z(x)+{y}_{0}(x),\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{0}(x)$ is a known particular solution.\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)={z}^{′}(x)+{y}_{0}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nIn our case, ${y}_{0}(x)=x+7.$ Since ${y}_{0}^{′}(x)=1$, we have\n\n$$\ny=z+x+7\\,⇒\\,{y}^{′}={z}^{′}+1.\n$$\n\nLet's substitute these expressions into our Riccati equation:\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}+1=8(z+x+7)^{2}-16(x+7)(z+x+7)+8(x+7)^{2}+1.\n$$\n\nNow, we expand the right-hand side, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{′}+1 & =8((x+7)^{2}+2(x+7)z+{z}^{2})-16(x+7)(z+x+7)+8(x+7)^{2}+1 \\\\ {z}^{′}+1 & =8{z}^{2}+1 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =8{z}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve the obtained equation using separation of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =8{z}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =8 \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 8\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int {z}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int 8\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ -\\frac{1}{z} & =8x+C \\\\ z & =-\\frac{1}{8x+C}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nFinally, let's substitute $z=y-(x+7)$ to recover the initial variable $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =-\\frac{1}{8x+C} \\\\ y-(x+7) & =-\\frac{1}{8x+C} \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{8x+C}+x+7 \\\\  & =x+7-\\frac{1}{8x+C}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308575 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307839 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308575 1069 "q-334144" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308575 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308575 1071 "Given that ${y}_{0}(x)=-6x$ is a particular solution of the Riccati differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=3{y}^{2}+36xy+108{x}^{2}-6\n$$\n\nfind the general solution of the equation." 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308575 1072 #uuid "e9e9479a-d94d-5ddc-ae94-abba6b6f4d0f" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308575 1074 "The equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=3{y}^{2}+36xy+108{x}^{2}-6,\n$$\n\nis a Riccati differential equation of the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=a(x){y}^{2}+b(x)y+c(x),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\na(x)=3,\\,b(x)=36x,\\,c(x)=108{x}^{2}-6.\n$$\n\nTo reduce the given Riccati equation to a Bernoulli equation, we use the substitution\n\n$$\ny(x)=z(x)+{y}_{0}(x),\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{0}(x)$ is a known particular solution.\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)={z}^{′}(x)+{y}_{0}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nIn our case, ${y}_{0}(x)=-6x.$ Since ${y}_{0}^{′}(x)=-6$, we have\n\n$$\ny=z-6x\\,⇒\\,{y}^{′}={z}^{′}-6.\n$$\n\nLet's substitute these expressions into our Riccati equation:\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}-6=3(z-6x)^{2}+36x(z-6x)+108{x}^{2}-6.\n$$\n\nNow, we expand the right-hand side, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{′}-6 & =3(36{x}^{2}-12xz+{z}^{2})+36xz-216{x}^{2}+108{x}^{2}-6 \\\\ {z}^{′}-6 & =3{z}^{2}-6 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =3{z}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve the obtained equation using separation of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =3{z}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =3 \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 3\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int {z}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int 3\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ -\\frac{1}{z} & =3x+C \\\\ z & =-\\frac{1}{3x+C}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nFinally, let's substitute $z=y+6x$ (that is, $y=z-6x$) to recover the initial variable $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =-\\frac{1}{3x+C} \\\\ y+6x & =-\\frac{1}{3x+C} \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{3x+C}-6x \\\\  & =-6x-\\frac{1}{3x+C}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308576 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308273 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308576 1069 "q-334148" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308576 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308576 1071 "Which of the following equations is a Clairaut differential equation?\n\n- $y=x{y}^{′}+({y}^{′}-1)^{4}$\n- $y{y}^{′}-x{y}^{5}-4{x}^{3}{y}^{3}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x){y}^{2}$\n- $x{y}^{′}+\\operatorname{tan}⁡({y}^{′}+2)-y=0$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308576 1072 #uuid "2d6213f1-60d6-5a67-a158-34abc9070a3b" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308576 1074 "A Clairaut differential equation has the form\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $g$ is a differentiable function of ${y}^{′}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- Equation I is a Clairaut differential equation of the form $y=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′})$ with \n\n$$\ng({y}^{′})=({y}^{′}-1)^{4}.\n$$\n\n- Equation II is *not* a Clairaut differential equation. Dividing the equation by $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-x{y}^{4}-4{x}^{3}{y}^{2}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)y.\n$$\n\nThis equation cannot be written in the form $y=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′}).$\n\n- Equation III is a Clairaut differential equation. Isolating the variable $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+\\operatorname{tan}⁡({y}^{′}+2),\n$$\n\nwhich is a Clairaut differential equation of the form $y=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′})$ with\n\n$$\ng({y}^{′})=\\operatorname{tan}⁡({y}^{′}+2).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308577 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308076 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308577 1069 "q-334150" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308577 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308577 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=y,\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nSuppose we wish to approximate the solution using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the exact solution is ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)={e}^{x},$ compute the local truncation error ${τ}_{21}$ for the step starting at $x=1$ with step size $Δx=0.05.$ Round your answer to three decimal places.\n\n${τ}_{21}≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308577 1072 #uuid "a14abf0c-d718-54a5-bcde-4ac2628a535c" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308577 1074 "The local truncation error (LTE) is the error made in a *single step* of a numerical method, when the step begins from an *exact value*. The LTE of a numerical method at step $n$ from $x$ with exact value ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)$ to ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{n} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-({y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)+Δy).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that the exact solution is ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)={e}^{x}.$ So, the $y$-value at the start of the step $x=1$ is ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(1)=e.$\n\nNow, let's find the $y$-increment given by the numerical method.\n\nFirst, the slope at the start of the step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{exact}}^{′}(1) & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(1) \\\\  & =e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the increment at this step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}^{′}(1)\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =e\\cdot 0.05 \\\\  & =0.05e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the exact value at ${x}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}}) & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(x+Δx) \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(1+0.05) \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(1.05) \\\\  & ={e}^{1.05}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the LTE for the step starting at $x=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{21} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(1.05)-({y}_{\\text{exact}}(1)+Δy) \\\\  & ={e}^{1.05}-(e+0.05e) \\\\  & ={e}^{1.05}-1.05e \\\\  & ≈0.003\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308578 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308116 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308578 1069 "q-334151" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308578 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308578 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x-2\\sqrt{y},\\,y(1)=1\n$$\n\nUsing the trapezoidal method with step size $Δx=1,$ the new value ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ is obtained by solving the equation $g({y}_{\\text{new}})=0$ at each step. Which of the following is the **specific function** $g({y}_{\\text{new}})$ used in the first step?" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308578 1072 #uuid "5954fe07-ca71-56cd-b722-9e93582b85a7" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308578 1074 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nIn this example, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(x-2\\sqrt{y}+{x}_{\\text{new}}-2\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}})\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{\\text{new}}-\\sqrt{y}-\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{\\text{new}}-\\sqrt{y}-\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMoving all terms to the left-hand side gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}_{\\text{new}}-y-\\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{\\text{new}}+\\sqrt{y}+\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}}=0.\n$$\n\nSo, the general form of the function $g$ for step size $Δx=1$ is\n\n$$\ng({y}_{\\text{new}})={y}_{\\text{new}}-y-\\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{\\text{new}}+\\sqrt{y}+\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}}.\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is\n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=1+1=2.\n$$\n\n Therefore, the function used in the first step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({y}_{\\text{new}}) & ={y}_{\\text{new}}-1-\\frac{1}{2}(1)-\\frac{1}{2}(2)+1+\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}} \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{new}}-1-\\frac{1}{2}-1+1+\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}} \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{new}}+\\sqrt{{y}_{\\text{new}}}-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308579 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307648 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308579 1069 "q-334168" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308579 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308579 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2x+1,\\,y(0)=0\n$$\n\nSuppose we wish to approximate the solution using the following numerical method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{6}(x+{y}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the exact solution is ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)={x}^{2}+x,$ compute the local truncation error, ${τ}_{6},$ for the step starting at $x=1$ with step size $Δx=0.2.$ Round your answer to three decimal places.\n\n${τ}_{6}≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308579 1072 #uuid "e12084a2-8c6a-5bce-82ea-c65fa37880b5" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308579 1074 "The local truncation error (LTE) is the error made in a *single step* of a numerical method, when the step begins from an *exact value*. The LTE of a numerical method at step $n$ from $x$ with exact value ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)$ to ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{n} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-({y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)+Δy).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that the exact solution is ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)={x}^{2}+x.$ So, the $y$-value at the start of the step $x=1$ is ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(1)=2.$ Now, let's find the $y$-increment given by the numerical method.\n\nFirst, the slope at the start of the step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{exact}}^{′}(1) & =2(1)+1 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the increment at this step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{6}(x+{y}_{\\text{exact}}^{′}(1))\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(1+3)\\cdot 0.2 \\\\  & ≈0.1333.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the exact value at ${x}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}}) & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(x+Δx) \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(1+0.2) \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(1.2) \\\\  & =(1.2)^{2}+1.2 \\\\  & =2.64\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the LTE for the step starting at $x=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{6} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(1.2)-({y}_{\\text{exact}}(1)+Δy) \\\\  & =2.64-(2+0.1333) \\\\  & ≈0.507\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308580 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308247 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308580 1069 "q-334197" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308580 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308580 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-x+4{y}^{2},\\,y(0.5)=0\n$$\n\nUse the trapezoidal method with one step and Newton's method to approximate $y(1).$ Round your answer to two decimal places.\n\n$y(1)≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308580 1072 #uuid "a9441152-7cbd-58de-ad5b-3369b24306ab" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308580 1074 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nSince we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=1,$ starting from $x=0.5,$ we use a step size\n\n$$\nΔx=1-0.5=0.5.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $Δx$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}((-x+4{y}^{2})+(-{x}_{\\text{new}}+4({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}))\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =-0.25x-0.25{x}_{\\text{new}}+{y}^{2}+({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =y-0.25x-0.25{x}_{\\text{new}}+{y}^{2}+({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMoving all terms to the left-hand side gives the equation\n\n$$\n({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-{y}_{\\text{new}}+{y}^{2}+y-0.25x-0.25{x}_{\\text{new}}=0.\n$$\n\nSo, the general form of the function $g$ for step size $Δx=0.5$ is\n\n$$\ng({y}_{\\text{new}})=({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-{y}_{\\text{new}}+{y}^{2}+y-0.25x-0.25{x}_{\\text{new}}.\n$$\n\nNow, in this step, the new $x$ value is\n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=0.5+0.5=1.\n$$\n\nThen, the function $g$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({y}_{\\text{new}}) & =({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-{y}_{\\text{new}}+0+0-0.25(0.5)-0.25(1) \\\\  & =({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-{y}_{\\text{new}}-0.375.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use a root-finding method with starting point $y(0.5)=0$ to solve $g({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.$ We'll use Newton's method:\n\n$$\n({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n+1}=({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-\\frac{g(({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n})}{{g}^{′}(({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n})}.\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $g$ is\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}({y}_{\\text{new}})=2{y}_{\\text{new}}-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton's method iterative scheme is\n\n$$\n({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n+1}=({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-\\frac{({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}^{\\,2}-({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-0.375}{2({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-1}.\n$$\n\nApplying several iterations of Newton's method starting with $({y}_{\\text{new}})_{1}=y(0.5)=0,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({y}_{\\text{new}})_{1} & =0 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{2} & ≈-0.375 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{3} & ≈-0.29464 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{4} & ≈-0.29058 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{5} & ≈-0.29057\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(1)≈-0.29,$\nrounded to two decimal places." 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308581 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307724 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308581 1069 "q-334199" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308581 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308581 1071 "$$\n{y}^{″}-3{y}^{′}+2y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{3x}$ is a solution to the equation.\n- ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{2x}$ is a solution to the equation.\n- The functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation." 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308581 1072 #uuid "d23e4c59-133c-5f47-8ffc-1426beb350c3" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308581 1074 "Recall that to determine whether $y(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute $y$ and its derivatives into the equation. \n\nLet's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. We find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{1}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =3{e}^{3x} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & =9{e}^{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{e}^{3x}-3(3{e}^{3x})+2({e}^{3x}) & =0 \\\\ 8 & =0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}(x)$ is not a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement II is true. We find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{2}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =2{e}^{2x} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =4{e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{e}^{2x}-3(2{e}^{2x})+2({e}^{2x}) & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${y}_{1}(x)$ is not a solution of the given differential equation, ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ don't form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308582 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307740 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308582 1069 "q-334203" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308582 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308582 1071 "Find the general solution of the Clairaut differential equation:\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+({y}^{′})^{3}-2{y}^{′}.\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308582 1072 #uuid "85a7a33f-021f-53f3-94c6-c894c8583569" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308582 1074 "To solve the Clairaut differential equation\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+({y}^{′})^{3}-2{y}^{′},\n$$\n\nwe start by setting\n$p={y}^{′}.$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny=xp+{p}^{3}-2p.\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =(xp+{p}^{3}-2p)^{′} \\\\ p & =p+x{p}^{′}+3{p}^{2}{p}^{′}-2{p}^{′} \\\\ 0 & ={p}^{′}(x+3{p}^{2}-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe product on the right-hand side equals zero, which means at least one of the factors must be zero.\n\nThe general solution of the Clairaut differential equation is obtained if ${p}^{′}=0.$ Then, $p$ is constant, say \n\n$$\np=c.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $p=c$ into $y=xp+{p}^{3}-2p,$ we get the general solution\n\n$$\ny=cx+{c}^{3}-2c.\n$$\n\nThis represents a family of straight lines, one line for each constant value of $c.$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308583 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307773 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308583 1069 "q-334205" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308583 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308583 1071 "Find the general solution of the Clairaut differential equation:\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}-({y}^{′})^{2}+4({y}^{′})^{3}.\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308583 1072 #uuid "2c5badef-08ba-5c2a-807f-5a668303d055" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308583 1074 "To solve the Clairaut differential equation\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}-({y}^{′})^{2}+4({y}^{′})^{3},\n$$\n\nwe start by setting\n$p={y}^{′}.$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny=xp-{p}^{2}+4{p}^{3}.\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =(xp-{p}^{2}+4{p}^{3})^{′} \\\\ p & =p+x{p}^{′}-2p{p}^{′}+12{p}^{2}{p}^{′} \\\\ 0 & ={p}^{′}(x-2p+12{p}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe product on the right-hand side equals zero, which means at least one of the factors must be zero.\n\nThe general solution of the Clairaut differential equation is obtained if ${p}^{′}=0.$ Then, $p$ is constant, say \n\n$$\np=c.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $p=c$ into $y=xp-{p}^{2}+4{p}^{3},$ we get the general solution\n\n$$\ny=cx-{c}^{2}+4{c}^{3}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a family of straight lines, one line for each constant value of $c.$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308584 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307827 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308584 1069 "q-334209" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308584 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308584 1071 "Find the general solution of the Clairaut differential equation:\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+{e}^{{y}^{′}}.\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308584 1072 #uuid "2cb6e91e-c1ea-5de9-ac02-6af8a7dd2b0f" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308584 1074 "To solve the Clairaut differential equation\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+{e}^{{y}^{′}},\n$$\n\nwe start by setting\n$p={y}^{′}.$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny=xp+{e}^{p}.\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =(xp+{e}^{p})^{′} \\\\ p & =p+x{p}^{′}+{e}^{p}{p}^{′} \\\\ 0 & ={p}^{′}(x+{e}^{p})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe product on the right-hand side equals zero, which means at least one of the factors must be zero.\n\nThe general solution of the Clairaut differential equation is obtained if ${p}^{′}=0.$ Then, $p$ is constant, say \n\n$$\np=c.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $p=c$ into $y=xp+{e}^{p},$ we get the general solution\n\n$$\ny=cx+{e}^{c}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a family of straight lines, one line for each constant value of $c.$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308585 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308211 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308585 1069 "q-334210" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308585 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308585 1071 "Find the general solution of the Clairaut differential equation:\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{{y}^{′}},\\,{y}^{′}\\ne 0.\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308585 1072 #uuid "3082b563-6675-5fa7-b95b-17b8bbf9573a" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308585 1074 "To solve the Clairaut differential equation\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{{y}^{′}},\n$$\n\nwe start by setting\n$p={y}^{′}.$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny=xp+\\frac{1}{p}.\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={(xp+\\frac{1}{p})}^{′} \\\\ p & =p+x{p}^{′}-\\frac{1}{{p}^{2}}{p}^{′} \\\\ 0 & ={p}^{′}(x-\\frac{1}{{p}^{2}})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe product on the right-hand side equals zero, which means at least one of the factors must be zero.\n\nThe general solution of the Clairaut differential equation is obtained if ${p}^{′}=0.$ Then, $p$ is constant, say \n\n$$\np=c,\n$$\n\nwith the restriction $c\\ne 0.$\n\nSubstituting $p=c$ into $y=xp+\\frac{1}{p},$ we get the general solution\n\n$$\ny=cx+\\frac{1}{c}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a family of straight lines, one line for each constant value of $c\\ne 0.$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308586 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307714 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308586 1069 "q-334211" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308586 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308586 1071 "Find the general solution of the Clairaut differential equation:\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{′}),\\,{y}^{′}>0.\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308586 1072 #uuid "610c036d-c63c-589a-acb8-16947a6feeb0" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308586 1074 "To solve the Clairaut differential equation\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwe start by setting\n$p={y}^{′}.$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny=xp+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(p).\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={(xp+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(p))}^{′} \\\\ p & =p+x{p}^{′}+\\frac{1}{p}{p}^{′} \\\\ 0 & ={p}^{′}(x+\\frac{1}{p})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe product on the right-hand side equals zero, which means at least one of the factors must be zero.\n\nThe general solution of the Clairaut differential equation is obtained if ${p}^{′}=0.$ Then, $p$ is constant, say \n\n$$\np=c,\n$$\n\nwith the restriction $c>0.$\n\nSubstituting $p=c$ into $y=xp+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(p),$ we get the general solution\n\n$$\ny=cx+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(c).\n$$\n\nThis represents a family of straight lines, one line for each constant value of $c>0.$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308587 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308138 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308587 1069 "q-334221" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308587 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308587 1071 "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -5 \\\\ 1 & -6\\end{aligned}]x(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n*Hint: The matrix has the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4+\\text{i}$ with the corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2+\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$*" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308587 1072 #uuid "d942a907-7558-5547-92ea-8c390f2de7e3" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308587 1074 "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -5 \\\\ 1 & -6\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe are given the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4+\\text{i}$ and the corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2+\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{1}$ is \n${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}.$ But we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4+\\text{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2+\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{(-4+\\text{i})t}.\n$$\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}2+\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{(-4+\\text{i})t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2+\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-4t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{-4t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\text{i}{e}^{-4t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\text{i}{e}^{-4t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-{e}^{-4t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ {e}^{-4t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}{e}^{-4t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-4t}+\\text{i}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-4t} \\\\  & ={\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+\\text{i}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${w}_{1}$ and ${w}_{2}$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-4t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-4t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2{c}_{1}+{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & =-[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-4t}+3[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-4t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-4t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308588 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308012 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308588 1069 "q-334223" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308588 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308588 1071 "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{aligned}]x(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n*Hint: The matrix has the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=3+\\text{i}$ with the corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$*" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308588 1072 #uuid "ee50735a-2690-5859-ba95-03c8e56ae446" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308588 1074 "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe are given the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=3+\\text{i}$ and the corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{1}$ is \n${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}.$ But we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=3+\\text{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{(3+\\text{i})t}.\n$$\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}2-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{(3+\\text{i})t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\text{i}{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\text{i}{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ {e}^{3t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}+\\text{i}[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t} \\\\  & ={\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+\\text{i}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${w}_{1}$ and ${w}_{2}$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2{c}_{1}-{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & =-[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}-3[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308589 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307973 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308589 1069 "q-334225" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308589 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308589 1071 "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]x(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n*Hint: One of the eigenvalues of the matrix is ${\\lambda }_{1}=1+\\text{i}.$*" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308589 1072 #uuid "bca40108-c437-5b74-9086-915d017c3a83" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308589 1074 "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is ${\\lambda }_{1}=1+\\text{i}.$ \n\nLet's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors. For ${\\lambda }_{1}=1+\\text{i},$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=[\\begin{aligned}-\\text{i} & 1 \\\\ -1 & -\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\text{i} \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{1}$ is \n${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}.$ But we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=1+\\text{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{(1+\\text{i})t}.\n$$\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{(1+\\text{i})t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\text{i}{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ {e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+\\text{i}[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t} \\\\  & ={\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+\\text{i}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${w}_{1}$ and ${w}_{2}$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & =1[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+0[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308590 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307809 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308590 1069 "q-334226" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308590 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308590 1071 "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -8 \\\\ 1 & -5\\end{aligned}]x(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n*Hint: The matrix has the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3+2\\text{i}$ with the corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2+2\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$*" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308590 1072 #uuid "ccb3ed77-23b6-5390-a8c5-5f10def9c882" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308590 1074 "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -8 \\\\ 1 & -5\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe are given the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3+2\\text{i}$ and the corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2+2\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{1}$ is \n${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}.$ But we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3+2\\text{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2+2\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{(-3+2\\text{i})t}.\n$$\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}2+2\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{(-3+2\\text{i})t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2+2\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{-3t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+2\\text{i}{e}^{-3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\text{i}{e}^{-3t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-2{e}^{-3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ {e}^{-3t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}{e}^{-3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-3t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))+\\text{i}{e}^{-3t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)) \\\\ {e}^{-3t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\text{i}{e}^{-3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}+\\text{i}[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t} \\\\  & ={\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+\\text{i}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${w}_{1}$ and ${w}_{2}$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2{c}_{1}+2{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & =2[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}-3[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308591 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307741 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308591 1069 "q-334255" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308591 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308591 1071 "For which of the following values is the implicit Euler method stable, given that $\\lambda $ is the parameter of the test equation?\n\n- $\\lambda Δx=0.95$\n- $\\lambda Δx=1.2$\n- $\\lambda Δx=0.4$\n- $\\lambda Δx=2.3$\n\n*Hint: The stability function $R$ of the implicit Euler method is given by*\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δx)=\\frac{1}{1+\\lambda Δx}.\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308591 1072 #uuid "9207ec17-19e6-5c10-b901-32c1b663c7f6" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308591 1074 "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda Δx)$ is referred to as the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda Δx)|<1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nThe stability function of the implicit Euler method is\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δx)=\\frac{1}{1+\\lambda Δx}.\n$$\n\nLet's check the stability of the Implicit Euler Method for each of the given values in turn.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=0.95,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(0.95)| & =|\\frac{1}{1+0.95}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1}{1.95}| \\\\  & =0.5128…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the implicit Euler method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=1.2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(1.2)| & =|\\frac{1}{1+1.2}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1}{2.2}| \\\\  & =0.4545…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the implicit Euler method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=0.4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(0.4)| & =|\\frac{1}{1+0.4}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1}{1.4}| \\\\  & =0.7142…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the implicit Euler method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=2.3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(2.3)| & =|\\frac{1}{1+2.3}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1}{3.3}| \\\\  & =0.3030…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the implicit Euler method is stable.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, III, and IV\".\n\nNote that, since the implicit Euler method is absolutely stable, it is stable for all possible values of $\\lambda Δx.$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308592 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307693 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308592 1069 "q-334258" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308592 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308592 1071 "$$\n{y}^{″}-2{y}^{′}+5y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n- ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n- The functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation." 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308592 1072 #uuid "b4674804-88cb-5499-be8a-d4a228b08c3f" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308592 1074 "Recall that to determine whether $y(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute $y$ and its derivatives into the equation. \n\nLet's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. We find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{1}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & ={e}^{2x}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{2x}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2x}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-2{e}^{2x}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+5{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =0 \\\\ 2(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x){e}^{2x} & =0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}(x)$ is not a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement II is true. We find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{2}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & ={e}^{x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)) \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{x}(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))-2{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))+5{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${y}_{1}(x)$ is not a solution of the given differential equation, ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ don't form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308593 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308337 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308593 1069 "q-334260" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308593 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308593 1071 "$$\n{y}^{″}-6{y}^{′}+9y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{3x}$ is a solution to the equation.\n- ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{3x-1}$ is a solution to the equation.\n- The functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation." 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308593 1072 #uuid "ce7557df-4bdb-5791-b8df-2efda5a01a24" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308593 1074 "Recall that to determine whether $y(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute $y$ and its derivatives into the equation. \n\nLet's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{1}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =3{e}^{3x} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & =9{e}^{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{e}^{3x}-6\\cdot 3{e}^{3x}+9{e}^{3x} & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement II is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{2}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =3{e}^{3x-1} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =9{e}^{3x-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{e}^{3x-1}-6\\cdot 3{e}^{3x-1}+9{e}^{3x-1} & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement III is false. Both ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the equation. To check if these functions are linearly independent, we compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3x} & {e}^{3x-1} \\\\ 3{e}^{3x} & 3{e}^{3x-1}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={e}^{3x}\\cdot 3{e}^{3x-1}-3{e}^{3x}\\cdot {e}^{3x-1} \\\\  & =3{e}^{6x-1}-3{e}^{6x-1} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the  Wronskian equals $0$ for all $x\\in R,$ the functions are not linearly independent. Hence, ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ don't form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308594 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307659 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308594 1069 "q-334265" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308594 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308594 1071 "Find the general solution of the Clairaut differential equation:\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}-7{y}^{′}.\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308594 1072 #uuid "f0fd827b-f2f0-5582-b4de-95a31af7e9a3" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308594 1074 "To solve the Clairaut differential equation\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}-7{y}^{′},\n$$\n\nwe start by setting\n$p={y}^{′}.$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny=xp-7p.\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={(xp-7p)}^{′} \\\\ p & =p+x{p}^{′}-7{p}^{′} \\\\ 0 & ={p}^{′}(x-7)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe product on the right-hand side equals zero, which means at least one of the factors must be zero.\n\nThe general solution of the Clairaut differential equation is obtained if ${p}^{′}=0.$ Then, $p$ is constant, say \n\n$$\np=c.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $p=c$ into $y=xp-7{p}^{′},$ we get the general solution\n\n$$\ny=cx-7c.\n$$\n\nThis represents a family of straight lines, one line for each constant value of $c.$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308595 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307602 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308595 1069 "q-334266" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308595 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308595 1071 "$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}-3x{y}^{′}+4y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{2}$ is a solution to the equation.\n- ${y}_{2}(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)$ is a solution to the equation for $x>0.$\n- The functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation on $(0,\\infty ).$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308595 1072 #uuid "fbddaa01-5158-5d3b-b619-76268088aef1" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308595 1074 "Recall that to determine whether $y(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute $y$ and its derivatives into the equation. \n\nLet's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{1}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =2x \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot 2-3x\\cdot 2x+4{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-6{x}^{2}+4{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement II is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{2}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′}(x) & =2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+x \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″}(x) & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+3)-3x(2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+x)+4{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x) & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+3{x}^{2}-6{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)-3{x}^{2}+4{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x) & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement III is true. Both ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the equation. To check if these functions are linearly independent, we compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & {x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x) \\\\ 2x & 2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+x\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+x)-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =2{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+{x}^{3}-2{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x) \\\\  & ={x}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the  Wronskian is not identically $0$ on $(0,\\infty ),$ the functions are linearly independent. Hence, ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation on $(0,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"." 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308596 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308045 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308596 1069 "q-334278" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308596 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308596 1071 "$$\n{y}^{″}+4y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n- ${y}_{2}(x)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n- The functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation." 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308596 1072 #uuid "3a08136e-69ff-52a7-8d77-c9b9c2b3571c" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308596 1074 "Recall that to determine whether $y(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute $y$ and its derivatives into the equation. \n\nLet's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{1}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{′}(x) & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) \\\\ {y}_{1}^{″}(x) & =-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4\\opera