,\\text{d}x$ in terms of $p.$\n\n${\\int }_{-4}^{0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt[3]{x}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308614 1072 #uuid "9849ceaf-4c25-5d53-bdbe-40f0739e33f4" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308614 1074 "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x),$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x).$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt[3]{x}}$ is an odd function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =\\frac{(-x)^{2}}{\\sqrt[3]{-x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{-\\sqrt[3]{x}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt[3]{x}} \\\\  & =-f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt[3]{x}}$ is an odd function, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-4}^{4}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt[3]{x}}\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nFinally, using the adjacent intervals rule for integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{4}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt[3]{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-4}^{0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt[3]{x}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt[3]{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-4}^{0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt[3]{x}}\\,\\text{d}x+p & =0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-4}^{0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\sqrt[3]{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-p.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308615 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307575 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308615 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307816 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308615 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308257 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308615 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308319 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308615 1069 "q-334382" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308615 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308615 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 5 \\\\ -5 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear ODEs ${x}^{′}=Ax$ with the matrix $A$ shown above. Compute the **standard** fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ for this system.\n\n| ${Φ}_{0}(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308615 1072 #uuid "64533ce4-1ae9-513d-ba89-484c2cf3a62c" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308615 1074 "Suppose $A$ is a constant $n\\times n$ matrix and $x(t)$ is an $n\\times 1$ column vector. The standard fundamental matrix of the linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ denoted ${Φ}_{0}(t),$ is the unique matrix solution of the initial value problem \n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}^{′}(t)=A{Φ}_{0}(t),\\,{Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere ${I}_{n}$ is the identity matrix. \n\nFurthermore, it can be shown that ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}.$\n\nWe have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 5 \\\\ -5 & 0\\end{aligned}]t+{\\alpha }_{0}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0} & 5{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ -5{\\alpha }_{1}t & {\\alpha }_{0}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are constants to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues of $At$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=5\\text{i}t,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-5\\text{i}t,\n$$\n\nwhich are complex conjugates. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r((5\\text{i})t) & ={e}^{(5\\text{i})t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}(5\\text{i})t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{0}+5\\text{i}{\\alpha }_{1}t & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we equate the real and imaginary parts, and solve for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t) \\\\ 5{\\alpha }_{1}t=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}=\\frac{1}{5t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t) \\\\ 5{\\alpha }_{1}t & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{5t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\cdot t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308616 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308206 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308616 1069 "q-334383" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308616 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308616 1071 "Given that ${\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{|x|}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x=p,$ express the value of ${\\int }_{-2}^{0}{e}^{|x|}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ in terms of $p.$\n\n${\\int }_{-2}^{0}{e}^{|x|}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308616 1072 #uuid "ae73a5ce-ed7c-55ec-b81b-c25a686080d4" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308616 1074 "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x),$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x).$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x)={e}^{|x|}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)$ is an odd function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & ={e}^{|-x|}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-x) \\\\  & ={e}^{|x|}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)) \\\\  & =-{e}^{|x|}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x) \\\\  & =-f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x)={e}^{|x|}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)$ is an odd function, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{e}^{|x|}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nFinally, using the adjacent intervals rule for integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{e}^{|x|}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-2}^{0}{e}^{|x|}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{|x|}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-2}^{0}{e}^{|x|}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x+p & =0 \\\\ {\\int }_{-2}^{0}{e}^{|x|}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-p.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308617 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307606 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308617 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307865 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308617 1069 "q-334387" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308617 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308617 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear ODEs ${x}^{′}=Ax$ with the matrix $A$ shown above. Compute the **standard** fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ for this system.\n\n| ${Φ}_{0}(t)=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | ${e}^{3t}-{e}^{-3t}$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | ${e}^{3t}-{e}^{-3t}$ | {{field-2}} |  |  |" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308617 1072 #uuid "ff10c64d-19c8-5825-958b-92dcd06d6e53" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308617 1074 "Suppose $A$ is a constant $n\\times n$ matrix and $x(t)$ is an $n\\times 1$ column vector. The standard fundamental matrix of the linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ denoted ${Φ}_{0}(t),$ is the unique matrix solution of the initial value problem \n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}^{′}(t)=A{Φ}_{0}(t),\\,{Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere ${I}_{n}$ is the identity matrix. \n\nFurthermore, it can be shown that ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}.$\n\nWe have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]t+{\\alpha }_{0}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0} & 3{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ 3{\\alpha }_{1}t & {\\alpha }_{0}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are constants to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues of $At$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=-3t,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=3t,\n$$\n\nboth of multiplicity one. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}r(-3t)={e}^{-3t} \\\\ r(3t)={e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}-3t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{-3t} \\\\ 3t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n- Subtracting the second equation from the first one, we get\n\n$$\n-6t{\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{-3t}-{e}^{3t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{6t}({e}^{-3t}-{e}^{3t}).\n$$\n\n- Adding the first and the second equations, we get\n\n$$\n2{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{-3t}+{e}^{3t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{0}=\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{-3t}+{e}^{3t}).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0} & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{-3t}+{e}^{3t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{3t}+{e}^{-3t}) \\\\ 3{\\alpha }_{1}t & =3\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{6t}({e}^{-3t}-{e}^{3t}))t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{3t}-{e}^{-3t})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3t}+{e}^{-3t} & {e}^{3t}-{e}^{-3t} \\\\ {e}^{3t}-{e}^{-3t} & {e}^{3t}+{e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308618 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308310 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308618 1069 "q-334402" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308618 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308618 1071 "Find the singular solution to the following Clairaut differential equation:\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}-({y}^{′})^{4}.\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308618 1072 #uuid "a61a3020-c49f-5adf-b3f7-a7d5dfccfd42" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308618 1074 "A Clairaut differential equation has the form\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $g$ is a differentiable function of ${y}^{′}.$\n\nTo solve the Clairaut differential equation\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}-({y}^{′})^{4},\n$$\n\nwe start by setting $p={y}^{′}.$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny=xp-{p}^{4}.\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =(xp-{p}^{4})^{′} \\\\ p & =p+x{p}^{′}-4{p}^{3}{p}^{′} \\\\ 0 & ={p}^{′}(x-4{p}^{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe product on the right-hand side equals zero, so at least one factor must be zero.\n\nThe singular solution of the Clairaut differential equation is obtained if $x-4{p}^{3}=0.$ Then,\n\n$$\nx=4{p}^{3}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute this into $y=xp-{p}^{4}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =xp-{p}^{4} \\\\  & =(4{p}^{3})p-{p}^{4} \\\\  & =4{p}^{4}-{p}^{4} \\\\  & =3{p}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the singular solution is given parametrically by\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=4{p}^{3} \\\\ y=3{p}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo eliminate $p,$ we first solve the first equation for $p:$\n\n$$\np={(\\frac{x}{4})}^{1/3}\n$$\n\nNext, let's substitute this expression for $p$ into the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3{p}^{4} \\\\  & =3{({(\\frac{x}{4})}^{1/3})}^{4} \\\\  & =3{(\\frac{x}{4})}^{4/3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the explicit form of the singular solution is\n\n$$\ny=3{(\\frac{x}{4})}^{4/3}.\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308619 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307595 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308619 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307600 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308619 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307858 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308619 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308053 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308619 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308129 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308619 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308179 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308619 1069 "q-334404" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308619 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308619 1071 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=2{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=3{x}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear ODEs above, where $t\\in R.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.$\\,$ The function $a(t)=[\\begin{aligned}4{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ \n{{field-1}} \na solution of the system.\n\n$\\,$ The function $b(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{3t} \\\\ 2{e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}]$ \n{{field-2}} \na solution of the system.\n\n$\\,$ The function $c(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}]$ \n{{field-3}} \na solution of the system.\n\n$\\,$ {{field-4}} \nis a set of fundamental solutions to the system because the Wronskian \n\n$$\nW({{field-5}})={{field-6}}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n$\\,$ for at least one $t\\in R.$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308619 1072 #uuid "940032dd-db72-5ffe-8f84-7331f242458a" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308619 1074 "The Wronskian $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})$ of two vector-valued functions \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t)\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{22}(t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis defined as\n\n$$\nW({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=det({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11}(t) & {x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t) & {x}_{22}(t)\\end{aligned}|.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- If the Wronskian is not identically zero, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If the Wronskian is identically zero, and it's known that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are solutions to the same system of linear ODEs, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nFirst, we write our system in matrix form:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute the given vectors into the matrix differential equation:\n\n- $a(t)=[\\begin{aligned}4{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ $\\text{is}$ a solution of the system. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}(4{e}^{2t})^{′} \\\\ (0)^{′}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}8{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot 4{e}^{2t}+1\\cdot 0 \\\\ 0\\cdot 4{e}^{2t}+3\\cdot 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}8{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}8{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $b(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{3t} \\\\ 2{e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}]$ $\\text{is}$ a solution of the system. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}(2{e}^{3t})^{′} \\\\ (2{e}^{3t})^{′}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{3t} \\\\ 2{e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}6{e}^{3t} \\\\ 6{e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot 2{e}^{3t}+1\\cdot 2{e}^{3t} \\\\ 0\\cdot 2{e}^{3t}+3\\cdot 2{e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}6{e}^{3t} \\\\ 6{e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}6{e}^{3t} \\\\ 6{e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}]\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $c(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}]$ $\\text{isn't}$ a solution of the system. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}({e}^{2t})^{′} \\\\ ({e}^{2t})^{′}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{2t} \\\\ 2{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot {e}^{2t}+1\\cdot {e}^{2t} \\\\ 0\\cdot {e}^{2t}+3\\cdot {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{2t} \\\\ 2{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{2t} \\\\ 3{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}]\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, consider the set of solutions ${a,b}.$ Its Wronskian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(a,b) & =|\\begin{aligned}4{e}^{2t} & 2{e}^{3t} \\\\ 0 & 2{e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(4{e}^{2t})\\cdot 2{e}^{3t}-(2{e}^{3t})\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =8{e}^{5t}-0 \\\\  & =8{e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is nonzero for at least one $t\\in R.$ Thus, since $a$ and $b$ are both solutions of the same linear system, and their Wronskian is nonzero for at least one $t\\in R,$ ${a,b}$ is a set of fundamental solutions of the system." 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308620 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308244 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308620 1069 "q-334426" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308620 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308620 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x+{y}^{2},\\,y(0)=0\n$$\n\nUse the trapezoidal method with one step and Newton's method to approximate $y(0.4).$ Round your answer to two decimal places.\n\n$y(0.4)≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308620 1072 #uuid "c0f72cb1-7955-5cb4-ba44-5d2b9143edea" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308620 1074 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nSince we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=0.4,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=0.4-0=0.4.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}((x+{y}^{2})+({x}_{\\text{new}}+({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}))\\cdot 0.4 \\\\  & =0.2x+0.2{y}^{2}+0.2{x}_{\\text{new}}+0.2({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+0.2x+0.2{y}^{2}+0.2{x}_{\\text{new}}+0.2({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMoving all terms to the left-hand side gives the equation\n\n$$\n0.2({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-{y}_{\\text{new}}+y+0.2x+0.2{y}^{2}+0.2{x}_{\\text{new}}=0.\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by $5$ to eliminate the decimals, we obtain\n\n$$\n({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-5{y}_{\\text{new}}+5y+x+{y}^{2}+{x}_{\\text{new}}=0.\n$$\n\nSo, the general form of the function $g$ for step size $Δx=0.4$ is\n\n$$\ng({y}_{\\text{new}})=({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-5{y}_{\\text{new}}+5y+x+{y}^{2}+{x}_{\\text{new}}.\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is\n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=0+0.4=0.4.\n$$\n\nThen, the function $g$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({y}_{\\text{new}}) & =({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-5{y}_{\\text{new}}+5\\cdot 0+0+{0}^{2}+0.4 \\\\  & =({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}-5{y}_{\\text{new}}+0.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use a root-finding method with starting point $y=0$ to solve $g({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.$ We'll use Newton's method:\n\n$$\n({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n+1}=({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-\\frac{g({y}_{\\text{new}})}{{g}^{′}({y}_{\\text{new}})}.\n$$\n\nNote that the derivative of $g$ is\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}({y}_{\\text{new}})=2({y}_{\\text{new}})-5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton's method iterative scheme is as follows:\n\n$$\n({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n+1}=({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-\\frac{(({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n})^{2}-5({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}+0.4}{2({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-5}.\n$$\n\nApplying several iterations of Newton's method starting with $y(0)=0,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({y}_{\\text{new}})_{1} & =0 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{2} & =0.08 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{3} & ≈0.081\\,322 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{4} & ≈0.081\\,323\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(0.4)≈0.08,$ rounded to two decimal places." 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308621 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308034 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308621 1069 "q-334431" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308621 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308621 1071 "For which of the following values is the Runge-Kutta Method (RK4) stable, given that $\\lambda $ is the parameter of the test equation?\n\n- $\\lambda Δx=0.5$\n- $\\lambda Δx=1.5$\n- $\\lambda Δx=2.5$\n- $\\lambda Δx=3.2$\n\n*Hint: The stability function $R$ of the RK4 method is given by*\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δ)=1-\\lambda Δx+\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{3}}{6}+\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{4}}{24}.\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308621 1072 #uuid "1f92808c-572e-59a4-9f7a-86aaaf0f9d81" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308621 1074 "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda Δx)$ is referred to as the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda Δx)|<1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nThe stability function of the RK4 Method is\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δx)=1-\\lambda Δx+\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{3}}{6}+\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{4}}{24}.\n$$\n\nLet's check the stability of the RK4 Method for each of the given values in turn.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=0.5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(0.5)| & =|1-0.5+\\frac{(0.5)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(0.5)^{3}}{6}+\\frac{(0.5)^{4}}{24}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{233}{384}| \\\\  & =0.606…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the RK4 Method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=1.5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(1.5)| & =|1-1.5+\\frac{(1.5)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(1.5)^{3}}{6}+\\frac{(1.5)^{4}}{24}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{35}{128}| \\\\  & =0.273…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the RK4 Method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=2.5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(2.5)| & =|1-2.5+\\frac{(2.5)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(2.5)^{3}}{6}+\\frac{(2.5)^{4}}{24}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{83}{128}| \\\\  & =0.648…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the RK4 Method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=3.2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(3)| & =|1-3.2+\\frac{(3.2)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(3.2)^{3}}{6}+\\frac{(3.2)^{4}}{24}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{3427}{1875}| \\\\  & =1.827…<1.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *outside* the stability region, the RK4 Method is unstable.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only\".\n\nNote that the stability region of the RK4 Method is $0<\\lambda Δx<2.785…,$ so it is not absolutely stable." 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308622 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308108 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308622 1069 "q-334435" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308622 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308622 1071 "For which of the following values is the Runge-Kutta Method (RK4) stable, given that $\\lambda $ is the parameter of the test equation?\n\n- $\\lambda Δx=0.2$\n- $\\lambda Δx=1.8$\n- $\\lambda Δx=2.7$\n- $\\lambda Δx=6.6$\n\n*Hint: The stability function $R$ of the RK4 method is given by*\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δ)=1-\\lambda Δx+\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{2}}{2}-