nd{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The GTE of the third step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}_{3} & =y({x}_{3})-{y}_{3} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot 0.6)-0.240 \\\\  & ≈0.122.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed table is as given below.\n\n| $n$ | ${x}_{n}$ | ${y}_{n}$ | $y({x}_{n})$ | ${e}_{n}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $1.000$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $0.2$ | $0.920$ | $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot 0.2)$ | $0.001$ |\n| $2$ | $0.4$ | $0.620$ | $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot 0.4)$ | $0.077$ |\n| $3$ | $0.6$ | $0.240$ | $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot 0.6)$ | $0.122$ |" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308640 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307719 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308640 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307769 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308640 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307908 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308640 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308304 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308640 1069 "q-334499" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308640 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308640 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear ODEs ${x}^{′}=Ax$ with the matrix $A$ shown above. Compute the **standard** fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ for this system.\n\n| ${Φ}_{0}(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308640 1072 #uuid "691520cf-4380-50be-8b5c-ac8a181c7472" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308640 1074 "Suppose $A$ is a constant $n\\times n$ matrix and $x(t)$ is an $n\\times 1$ column vector. The standard fundamental matrix of the linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ denoted ${Φ}_{0}(t),$ is the unique matrix solution of the initial value problem \n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}^{′}(t)=A{Φ}_{0}(t),\\,{Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere ${I}_{n}$ is the identity matrix. \n\nFurthermore, it can be shown that ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}.$\n\nWe have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]t+{\\alpha }_{0}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & {\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ 0 & 5{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are constants to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The only eigenvalue of $At$ is \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=5t\n$$\n\nof multiplicity two. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}r(5t)={e}^{5t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\lambda }r(5t)={e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}5t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{5t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n- From the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{5t}.\n$$\n\n- Substituting this into the first one, we get\n\n$$\n5t{e}^{5t}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{5t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{5t}(1-5t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =5{e}^{5t}t+{e}^{5t}(1-5t) \\\\  & ={e}^{5t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}t & ={e}^{5t}t \\\\  & =t{e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{5t} & t{e}^{5t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308641 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307598 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308641 1069 "q-33450" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308641 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308641 1071 "Which of the following gives the equation of the tangent to the curve $r(t)=t\\,i+({t}^{3}+1)\\,j+{t}^{5}\\,k$ at the point $P(1,2,1),$ \nvalid for real values of $u?$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308641 1072 #uuid "d9ca2f91-7dd0-5fda-a8e2-20d53e5766ab" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308641 1074 "The point $P(1,2,1)$ is at the tip of $r(t)$ for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t.$ At this point,  the vector ${r}^{′}({t}_{0})$ is tangent to the curve, so the tangent line to the curve at $P$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nR(u)=r({t}_{0})+u\\,{r}^{′}({t}_{0}),\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to $P.$ We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =i+2\\,j+k \\\\ t\\,i+({t}^{3}+1)\\,j+{t}^{5}\\,k & =i+2\\,j+k,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand equating the coefficients of $i,j,$ and $k$ yields the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}t=1 \\\\ {t}^{3}+1=2 \\\\ {t}^{5}=1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $t=1.$ This value also satifies the other two equations. Therefore, $P(1,2,1)$ is at the tip of the vector $r(1).$  \n\nWe calculate ${r}^{′}(t):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}+1)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{5})\\,k \\\\  & =i+3{t}^{2}\\,j+5{t}^{4}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we evaluate ${r}^{′}(t)$ at $t=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(1) & =i+3(1)^{2}\\,j+5(1)^{4}\\,k \\\\  & =i+3\\,j+5\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the given curve at the point $P(1,2,1)$ is \n\n$$\nR(u)=i+2\\,j+\\,k+u(\\,i+3\\,j+5\\,k),\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308642 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308254 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308642 1069 "q-33451" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308642 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308642 1071 "Find the shortest distance between the point $P(2,0,-5)$ and the plane $Π$ with equation $(r-[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}])\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]=0.$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308642 1072 #uuid "5cc5509e-3cff-5470-9ead-3ffe928da922" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308642 1074 "Let's pick a point on the plane, for instance, $Q(6,3,1).$ Then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, from the equation of the plane, we get a normal vector $n=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$\n\nFinally, the shortest distance from the point $P$ to the plane $Π$ is the absolute value of the scalar projection of $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ onto the normal vector of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|{\\text{comp}}_{n}\\,\\overset{\\to }{PQ}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot n|}{|n|} \\\\  & =\\frac{|4\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 2+6\\cdot 3|}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{3}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|4+6+18|}{\\sqrt{1+4+9}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|28|}{\\sqrt{14}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{14}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308643 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307611 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308643 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307734 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308643 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307821 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308643 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308021 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308643 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308094 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308643 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308162 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308643 1069 "q-334527" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308643 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308643 1071 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-3{x}_{1}(t)-3{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=3{x}_{1}(t)+3{x}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear ODEs above, where $t\\in R.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.$\\,$ The function $a(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1-3t \\\\ 3t\\end{aligned}]$  \n{{field-1}}  \na solution of the system.\n\n$\\,$ The function $b(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2(1-3t) \\\\ 6t\\end{aligned}]$  \n{{field-2}}  \na solution of the system.\n\n$\\,$ The function $c(t)=[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ t\\end{aligned}]$  \n{{field-3}}  \na solution of the system.\n\n$\\,$ {{field-4}}  \ndoes not give a set of fundamental solutions to the system because the Wronskian  \n\n$$\nW({{field-5}})={{field-6}}\n$$\n\n$\\,$ for all $t\\in R.$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308643 1072 #uuid "57f185dd-4868-5d08-a6b7-2a3380ec12f7" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308643 1074 "The Wronskian $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})$ of two vector-valued functions\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t)\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{22}(t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis defined as\n\n$$\nW({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=det({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11}(t) & {x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t) & {x}_{22}(t)\\end{aligned}|.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- If the Wronskian is not identically zero, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If the Wronskian is identically zero, and it's known that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are solutions to the same system of linear ODEs, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly dependent. In this case, they do **not** form a fundamental set of solutions.\n\nFirst, we write our system in matrix form:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}]x(t).\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute the given vectors into the matrix differential equation:\n\n- $a(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1-3t \\\\ 3t\\end{aligned}]$ $\\text{is}$ a solution of the system. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}(1-3t)^{′} \\\\ (3t)^{′}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1-3t \\\\ 3t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-3(1-3t)+(-3)\\cdot 3t \\\\ 3(1-3t)+3\\cdot 3t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $b(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2(1-3t) \\\\ 6t\\end{aligned}]$ $\\text{is}$ also a solution of the system. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}(2(1-3t))^{′} \\\\ (6t)^{′}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2(1-3t) \\\\ 6t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-3\\cdot 2(1-3t)+(-3)\\cdot 6t \\\\ 3\\cdot 2(1-3t)+3\\cdot 6t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $c(t)=[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ t\\end{aligned}]$ $\\text{isn't}$ a solution of the system. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}(t)^{′} \\\\ (t)^{′}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-3t+(-3)t \\\\ 3t+3t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-6t \\\\ 6t\\end{aligned}]\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, consider the set of solutions ${a,b}.$ Its Wronskian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(a,b) & =|\\begin{aligned}1-3t & 2(1-3t) \\\\ 3t & 6t\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(1-3t)\\cdot 6t-2(1-3t)\\cdot 3t \\\\  & =6t(1-3t)-6t(1-3t) \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is zero for all $t\\in R.$ Thus, since $a$ and $b$ are both solutions of the same linear system, but their Wronskian is identically zero, they are linearly dependent and do **not** form a fundamental set of solutions of the system." 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308644 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307529 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308644 1069 "q-33454" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308644 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308644 1071 "Find the shortest distance between the point $P(4,-2,7)$ and the plane $Π$ with equation $r\\cdot ⟨-3,2,3⟩=27.$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308644 1072 #uuid "1df7d5a8-576c-5e6b-b09e-347611e72e67" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308644 1074 "First, we need to find a point on the plane. If $r=⟨x,y,z⟩,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨-3,2,3⟩ & =27 \\\\ -3x+2y+3z & =27 \\\\ 3z & =27+3x-2y \\\\ z & =9+x-\\frac{2}{3}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting $x=6$ and $y=3,$ we get \n\n$$\nz=9+6-\\frac{2}{3}(3)=13.\n$$\n\nSo, the point $Q(6,3,13)$ lies on the plane. \n\nNow, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =⟨6,3,13⟩-⟨4,-2,7⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,5,6⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the equation of a plane, we get a normal vector $n=⟨-3,2,3⟩.$\n\nFinally, the shortest distance from the point $P$ to the plane $Π$ is the absolute value of the scalar projection of $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ onto the normal vector of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|{\\text{comp}}_{n}\\,\\overset{\\to }{PQ}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot n|}{|n|} \\\\  & =\\frac{|2\\cdot (-3)+5\\cdot 2+6\\cdot 3|}{\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+{2}^{2}+{3}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|-6+10+18|}{\\sqrt{9+4+9}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|22|}{\\sqrt{22}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{22}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308645 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307927 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308645 1069 "q-334542" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308645 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308645 1071 "A numerical method is used to approximate the solution to an initial value problem.\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.12,$ the observed global truncation error is $e(0.12)=0.310.$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.06,$ the observed global truncation error is $e(0.06)=0.078.$\n\nUse this data to estimate the order of the numerical method." 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308645 1072 #uuid "49aaf671-dedd-5860-803a-66d152eac8ca" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308645 1074 "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the GTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.12$ to $0.06.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.12}{0.06}=2.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the GTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p}={2}^{p}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the GTE actually decreases from $0.310$ to $0.078.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.310}{0.078}=3.974….\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the GTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{p} & ≈3.974… \\\\ p & ≈{log}_{2}⁡(3.974…) \\\\ p & ≈1.990….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the approximate order of the method is $2.$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308646 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307705 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308646 1069 "q-334544" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308646 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308646 1071 "A numerical method is used to approximate the solution to an initial value problem.\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.18,$ the observed global truncation error is $e(0.18)=0.740.$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.06,$ the observed global truncation error is $e(0.06)=0.090.$\n\nUse this data to estimate the order of the numerical method." 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308646 1072 #uuid "d97636b8-044d-5032-9858-9a92e3347d6a" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308646 1074 "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the GTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.18$ to $0.06.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.18}{0.06}=3.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the GTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p}={3}^{p}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the GTE actually decreases from $0.740$ to $0.090.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.740}{0.090}=8.222….\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the GTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{p} & ≈8.222… \\\\ p & ≈{log}_{3}⁡(8.222…) \\\\ p & ≈1.917….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the approximate order of the method is $2.$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308647 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307996 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308647 1069 "q-334546" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308647 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308647 1071 "A numerical method is used to approximate the solution to an initial value problem.\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.18,$ the observed global truncation error is $e(0.18)=0.486.$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.06,$ the observed global truncation error is $e(0.06)=0.006.$\n\nUse this data to estimate the order of the numerical method." 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308647 1072 #uuid "3f94b6c1-fa0e-5c48-970a-a0d605d1856d" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308647 1074 "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the GTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.18$ to $0.06.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.18}{0.06}=3.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the GTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p}={3}^{p}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the GTE actually decreases from $0.486$ to $0.006.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.486}{0.006}=81.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the GTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{p} & ≈81 \\\\ p & ≈{log}_{3}⁡(81) \\\\ p & ≈4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the approximate order of the method is $4.$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308648 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307753 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308648 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307803 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308648 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307904 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308648 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307997 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308648 1069 "q-334569" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308648 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308648 1071 "The global truncation error (GTE) of a numerical method with step size $Δx$ is\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=5(Δx)^{4}-(Δx)^{5}.\n$$\n\nComplete the following sentences.$\\,$ With step size $Δx=0.7,$ the global truncation error is $≈$\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ With step size $Δx=0.2,$ the global truncation error is $≈$\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Decreasing from $Δx=0.7$ to $Δx=0.2,$ the GTE is reduced by a factor of $≈$\n{{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ According to this data, the order of the method is approximately\n{{field-4}}." 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308648 1072 #uuid "2d2f0e2d-e315-598e-85b7-5c7b01c99d78" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308648 1074 "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the GTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p}.$\n\nFirst, we compute the GTE for the given step sizes:\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.7,$ the GTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}e(0.7) & =5(0.7)^{4}-(0.7)^{5} \\\\  & =5\\cdot 0.2401-1\\cdot 0.168\\,07 \\\\  & =1.200\\,5-0.168\\,07 \\\\  & =1.032\\,43 \\\\  & ≈1.032.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.2,$ the GTE is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}e(0.2) & =5(0.2)^{4}-(0.2)^{5} \\\\  & =5\\cdot 0.001\\,6-1\\cdot 0.000\\,32 \\\\  & =0.008-0.000\\,32 \\\\  & =0.007\\,68 \\\\  & ≈0.008.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the order by comparing the errors. The step size decreases from $0.7$ to $0.2.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.7}{0.2}=3.5.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the GTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p}=(3.5)^{p}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the GTE actually decreases from $1.032\\,43$ to $0.007\\,68.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{1.032\\,43}{0.007\\,68}=134.430\\,99…≈134.431.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the GTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3.5)^{p} & ≈134.430\\,99… \\\\ p & ≈{log}_{3.5}⁡(134.430\\,99…) \\\\ p & ≈3.912….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is\n$4.$\n\nIndeed, this coincides with the definition of the order from the expression for the GTE:\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=5(Δx)^{4}-(Δx)^{5}\\,⇒\\,e(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{4})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nHence, the order of the method is $4.$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308649 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308241 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308649 1069 "q-33457" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308649 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308649 1071 "Which of the following gives the equation of the tangent to the curve $r(t)=({t}^{2}-1)\\,i+\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}\\,j+(t-1)\\,k$ at the point corresponding to $t=2,$  valid for all  real values of $u?$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308649 1072 #uuid "eafab8fd-4a82-53c3-b03e-eaa84e01b098" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308649 1074 "At the point where $t=2$,  the vector ${r}^{′}(2)$ is tangent to the curve, so the tangent line to the curve at $P$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nR(u)=r(2)+u{r}^{′}(2),\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $r(2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(2) & =({2}^{2}-1)\\,i+\\sqrt{1+{2}^{2}}\\,j+(2-1)\\,k \\\\  & =3\\,i+\\sqrt{5}\\,j+\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-1)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-1)\\,k \\\\  & =2t\\,i+\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}}\\,j+\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we evaluate ${r}^{′}(t)$ at $t=2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(2) & =2(2)\\,i+\\frac{(2)}{\\sqrt{1+(2)^{2}}}\\,j+\\,k \\\\  & =4\\,i+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\,j+\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the given curve at the point \n where $t=2$ is \n\n$$\nR(u)=3i+\\sqrt{5}\\,j+\\,k+u(4\\,i+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\,j+\\,k),\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308650 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307963 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308650 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308164 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308650 1069 "q-334581" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308650 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308650 1071 "Find ${e}^{At}$ for $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}].$ \n\n*Hint: The eigenvalues of $A$ are $1$ and $4.$*\n\n| ${e}^{At}=\\,\\,\\frac{1}{3}$ |  | ${e}^{4t}+2{e}^{t}$ | $2{e}^{4t}-2{e}^{t}$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |  |" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308650 1072 #uuid "51cffc2a-3512-573b-9f6a-f449e54b28ee" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308650 1074 "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]t+{\\alpha }_{0}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & 2{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}t & 3{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues of $At$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=t,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=4t,\n$$\n\nboth of multiplicity one. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}r(t)={e}^{t} \\\\ r(4t)={e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{t} \\\\ 4t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n- Subtracting the first equation from the second one, we get\n\n$$\n3t{\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{4t}-{e}^{t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{1}=\\frac{1}{3t}({e}^{4t}-{e}^{t}).\n$$\n\n- Multiplying the first equation by $4$ and subtracting the second one, we get\n\n$$\n3{\\alpha }_{0}=4{e}^{t}-{e}^{4t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{0}=\\frac{1}{3}(4{e}^{t}-{e}^{4t}).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{1}t & =(\\frac{1}{3t}({e}^{4t}-{e}^{t}))t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}({e}^{4t}-{e}^{t}), \\\\ 3{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =3(\\frac{1}{3t}({e}^{4t}-{e}^{t}))t+\\frac{1}{3}(4{e}^{t}-{e}^{4t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}((3{e}^{4t}-3{e}^{t})+(4{e}^{t}-{e}^{4t})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(2{e}^{4t}+{e}^{t}),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{e}^{At}=\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4t}+2{e}^{t} & 2{e}^{4t}-2{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{4t}-{e}^{t} & 2{e}^{4t}+{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308651 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307846 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308651 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307901 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308651 1069 "q-334586" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308651 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308651 1071 "Find ${e}^{At}$ for $A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}].$ \n\n*Hint: The eigenvalues of $A$ are $0$ and $1.$*\n\n| ${e}^{At}=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $2-2{e}^{t}$ | $2{e}^{t}-1$ |  |  |" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308651 1072 #uuid "ce10f4df-94a1-54d0-b0b8-734df6cd8ed5" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308651 1074 "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}]t+{\\alpha }_{0}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & {\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ -2{\\alpha }_{1}t & 2{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues of $At$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=0,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=t,\n$$\n\nboth of multiplicity one. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}r(0)={e}^{0} \\\\ r(t)={e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0}=1 \\\\ t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n- From the first equation, we immediately get\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{0}=1.\n$$\n\n- Substituting ${\\alpha }_{0}=1$ into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\nt{\\alpha }_{1}+1={e}^{t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{1}=\\frac{1}{t}({e}^{t}-1).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =-(\\frac{1}{t}({e}^{t}-1))t+1 \\\\  & =-({e}^{t}-1)+1 \\\\  & =-{e}^{t}+2 \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}t & =(\\frac{1}{t}({e}^{t}-1))t \\\\  & ={e}^{t}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{t}+2 & {e}^{t}-1 \\\\ 2-2{e}^{t} & 2{e}^{t}-1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308652 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307609 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308652 1069 "q-334595" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308652 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308652 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-x+3y,\\,y(0)=2\n$$\n\nThe trapezoidal method is applied with step size $Δx=1.$ Find the general formula for the new $y$ value, ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ in terms of the current values $x$ and $y,$ and the new value ${x}_{\\text{new}}.$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308652 1072 #uuid "fddef3e6-76fd-5dc5-bf4e-e48ec78de829" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308652 1074 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nIn this example, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-x+3y+(-{x}_{\\text{new}}+3{y}_{\\text{new}}))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-x-{x}_{\\text{new}}+3y)+\\frac{3}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{2}(-x-{x}_{\\text{new}}+3y)+\\frac{3}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{2}(-x-{x}_{\\text{new}}+5y)+\\frac{3}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{2}(-x-{x}_{\\text{new}}+5y) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =x+{x}_{\\text{new}}-5y\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308653 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307684 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308653 1069 "q-334596" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308653 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308653 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=4x-5y,\\,y(-2)=1\n$$\n\nThe trapezoidal method is applied with step size $Δx=5.$ Find the general formula for the new $y$ value, ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ in terms of the current values $x$ and $y,$ and the new value ${x}_{\\text{new}}.$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308653 1072 #uuid "74ef19a7-1756-5514-aa85-f55ea229ec0f" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308653 1074 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nIn this example, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(4x-5y+(4{x}_{\\text{new}}-5{y}_{\\text{new}}))\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}(4x+4{x}_{\\text{new}}-5y)-\\frac{25}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{5}{2}(4x+4{x}_{\\text{new}}-5y)-\\frac{25}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{5}{2}\\,(4x+4{x}_{\\text{new}}-\\frac{23}{5}y)-\\frac{25}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{27}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{5}{2}\\,(4x+4{x}_{\\text{new}}-\\frac{23}{5}y) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{5}{27}\\,(4x+4{x}_{\\text{new}}-\\frac{23}{5}y) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{27}(20x+20{x}_{\\text{new}}-23y)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308654 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308178 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308654 1069 "q-334602" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308654 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308654 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={e}^{2x}+{x}^{2}y,\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nThe trapezoidal method is applied with step size $Δx=2.$ Find the general formula for the new $y$ value, ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ in terms of the current values $x$ and $y,$ and the new value ${x}_{\\text{new}}.$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308654 1072 #uuid "5fac9ee1-d796-58d4-bff9-73a6749c88fe" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308654 1074 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nIn this example, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{2x}+{x}^{2}y+({e}^{2{x}_{\\text{new}}}+{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}))\\cdot 2 \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}+{e}^{2{x}_{\\text{new}}}+{x}^{2}y+{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+{e}^{2x}+{e}^{2{x}_{\\text{new}}}+{x}^{2}y+{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & ={e}^{2x}+{e}^{2{x}_{\\text{new}}}+({x}^{2}+1)y+{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}}-{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} & ={e}^{2x}+{e}^{2{x}_{\\text{new}}}+({x}^{2}+1)y \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}}(1-{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}) & ={e}^{2x}+{e}^{2{x}_{\\text{new}}}+({x}^{2}+1)y \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{{e}^{2x}+{e}^{2{x}_{\\text{new}}}+({x}^{2}+1)y}{1-{x}_{\\text{new}}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308655 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308172 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308655 1069 "q-334603" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308655 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308655 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)-2xy,\\,y(0)=-2\n$$\n\nThe trapezoidal method is applied with step size $Δx=3.$ Find the general formula for the new $y$ value, ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ in terms of the current values $x$ and $y,$ and the new value ${x}_{\\text{new}}.$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308655 1072 #uuid "2e6fe7d5-1d57-5f56-ad69-d91395e9a5f5" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308655 1074 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nIn this example, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)-2xy+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{\\text{new}}+1)-2{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}}))\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{\\text{new}}+1)-2xy)-3{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{3}{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{\\text{new}}+1)-2xy)-3{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{3}{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{\\text{new}}+1)-2xy+\\frac{2}{3}y)-3{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}}+3{x}_{\\text{new}}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{3}{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{\\text{new}}+1)-2xy+\\frac{2}{3}y) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}}(1+3{x}_{\\text{new}}) & =\\frac{3}{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{\\text{new}}+1)-2xy+\\frac{2}{3}y) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{\\text{new}}+1)-6xy+2y}{2+6{x}_{\\text{new}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308656 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307987 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308656 1069 "q-33461" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308656 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308656 1071 "Which of the following gives the equation of the tangent to the curve $r(t)=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+{sin}^{2}t\\,k$ at the point corresponding to $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$  valid for all  real values of $u?$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308656 1072 #uuid "df456207-ebf6-517a-a452-6527239e70c8" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308656 1074 "At the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$,  the vector ${r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ is tangent to the curve, so the tangent line to the curve at $P$ can be parametrized as\n\n \n\n$$\nR(u)=r(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+u{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}),\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we find $r(\\frac{\\pi }{4}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,i+\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,j+{sin}^{2}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,k \\\\  & =\\,i+\\,j+\\frac{1}{2}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n   \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({sin}^{2}t)\\,k \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we evaluate ${r}^{′}(t)$ at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,i+\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,j-\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})k \\\\  & =\\,i-\\,j+\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the given curve at the point \n where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is\n\n$$\nR(u)=\\,i+\\,j+\\frac{1}{2}\\,k+u(\\,i-\\,j+\\,k),\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308657 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307775 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308657 1069 "q-334620" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308657 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308657 1071 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-2x(t)+4y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-x(t)+2y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvector corresponding to the only eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ of the system's matrix $A$ is $v=[2,\\text{ }1]^{T}.$ The vector $w=[-1,\\text{ }0]^{T}$ is such that $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$  Which of the following could represent the phase portrait of the system?\n\n*Hint: Here, the dotted line represents the equilibrium solutions.*" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308657 1072 #uuid "22d70586-a868-5b19-aa40-b97ef9910f2f" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308657 1074 "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $v,$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\lambda =0,\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}] & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2t-1 \\\\ t\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that ${x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ are equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=\\frac{1}{2}x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/5a/5afce1bca483dbd9bd462810c351d710469b744813cd9f54ea3234f0c7343d35.png)\n\nNow, we consider some general solutions of the form\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2t-1 \\\\ t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFor example:\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=2t+1 \\\\ y=t+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](images/f7/f7be54fdf4b1cbe65be9730989c06b934604bc8f0b4300bd6b616ece9de7b8f5.png)\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=-1$ and ${c}_{2}=-1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-2t-1 \\\\ y=-t-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](images/47/47c41f2d62aea217d56259bf9918b1eba5bed3141d2bcfcfd486241bc32df048.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/57/57684286e917c2dcbcb6f6a7f19a82d5603c1fe11a39cb8a6b12473cce5105f5.png)" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308658 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307650 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308658 1069 "q-334624" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308658 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308658 1071 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2x(t)-2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=2x(t)-2y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvector corresponding to the only eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ of the system's matrix $A$ is $v=[1,\\text{ }1]^{T}.$ The vector $w={[\\frac{1}{2},0]}^{T}$ is such that $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$  Which of the following could represent the phase portrait of the system?\n\n*Hint: Here, the dotted line represents the equilibrium solutions.*" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308658 1072 #uuid "aba52b16-35a3-5bbb-9f22-8bbebc7d0f24" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308658 1074 "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $v,$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n\\lambda =0,\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}] & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t+[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}t+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ t\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that ${x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ are equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/58/58564c40fcde276fb69732ce5926b7e350fe8963b51b21df0ce4de39e8481514.png)\n\nNow, we consider some general solutions of the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}t+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFor example:\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=1,$ we get the following curve: \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=t+\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ y=t+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](images/d0/d03f88d2eab48a0557f05311cd0d13d956b2af954277d322fabc4091f3aef2d4.png)\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=-1$ and ${c}_{2}=-1,$ we get the following curve: \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-t-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ y=-t-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](images/e0/e0748b246241a44132c526d1419d301ec67579c1d1cfbac0854c8bfccea67dff.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/66/66a32254dcc579eb248d8691d0c079815e29b59c650fc4b38eaa47bfb1525ddc.png)" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308659 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307907 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308659 1069 "q-334627" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308659 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308659 1071 "For which of the following step sizes is Euler's Method stable, given that $\\lambda =4$ is the parameter of the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),y(0)=1,x\\ge 0,$ and step size $Δx?$\n\n- $Δx=0.2$\n- $Δx=0.45$\n- $Δx=0.9$\n- $Δx=2.2$\n\n*Hint: The stability function for Euler's method is given by* $R(\\lambda Δx)=1-\\lambda Δx.$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308659 1072 #uuid "faa06cb1-bef4-588f-8333-1536faf16397" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308659 1074 "A numerical method ${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$is stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nFor stability, we require that the following condition holds:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(\\lambda Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |1-\\lambda Δx| & <1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this gives\n\n$$\n0<\\lambda Δx<2.\n$$\n\nThis means that Euler's method is stable when applied to the test equation for values in the region $0<\\lambda Δx<2.$\n\nIn our case, where $\\lambda =4,$ this region simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & <4Δx<2 \\\\ 0 & <Δx<\\frac{2}{4} \\\\ 0 & <Δx<0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, of the given step sizes:\n\n- $Δx=0.2$ satisfies the inequality,\n\n- $Δx=0.45$ satisfies the inequality,\n\n- $Δx=0.9$ doesn't satisfy the inequality, and\n\n- $Δx=2.2$ doesn't satisfy the inequality.\n\nTherefore, Euler’s method is stable for step sizes $Δx=0.2$ and $Δx=0.45.$\n\nThus, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308660 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307745 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308660 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308240 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308660 1069 "q-334629" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308660 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308660 1071 "Find ${e}^{At}$ for $A=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 8 \\\\ -4 & -3\\end{aligned}].$ \n\n*Hint: The eigenvalues of $A$ are $1\\pm 4\\text{i}.$*\n\n| ${e}^{At}={e}^{t}\\,\\,$ |  | $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$ | $2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |  |" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308660 1072 #uuid "29635f2f-538b-5717-bb55-2555552241c6" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308660 1074 "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}[\\begin{aligned}5 & 8 \\\\ -4 & -3\\end{aligned}]t+{\\alpha }_{0}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & 8{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ -4{\\alpha }_{1}t & -3{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues or $At$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=(1+4\\text{i})t,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=(1-4\\text{i})t,\n$$\n\nwhich are complex conjugates. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r((1+4\\text{i})t) & ={e}^{(1+4\\text{i})t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}(1+4\\text{i})t+{\\alpha }_{0} & ={e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) \\\\ ({\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0})+4{\\alpha }_{1}\\text{i}t & ={e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\text{i}\\,{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we equate the real and imaginary parts, and solve for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ 4t{\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{1}=\\frac{1}{4t}{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t))\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{4t}{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\cdot t+{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\  & ={e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) \\\\ -4{\\alpha }_{1}t & =-4\\cdot \\frac{1}{4t}{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\cdot t \\\\  & =-{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{e}^{At}={e}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) & 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308661 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308060 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308661 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308158 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308661 1069 "q-334630" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308661 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308661 1071 "Find ${e}^{At}$ for $A=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\n*Hint: The eigenvalues of $A$ are $-1\\pm 2\\text{i}.$*\n\n| ${e}^{At}={e}^{-t}\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | $2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$ | {{field-2}} |  |  |" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308661 1072 #uuid "f28e1404-987d-53df-a0ad-e0b6d8de5b42" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true]