ntial equations above. The eigenvector corresponding to the only eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ of the system's matrix $A$ is $v=[2,\\text{ }1]^{T}.$ The vector $w={[\\frac{1}{2},0]}^{T}$ is such that $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$  Which of the following could represent the phase portrait of the system?\n\n*Hint: Here, the dotted line represents the equilibrium solutions.*" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308668 1072 #uuid "f7a8620e-ad1d-5894-b095-0459bbd949b9" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308668 1074 "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $v,$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n\\lambda =0,\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}] & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t+[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2t+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ t\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that ${x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ are equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=\\frac{1}{2}x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/bf/bff5cf9d21f0b01571b577ebe3d98ef08525d13a70a1d7379b537f6ba5319613.png)\n\nNow, we consider some general solutions of the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2t+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ t\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFor example:\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=2,$ we get the following curve: \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=4t+3 \\\\ y=2t+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](images/7d/7d590ee38f7f1b6b43c9b650890f42fa31b4c15951dab8bcb003b1ac1cc45c39.png)\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=-1$ and ${c}_{2}=-2,$ we get the following curve: \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-4t-3 \\\\ y=-2t-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](images/b9/b965e3e244bc49b5e0f52840550c2ae997a0e38dc40e8757f0f51905c448d7af.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/1b/1b785127a21453e56d7d0df4f641133a44d2109008e21f8bd3057d8ac22a351f.png)" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308669 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307938 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308669 1069 "q-334644" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308669 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308669 1071 "A numerical method is used to approximate the solution to an initial value problem.\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.25,$ the observed local truncation error is $τ(0.25)=0.50.$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.10,$ the observed local truncation error is $τ(0.10)=0.01.$\n\nEstimate the order of the numerical method." 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308669 1072 #uuid "c9e60f41-0074-5df6-9afc-69a24a0bbeb5" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308669 1074 "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.25$ to $0.10.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.25}{0.10}=2.5.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the LTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p+1}=(2.5)^{p+1}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the LTE actually decreases from $0.50$ to $0.01.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.50}{0.01}=50.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the LTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2.5)^{p+1} & ≈50 \\\\ p+1 & ≈{log}_{2.5}⁡(50) \\\\ p+1 & ≈4.269… \\\\ p & ≈3.269….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is $3.$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308670 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307975 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308670 1069 "q-334645" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308670 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308670 1071 "A numerical method is used to approximate the solution to an initial value problem.\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.16,$ the observed local truncation error is $τ(0.16)=0.40.$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.04,$ the observed local truncation error is $τ(0.04)=0.005.$\n\nEstimate the order of the numerical method." 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308670 1072 #uuid "fcb96b5a-0e95-52c4-ac1f-597bc14204c1" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308670 1074 "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.16$ to $0.04.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.16}{0.04}=4.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the LTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p+1}={4}^{p+1}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the LTE actually decreases from $0.40$ to $0.005.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.40}{0.005}=80.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the LTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4}^{p+1} & ≈80 \\\\ p+1 & ≈{log}_{4}⁡(80) \\\\ p+1 & ≈3.161… \\\\ p & ≈2.161….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is $2.$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308671 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308113 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308671 1069 "q-334647" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308671 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308671 1071 "A numerical method is used to approximate the solution to an initial value problem.\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.15,$ the observed local truncation error is $τ(0.15)=0.54.$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.05,$ the observed local truncation error is $τ(0.05)=0.003.$\n\nEstimate the order of the numerical method." 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308671 1072 #uuid "b8e9fee6-366d-51d7-82a2-1629aefe0b96" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308671 1074 "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.15$ to $0.05.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.15}{0.05}=3.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the LTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p+1}={3}^{p+1}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the LTE actually decreases from $0.54$ to $0.003.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.54}{0.003}=180.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the LTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{p+1} & ≈180 \\\\ p+1 & ≈{log}_{3}⁡(180) \\\\ p+1 & ≈4.726… \\\\ p & ≈3.726….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is $4.$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308672 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308287 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308672 1069 "q-334648" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308672 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308672 1071 "A numerical method is used to approximate the solution to an initial value problem.\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.35,$ the observed local truncation error is $τ(0.35)=0.75.$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.10,$ the observed local truncation error is $τ(0.10)=0.005.$\n\nEstimate the order of the numerical method." 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308672 1072 #uuid "6b20caa4-47fd-550a-81fb-67a189c54897" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308672 1074 "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.35$ to $0.10.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.35}{0.10}=3.5.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the LTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p+1}=(3.5)^{p+1}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the LTE actually decreases from $0.75$ to $0.005.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.75}{0.005}=150.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the LTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3.5)^{p+1} & ≈150 \\\\ p+1 & ≈{log}_{3.5}⁡(150) \\\\ p+1 & ≈3.999… \\\\ p & ≈2.999….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is $3.$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308673 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308222 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308673 1069 "q-334650" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308673 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308673 1071 "A numerical method is used to approximate the solution to an initial value problem.\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.20,$ the observed local truncation error is $τ(0.20)=0.78.$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.08,$ the observed local truncation error is $τ(0.08)=0.008.$\n\nEstimate the order of the numerical method." 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308673 1072 #uuid "f92b889e-ac1a-5f7b-b100-034485795a76" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308673 1074 "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.20$ to $0.08.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.20}{0.08}=2.5.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the LTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p+1}=(2.5)^{p+1}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the LTE actually decreases from $0.78$ to $0.008.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.78}{0.008}=97.5.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the LTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2.5)^{p+1} & ≈97.5 \\\\ p+1 & ≈{log}_{2.5}⁡(97.5) \\\\ p+1 & ≈4.99… \\\\ p & ≈3.99….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is $4.$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308674 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308343 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308674 1069 "q-33467" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308674 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308674 1071 "Find the shortest distance between the point $P(4,15,-5)$ and the plane $Π$ with equation $(r-[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}])\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=0.$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308674 1072 #uuid "a8925614-d403-5814-9cbc-785e6ec5b0c2" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308674 1074 "Let's pick a point on the plane, for instance, $Q(6,9,2).$ Then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 15 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, from the equation of the plane, we get a normal vector $n=[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].$\n\nFinally, the shortest distance from the point $P$ to the plane $Π$ is the absolute value of the scalar projection of $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ onto the normal vector of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|{\\text{comp}}_{n}\\,\\overset{\\to }{PQ}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot n|}{|n|} \\\\  & =\\frac{|2\\cdot (-5)+(-6)\\cdot 1+7\\cdot (-2)|}{\\sqrt{(-5)^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|-10-6-14|}{\\sqrt{25+1+4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|-30|}{\\sqrt{30}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{30}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308675 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308119 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308675 1069 "q-334715" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308675 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308675 1071 "For which of the following values is the modified Euler method stable, given that $\\lambda $ is the parameter of the test equation?\n\n- $\\lambda Δx=0.5$\n- $\\lambda Δx=1.6$\n- $\\lambda Δx=2.2$\n- $\\lambda Δx=3.5$\n\n*Hint: The stability function $R$ of the modified Euler method is given by*\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δx)=1-\\lambda Δx+\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{2}}{2}.\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308675 1072 #uuid "331acb28-49e8-50e4-b8d3-d67c401cc67e" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308675 1074 "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda Δx)$ is referred to as the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda Δx)|<1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nThe stability function of the modified Euler method is\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δx)=1-\\lambda Δx+\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's check the stability of the modified Euler method for each of the given values in turn.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=0.5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(0.5)| & =|1-0.5+\\frac{(0.5)^{2}}{2}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{5}{8}| \\\\  & =0.625<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the modified Euler method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=1.6,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(1.6)| & =|1-1.6+\\frac{(1.6)^{2}}{2}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{17}{25}| \\\\  & =0.68<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the modified Euler method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=2.2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(2.2)| & =|1-2.2+\\frac{(2.2)^{2}}{2}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{61}{50}| \\\\  & =1.22>1.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *outside* the stability region, the modified Euler method is unstable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=3.5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(3.5)| & =|1-3.5+\\frac{(3.5)^{2}}{2}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{29}{8}| \\\\  & =3.625>1.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *outside* the stability region, the modified Euler method is unstable.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\".\n\nNote that the stability region of the Modified Euler Method is $0<\\lambda Δx<2,$ so it is not absolutely stable." 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308676 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307718 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308676 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308306 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308676 1069 "q-334729" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308676 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308676 1071 "Find ${e}^{At}$ for $A=[\\begin{aligned}6 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}].$ \n\n*Hint: The only eigenvalue of $A$ is $3,$ and it has multiplicity two.*\n\n| ${e}^{At}={e}^{3t}\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $3t$ | $1-3t$ |  |  |" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308676 1072 #uuid "79079e07-e6bc-5f6a-8ed1-2ff769b8b920" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308676 1074 "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}[\\begin{aligned}6 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]t+{\\alpha }_{0}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & -3{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ 3{\\alpha }_{1}t & {\\alpha }_{0}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The only eigenvalue of $At$ is \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=3t\n$$\n\nof multiplicity two. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}r(3t)={e}^{3t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\lambda }r(3t)={e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}3t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{3t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n- From the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{3t}.\n$$\n\n- Substituting this into the first one, we get\n\n$$\n3t{e}^{3t}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{3t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{3t}(1-3t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =6{e}^{3t}t+{e}^{3t}(1-3t) \\\\  & ={e}^{3t}(1+3t), \\\\ -3{\\alpha }_{1}t & =-3{e}^{3t}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{e}^{At}={e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}1+3t & -3t \\\\ 3t & 1-3t\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308677 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307567 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308677 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307880 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308677 1069 "q-334732" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308677 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308677 1071 "Find ${e}^{At}$ for $A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ -9 & 4\\end{aligned}].$ \n\n*Hint: The only eigenvalue of $A$ is $1,$ and it has multiplicity two.*\n\n| ${e}^{At}={e}^{t}\\,\\,$ |  | $1-3t$ | $t$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |  |" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308677 1072 #uuid "3361c13c-b482-5551-8d22-7ff3a0ff8596" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308677 1074 "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ -9 & 4\\end{aligned}]t+{\\alpha }_{0}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & {\\