ssion $τ(Δx)=5(Δx)^{4}+2(Δx)^{6},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $5(Δx)^{4}=5(Δx)^{3+1}.$\nSo, statement I corresponds to an order $3$ method.\n\n- For the GTE expression $e(Δx)=4(Δx)^{3}+7(Δx)^{4},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $4(Δx)^{3}=4(Δx)^{3}.$\nSo, statement II corresponds to an order $3$ method.\n\n- For the LTE expression $τ(Δx)=0.3(Δx)^{2}+6(Δx)^{3},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $0.3(Δx)^{2}=0.3(Δx)^{1+1}.$\nSo, statement III corresponds to an order $1$ method.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308687 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307592 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308687 1069 "q-334757" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308687 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308687 1071 "Which of the following expressions for the global truncation error ($e$) or local truncation error ($τ$) represent a fourth-order numerical method?\n\n- $e(Δx)=5(Δx)^{4}-3(Δx)^{6}$\n- $e(Δx)=2(Δx)^{3}+7(Δx)^{4}$\n- $τ(Δx)=0.4(Δx)^{5}+(Δx)^{7}$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308687 1072 #uuid "3b0cbcd0-f13f-5586-9c22-f5d860877a09" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308687 1074 "For a given numerical method, if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that\n\n- the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0,\n$$\n\n- the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0,\n$$\n\nthen the method is of order $p.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option in turn:\n\n- For the GTE expression $e(Δx)=5(Δx)^{4}-3(Δx)^{6},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $5(Δx)^{4}=5(Δx)^{4}.$ So, statement I corresponds to an order $4$ method.\n\n- For the GTE expression $e(Δx)=2(Δx)^{3}+7(Δx)^{4},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $2(Δx)^{3}=2(Δx)^{3}.$ So, statement II corresponds to an order $3$ method.\n\n- For the LTE expression $τ(Δx)=0.4(Δx)^{5}+(Δx)^{7},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $0.4(Δx)^{5}=0.4(Δx)^{4+1}.$ So, statement III corresponds to an order $4$ method.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"." 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308688 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307863 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308688 1069 "q-334761" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308688 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308688 1071 "Which of the following expressions for the global truncation error ($e$) or local truncation error ($τ$) represent a third-order numerical method?\n\n- $e(Δx)=6(Δx)^{3}+4(Δx)^{4}$\n- $τ(Δx)=0.5(Δx)^{4}+3(Δx)^{7}$\n- $e(Δx)=2(Δx)^{2}+(Δx)^{5}$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308688 1072 #uuid "a147217d-4ada-5afd-bf77-eb76079d5271" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308688 1074 "For a given numerical method, if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that\n\n- the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0,\n$$\n\n- the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0,\n$$\n\nthen the method is of order $p.$\n\nWith that in mind, let’s check each option:\n\n- For $e(Δx)=6(Δx)^{3}+4(Δx)^{4},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $6(Δx)^{3}=6(Δx)^{3}.$\nSo, statement I corresponds to an order $3$ method.\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=0.5(Δx)^{4}+3(Δx)^{7},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $0.5(Δx)^{4}=0.5(Δx)^{3+1}.$\nSo, statement II corresponds to an order $3$ method.\n\n- For $e(Δx)=2(Δx)^{2}+(Δx)^{5},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $2(Δx)^{2}=2(Δx)^{2}.$\nSo, statement III corresponds to an order $2$ method.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308689 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308188 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308689 1069 "q-334763" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308689 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308689 1071 "A numerical method for approximating a solution to an initial value problem is known to be order $3.$  Suppose that using step size $Δx=0.2$ produces a global truncation error (GTE) of $e(0.2)≈0.064.$  If we instead use a step size $Δx=0.1,$ what GTE should we expect?\n\n$e(0.1)≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308689 1072 #uuid "e11ea327-0283-5223-89c9-1dcc1d605dba" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308689 1074 "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the GTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.2$ to $0.1.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.2}{0.1}=2.\n$$\n\nNow, since the numerical method is of order $3,$ the error is expected to decrease by a factor of\n\n$$\n{k}^{3}={2}^{3}=8.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using a step size of $Δx=0.1,$ the expected GTE is\n\n$$\ne(0.1)≈\\frac{0.064}{8}=0.008.\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308690 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307661 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308690 1069 "q-334766" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308690 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308690 1071 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=6x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=6y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives the general solution to the system of ODEs above?\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{6t} & t{e}^{6t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{6t}\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}].$*" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308690 1072 #uuid "c150290d-e2c6-53a1-8ef9-89cbb210da93" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308690 1074 "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}].$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the general solution of the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)=Φ(t)\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix of the system, and $c$ is a constant vector.\n\nNow, using the given information, the standard fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{6t} & t{e}^{6t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{6t}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,c \\\\  & ={e}^{At}\\,c \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{6t} & t{e}^{6t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{6t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{6t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}t{e}^{6t} \\\\ {e}^{6t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{6t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{6t},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308691 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308055 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308691 1069 "q-334767" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308691 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308691 1071 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-5x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-5y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives the general solution to the system of ODEs above?\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-5t} & t{e}^{-5t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-5t}\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}].$*" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308691 1072 #uuid "1af63baf-333c-5222-a3cc-2c3c736daedf" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308691 1074 "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}].$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the general solution of the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)=Φ(t)\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix of the system, and $c$ is a constant vector.\n\nNow, using the given information, the standard fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-5t} & t{e}^{-5t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-5t}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,c \\\\  & ={e}^{At}\\,c \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-5t} & t{e}^{-5t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-5t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-5t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}t{e}^{-5t} \\\\ {e}^{-5t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-5t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-5t},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308692 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308098 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308692 1069 "q-334781" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308692 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308692 1071 "A numerical method for approximating a solution to an initial value problem is known to be order $4.$\nSuppose that using step size $Δx=0.1$ produces a global truncation error (GTE) of $e(0.1)≈0.512.$\nIf we instead use a step size $Δx=0.025,$ what GTE should we expect?\n\n$e(0.025)≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308692 1072 #uuid "5e9fb99c-0502-586a-b64c-76d5a0146e7d" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308692 1074 "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the GTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.1$ to $0.025.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.1}{0.025}=4.\n$$\n\nNow, since the numerical method is of order $4,$ the error is expected to decrease by a factor of\n\n$$\n{k}^{4}={4}^{4}=256.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using a step size of $Δx=0.025,$ the expected GTE is\n\n$$\ne(0.025)≈\\frac{0.512}{256}=0.002.\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308693 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308155 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308693 1069 "q-334796" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308693 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308693 1071 "A numerical method for approximating a solution to an initial value problem is known to be order $2.$  Suppose that using step size $Δx=0.15$ produces a global truncation error (GTE) of $e(0.15)≈0.0225.$  If we instead use a step size $Δx=0.10,$ what GTE should we expect?\n\n$e(0.10)≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308693 1072 #uuid "d047c5c7-9757-5ee5-9e01-a79118905089" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308693 1074 "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the GTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.15$ to $0.10.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.15}{0.10}=1.5.\n$$\n\nNow, since the numerical method is of order $2,$ the error is expected to decrease by a factor of\n\n$$\n{k}^{2}=(1.5)^{2}=2.25.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using a step size of $Δx=0.10,$ the expected GTE is\n\n$$\ne(0.10)≈\\frac{0.0225}{2.25}=0.01.\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308694 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307778 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308694 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308160 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308694 1069 "q-334824" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308694 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308694 1071 "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]x+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}],\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWrite down the expression that gives the solution of the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3t} & t{e}^{3t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].$*\n\n| $x(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  | $\\,\\,+{\\int }_{0}^{t}\\,\\,$ |  | $(t-s){e}^{3t}$ |  | $\\,\\,\\text{d}s$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $0$ |  |  | {{field-2}} |  |  |  |  |" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308694 1072 #uuid "001f1d7b-e465-5a31-aa71-fce5efbdd939" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308694 1074 "Given the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as the sum of the homogeneous part and a particular solution:\n\n$$\nx(t)={{{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{homogeneous part}}+{{{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s}_{⏟}}_{\\text{particular solution}},\n$$\n\nwhere $x({t}_{0})$ is the initial condition vector. \n\nLet's evaluate each part separately:\n\n- First, we find the homogeneous part. We are given that ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3t} & t{e}^{3t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}].$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At}\\,x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3t} & t{e}^{3t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Notice that \n\n$$\n{e}^{A(t-s)}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3(t-s)} & (t-s){e}^{3(t-s)} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{3(t-s)}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s) & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3(t-s)} & (t-s){e}^{3(t-s)} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{3(t-s)}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{3s}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(t-s){e}^{3t} \\\\ {e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{\\int }_{0}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}(t-s){e}^{3t} \\\\ {e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}]\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308695 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308228 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308695 1069 "q-334827" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308695 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308695 1071 "A numerical method for approximating a solution to an initial value problem is known to be order $2.$  Suppose that using step size $Δx=0.18$ produces a global truncation error (GTE) of $e(0.18)≈0.2025.$  If we instead use a step size $Δx=0.04,$ what GTE should we expect?\n\n$e(0.04)≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308695 1072 #uuid "349cbec4-4185-5bf7-8486-3d5b581851b8" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308695 1074 "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the GTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.18$ to $0.04.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.18}{0.04}=4.5.\n$$\n\nNow, since the numerical method is of order $2,$ the error is expected to decrease by a factor of\n\n$$\n{k}^{2}=(4.5)^{2}=20.25.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using a step size of $Δx=0.04,$ the expected GTE is\n\n$$\ne(0.04)≈\\frac{0.2025}{20.25}=0.01.\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308696 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307875 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308696 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308270 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308696 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308329 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308696 1069 "q-334832" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308696 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308696 1071 "Suppose that $f(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty )$, has exponential order $a,$ and its Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}\n$$\n\nexists for $s>a.$ Then, $F(s)$ is differentiable at every point $s>a,$ and we have\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(s)=-\\,L{t\\cdot f(t)}.\n$$\n\nA proof of this statement is given below. \n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ $F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$ exists for $s>a.$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${F}^{′}(s)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ ${F}^{′}(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{∂}{∂s}({e}^{-st}f(t))\\,\\text{d}t$\n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ $\\frac{∂}{∂s}({e}^{-st}f(t))=-t{e}^{-st}f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ ${F}^{′}(s)=-{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }t{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$\n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ ${F}^{′}(s)=-L{t\\cdot f(t)}$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ The integral in line $\\text{L1}$ converges since\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L2}$ follows from line $\\text{L1}$ and\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L3}$ follows from $\\text{L2}$ and {{field-3}}." 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308696 1072 #uuid "88677c48-4d7b-5ae1-9439-ac753b0c1c9f" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308696 1074 "If a function $f(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty )$ and has exponential order $a,$ then its Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}\n$$\n\nexists for every real $s>a,$ and $F$ is infinitely differentiable in this region of convergence. \n\nThe smoothness property states that, for each integer $n\\ge 0$ and $s>a,$ we have\n\n$$\n{F}^{(n)}(s)=(-1)^{n}\\,L{{t}^{n}f(t)}.\n$$\n\nWe're asked to prove the smoothness property in the case $n=1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- We first consider line $\\text{L1}.$ Since $f\\text{ is of exponential order and is piecewise continuous}$, \nthere exist constants $K,T>0$ and $a$ such that\n\n$$\n|f(t)|\\le K{e}^{at},\\,t>T.\n$$\n\nWhen computing the Laplace transform, we multiply $f(t)$ by ${e}^{-st}.$ If $s>a$, then for all sufficiently large $t$,\n\n$$\n|{e}^{-st}f(t)|\\le K{e}^{-(s-a)t}.\n$$\n\nBecause $s-a>0$, the function ${e}^{-(s-a)t}$ is integrable on $[T,\\infty )$, and\n$f$ is bounded on $[0,T].$\nTherefore, the entire integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt\n$$\n\nconverges. This justifies the expression in line $\\text{L1}.$\n\n- We now consider line $\\text{L2}.$ From line $\\text{L1}$ we know that\n\n$$\nF(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt.\n$$\n\nBy $\\text{the definition of the derivative with respect to }s$, we get\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(s)=\\frac{d}{ds}F(s)=\\frac{d}{ds}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt,\n$$\n\nwhich is exactly the statement in line $\\text{L2}.$\n\n- Finally, we consider line $\\text{L3}.$ From $\\text{L2}$ we have\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(s)=\\frac{d}{ds}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt.\n$$\n\nSince $f$ is of exponential order, the integral converges, and we can\ndifferentiate under the integral sign:\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{∂}{∂s}({e}^{-st}f(t))\\,dt.\n$$\n\nTherefore, line $\\text{L3}$ follows from $\\text{L2}$ and $\\text{differentiating under the integral sign}.$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308697 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307840 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308697 1069 "q-334844" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308697 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308697 1071 "Suppose $f(x)={x}^{2}+3{x}^{4}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f=O({x}^{2})$ as $x\\to 0$\n- $f=O({x}^{3})$ as $x\\to 0$\n- $f=O({x}^{4})$ as $x\\to 0$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308697 1072 #uuid "e6c60236-078a-5017-8be7-a68ef13f931d" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308697 1074 "Suppose we wish to test whether the following statement is true:\n\n$$\nf(x)=O(g(x))\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is either finite or $+\\infty .$ To do this, we can examine the following limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}\n$$\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- If the limit is *finite*, the statement is true.\n\n- If the limit is *infinite*, the statement is false.\n\n- If the limit does not exist, the test is inconclusive.\n\nHere, we must consider the function\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{2}+3{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Let $g(x)={x}^{2}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}+3{x}^{4}}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(1+3{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =1+0 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the limit is finite, and $f=O({x}^{2})$ as $x\\to 0.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let $g(x)={x}^{3}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}+3{x}^{4}}{{x}^{3}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{1}{x}+3x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs $x\\to 0,$ the term $\\frac{1}{x}$ becomes arbitrarily large in magnitude, while $3x\\to 0.$\nSo\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{1}{x}+3x)=\\infty .\n$$\n\nHence, the limit is infinite, and $f\\ne O({x}^{3})$ as $x\\to 0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Let $g(x)={x}^{4}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}+3{x}^{3}}{{x}^{4}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs $x\\to 0,$ the term $\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}$ becomes arbitrarily large in magnitude, so\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}+3)=\\infty .\n$$\n\nHence, the limit is infinite, and $f\\ne O({x}^{4})$ as $x\\to 0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308698 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307625 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308698 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307991 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308698 1069 "q-334848" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308698 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308698 1071 "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]x+[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{5t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,x(1)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWrite down the expression that gives the solution of the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{5t} & t{e}^{5t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}].$*\n\n| $x(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  | $\\,\\,+{\\int }_{1}^{t}\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  | $\\,\\,\\text{d}s$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $0$ |  |  | $0$ |  |  |  |  |" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308698 1072 #uuid "cc8319e7-c74a-546c-ac46-138a893fec53" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308698 1074 "Given the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as the sum of the homogeneous part and a particular solution:\n\n$$\nx(t)={{{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{homogeneous part}}+{{{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s}_{⏟}}_{\\text{particular solution}},\n$$\n\nwhere $x({t}_{0})$ is the initial condition vector. \n\nLet's evaluate each part separately:\n\n- First, we find the homogeneous part. We are given that ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{5t} & t{e}^{5t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}].$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-1)}\\,x(1) & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{5(t-1)} & (t-1){e}^{5(t-1)} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{5(t-1)}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{5(t-1)} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Notice that \n\n$$\n{e}^{A(t-s)}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{5(t-s)} & (t-s){e}^{5(t-s)} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{5(t-s)}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s) & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{5(t-s)} & (t-s){e}^{5(t-s)} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{5(t-s)}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{e}^{5s} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{5t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{5(t-1)} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{\\int }_{1}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{5t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308699 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307782 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308699 1069 "q-334851" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308699 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308699 1071 "Suppose $f(x)=3{x}^{5}-2x.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f=O(1)$ as $x\\to 0$\n- $f=O({x}^{2})$ as $x\\to 0$\n- $f=O({x}^{3})$ as $x\\to 0$" 13194