x=0.1,$ what LTE should we expect?\n\n$τ(0.1)≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308705 1072 #uuid "ce506036-afe4-59f4-88ca-ada7f672db57" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308705 1074 "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.3$ to $0.1.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.3}{0.1}=3.\n$$\n\nNow, since the numerical method is of order $3,$ the error is expected to decrease by a factor of\n\n$$\n{k}^{3+1}={3}^{4}=81.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using a step size of $Δx=0.1,$ the expected LTE is\n\n$$\nτ(0.1)≈\\frac{0.162}{81}=0.002.\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308706 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307712 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308706 1069 "q-334867" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308706 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308706 1071 "Suppose $f(x)=-2{x}^{2}+{e}^{x}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f=O(1)$ as $x\\to 0$\n- $f=O(x)$ as $x\\to 0$\n- $f=O({x}^{2})$ as $x\\to 0$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308706 1072 #uuid "97b1feee-658e-5bb1-8630-22a1f9c057aa" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308706 1074 "Suppose we wish to test whether the following statement is true:\n\n$$\nf(x)=O(g(x))\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\nTo do this, we can examine the limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}.\n$$\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- If the limit is *finite*, the statement is true.\n\n- If the limit is *infinite*, the statement is false.\n\n- If the limit does not exist, the test is inconclusive.\n\nHere, we must consider the function\n\n$$\nf(x)=-2{x}^{2}+{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Let $g(x)=1.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(-2{x}^{2}+{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f=O(1)$ as $x\\to 0.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let $g(x)=x.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(-2x+\\frac{{e}^{x}}{x}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNear $x=0,$ we have ${e}^{x}≈1,$ so\n\n$$\n\\frac{{e}^{x}}{x}≈\\frac{1}{x},\n$$\n\nwhich becomes arbitrarily large in magnitude as $x\\to 0.$\nThus,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}(-2x+\\frac{{e}^{x}}{x})=\\infty .\n$$\n\nHence, $f\\ne O(x)$ as $x\\to 0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Let $g(x)={x}^{2}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(-2+\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{x}^{2}}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{x}≈1$ near $x=0,$\n\n$$\n\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{x}^{2}}≈\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich becomes arbitrarily large in magnitude as $x\\to 0.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}(-2+\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{x}^{2}})=\\infty .\n$$\n\nThus, $f\\ne O({x}^{2})$ as $x\\to 0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308707 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307617 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308707 1069 "q-334870" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308707 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308707 1071 "Suppose $f(x)=-5{x}^{6}+9{x}^{4}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f=O(1)$ as $x\\to \\infty $\n- $f=O({x}^{8})$ as $x\\to \\infty $\n- $f=O({x}^{5})$ as $x\\to \\infty $" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308707 1072 #uuid "a21bb853-c833-53c0-be43-6d5a120a6d45" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308707 1074 "Suppose we wish to test whether the following statement is true:\n\n$$\nf(x)=O(g(x))\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\nTo do this, we can examine the limit\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}.\n$$\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- If the limit is *finite*, the statement is true.\n\n- If the limit is *infinite*, the statement is false.\n\n- If the limit does not exist, the test is inconclusive.\n\nHere, we must consider the polynomial\n\n$$\nf(x)=-5{x}^{6}+9{x}^{4}.\n$$\n\nNow, let us analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Let $g(x)=1.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(-5{x}^{6}+9{x}^{4}) \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f\\ne O(1)$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\n- Statement II is true. Let $g(x)={x}^{8}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{-5{x}^{6}+9{x}^{4}}{{x}^{8}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(-\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{9}{{x}^{4}}) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f=O({x}^{8})$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\n- Statement III is false. Let $g(x)={x}^{5}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{-5{x}^{6}+9{x}^{4}}{{x}^{5}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(-5x+\\frac{9}{{x}^{4}}) \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f\\ne O({x}^{5})$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308708 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307623 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308708 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308035 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308708 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308285 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308708 1069 "q-334876" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308708 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308708 1071 "Suppose that $f(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty )$, has exponential order $a,$ and its Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}\n$$\n\nexists for $s>a.$ Then, $F(s)$ is twice differentiable at every point $s>a,$ and we have\n\n$$\n{F}^{″}(s)=L{{t}^{2}\\cdot f(t)}.\n$$\n\nA proof of this statement is given below. \n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ $F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt$ exists for $s>a.$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${F}^{″}(s)=\\frac{{d}^{2}}{d{s}^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt$\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ ${F}^{″}(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂{s}^{2}}({e}^{-st}f(t))\\,dt$\n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ $\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂{s}^{2}}({e}^{-st}f(t))={t}^{2}{e}^{-st}f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ ${F}^{″}(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{t}^{2}{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt$\n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ ${F}^{″}(s)=L{{t}^{2}\\cdot f(t)}$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ The integral in line $\\text{L1}$ converges since\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L3}$ follows from line $\\text{L2}$ and\n{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L5}$ follows from lines $\\text{L3}$ and $\\text{L4}$ by\n{{field-3}}." 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308708 1072 #uuid "96f868d1-0437-58d3-9b50-b0097405beb2" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308708 1074 "If a function $f(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty )$ and has exponential order $a,$ then its Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}\n$$\n\nexists for every real $s>a,$ and $F$ is infinitely differentiable in this region of convergence. \n\nThe smoothness property states that, for each integer $n\\ge 0$ and $s>a,$ we have\n\n$$\n{F}^{(n)}(s)=(-1)^{n}\\,L{{t}^{n}f(t)}.\n$$\n\nWe're asked to prove the smoothness property in the case $n=2.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- We first consider line $\\text{L1}.$ Since $f\\text{ is of exponential order and is piecewise continuous}$, \nthere exist constants $K,T>0$ and $a$ such that\n\n$$\n|f(t)|\\le K{e}^{at},\\,t>T.\n$$\n\nWhen computing the Laplace transform, we multiply $f(t)$ by ${e}^{-st}.$ If $s>a$, then for all sufficiently large $t$,\n\n$$\n|{e}^{-st}f(t)|\\le K{e}^{-(s-a)t}.\n$$\n\nBecause $s-a>0$, the function ${e}^{-(s-a)t}$ is integrable on $[T,\\infty )$, and\n$f$ is bounded on $[0,T].$\nTherefore, the entire integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt\n$$\n\nconverges. This justifies the expression in line $\\text{L1}.$\n\n- Next we consider line $\\text{L3}.$ From $\\text{L2}$ we have\n\n$$\n{F}^{″}(s)=\\frac{{d}^{2}}{d{s}^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt.\n$$\n\nSince $f$ is of exponential order, the integral converges, and we can\ndifferentiate under the integral sign:\n\n$$\n{F}^{″}(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂{s}^{2}}({e}^{-st}f(t))\\,dt.\n$$\n\nTherefore, line $\\text{L3}$ follows from $\\text{L2}$ and $\\text{differentiating under the integral sign}.$\n\n- We now consider line $\\text{L5}.$ From line $\\text{L3}$ we have\n\n$$\n{F}^{″}(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂{s}^{2}}({e}^{-st}f(t))\\,dt.\n$$\n\nLine $\\text{L4}$ tells us that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂{s}^{2}}({e}^{-st}f(t))={t}^{2}{e}^{-st}f(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this expression into the integrand in line $\\text{L3}$ gives\n\n$$\n{F}^{″}(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{t}^{2}{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt.\n$$\n\nTherefore, line $\\text{L5}$ follows from lines $\\text{L3}$ and $\\text{L4}$ by $\\text{substitution}.$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308709 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308074 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308709 1069 "q-334881" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308709 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308709 1071 "Let $f(t)$ be a piecewise continuous function on $[0,\\infty )$ with exponential order $a,$ and consider its Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)},\\,s>a.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- If $f(t)$ is of exponential order $a,$ then the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt$ converges for every $s>a.$\n- If $f(t)$ is of exponential order $a,$ then its Laplace transform $F(s)$ exists for all $s>a.$\n- The Laplace transform of any piecewise continuous function of exponential order is infinitely differentiable in its region of convergence." 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308709 1072 #uuid "448418f7-1bbb-5414-b0a2-b045e5f85194" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308709 1074 "If a function $f(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty )$ and has exponential order $a,$ then its Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}\n$$\n\nexists for every real $s>a,$ and $F$ is infinitely differentiable in this region of convergence.\n\nThe smoothness property states that, for each integer $n\\ge 0$ and every $s>a,$\n\n$$\n{F}^{(n)}(s)=(-1)^{n}\\,L{{t}^{n}f(t)}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $f$ is of exponential order and is piecewise continuous, there exist constants $K,T>0$ and $a$ such that\n\n$$\n|f(t)|\\le K{e}^{at},\\,t>T.\n$$\n\nWhen computing the Laplace transform, we multiply $f(t)$ by ${e}^{-st}.$ If $s>a$, then for all sufficiently large $t$,\n\n$$\n|{e}^{-st}f(t)|\\le K{e}^{-(s-a)t}.\n$$\n\nBecause $s-a>0$, the function ${e}^{-(s-a)t}$ is integrable on $[T,\\infty )$, and\n$f$ is bounded on $[0,T].$\nTherefore, the entire integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt\n$$\n\nconverges.\n\n- Statement II is true. The standard existence theorem for the Laplace transform says that if $f$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty )$ and of exponential order $a,$ then the improper integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt\n$$\n\nconverges for every $s>a.$ This is exactly what it means for $F(s)$ to exist for all $s>a.$\n\n- Statement III is true. The smoothness theorem guarantees\n\n$$\n{F}^{(n)}(s)=(-1)^{n}\\,L{{t}^{n}f(t)}\n$$\n\nfor every $n\\ge 0$ and each $s>a,$ which shows that $F(s)$ is infinitely differentiable, even if $f(t)$ itself is not.\n\nTherefore, the answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308710 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308159 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308710 1069 "q-33491" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308710 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308710 1071 "Which of the following gives the equation of the tangent to the curve $r(t)={e}^{t}\\,i+3t\\,j+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,k$ at the point corresponding to $t=\\pi ,$  valid for all  real values of $u?$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308710 1072 #uuid "0f51e43b-185c-528f-ad05-7f902b9373ab" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308710 1074 "At the point where $t=\\pi $,  the vector ${r}^{′}(\\pi )$ is tangent to the curve, so the tangent line to the curve at $P$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nR(u)=r(\\pi )+u{r}^{′}(\\pi ),\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we find $r(\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(\\pi ) & ={e}^{\\pi }\\,i+3(\\pi )\\,j+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2(\\pi )\\,k \\\\  & ={e}^{\\pi }\\,i+3\\pi \\,j+\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)\\,k \\\\  & ={e}^{t}\\,i+3\\,j-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we evaluate ${r}^{′}(t)$ at $t=\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(\\pi ) & ={e}^{(\\pi )}\\,i+3\\,j+\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2(\\pi )k \\\\  & ={e}^{\\pi }\\,i+3\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nFinally, the tangent line to the given curve at the point \n where $t=\\pi $ is \n\n$$\nR(u)={e}^{\\pi }\\,i+3\\pi \\,j+\\,k+u({e}^{\\pi }\\,i+3\\,j),\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308711 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307576 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308711 1069 "q-334931" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308711 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308711 1071 "Given that $I(t)={\\int }_{0}^{2}(x-4{t}^{3})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x,$ calculate ${I}^{′}(t)$ for $t\\in R$ by differentiating under the integral sign.\n\n${I}^{′}(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308711 1072 #uuid "932e788a-5d6d-5848-ade5-e4094868063b" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308711 1074 "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWe wish to compute ${I}^{′}(t),$ where\n\n$$\nI(t)={\\int }_{0}^{2}(x-4{t}^{3})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating under the integral sign, we get\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(x-4{t}^{3})^{2}]\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNext, let's compute the partial derivative with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(x-4{t}^{3})^{2}] & =2(x-4{t}^{3})\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂t}(x-4{t}^{3}) \\\\  & =2(x-4{t}^{3})\\cdot (-12{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =-24{t}^{2}(x-4{t}^{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this back into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{′}(t) & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}-24{t}^{2}(x-4{t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-24{t}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(x-4{t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's evaluate the remaining integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(x-4{t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{0}^{2}4{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}{|}_{0}^{2}-4{t}^{3}x{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =2-8{t}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{′}(t) & =-24{t}^{2}(2-8{t}^{3}) \\\\  & =48{t}^{2}(4{t}^{3}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308712 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308225 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308712 1069 "q-334935" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308712 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308712 1071 "Given that $K(t)={\\int }_{1}^{2}({x}^{2}+{t}^{3})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x,$ calculate ${K}^{′}(t)$ for $t\\in R$ by differentiating under the integral sign.\n\n${K}^{′}(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308712 1072 #uuid "b4de1f09-9a29-5871-b400-9e62eb35e8bc" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308712 1074 "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWe wish to compute ${K}^{′}(t),$ where\n\n$$\nK(t)={\\int }_{1}^{2}({x}^{2}+{t}^{3})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating under the integral sign, we get\n\n$$\n{K}^{′}(t)={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[({x}^{2}+{t}^{3})^{2}]\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNext, let's compute the partial derivative with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[({x}^{2}+{t}^{3})^{2}] & =2({x}^{2}+{t}^{3})\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂t}({x}^{2}+{t}^{3}) \\\\  & =2({x}^{2}+{t}^{3})\\cdot 3{t}^{2} \\\\  & =6{t}^{2}({x}^{2}+{t}^{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this back into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{K}^{′}(t) & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}6{t}^{2}({x}^{2}+{t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6{t}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}({x}^{2}+{t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's evaluate the remaining integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}({x}^{2}+{t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{2}{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}{|}_{1}^{2}+{t}^{3}x{|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{3}+{t}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{K}^{′}(t) & =6{t}^{2}(\\frac{7}{3}+{t}^{3}) \\\\  & =2{t}^{2}(3{t}^{3}+7).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308713 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307952 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308713 1069 "q-334937" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308713 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308713 1071 "Given that $Q(t)={\\int }_{-1}^{1}({x}^{2}+x+{t}^{3})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x,$ calculate ${Q}^{′}(t)$ for $t\\in R$ by differentiating under the integral sign.\n\n${Q}^{′}(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308713 1072 #uuid "8ff8df8a-0811-5725-920c-fa5dffc48561" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308713 1074 "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWe wish to compute ${Q}^{′}(t),$ where\n\n$$\nQ(t)={\\int }_{-1}^{1}({x}^{2}+x+{t}^{3})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating under the integral sign, we get\n\n$$\n{Q}^{′}(t)={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[({x}^{2}+x+{t}^{3})^{2}]\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNext, let's compute the partial derivative with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[({x}^{2}+x+{t}^{3})^{2}] & =2({x}^{2}+x+{t}^{3})\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂t}({x}^{2}+x+{t}^{3}) \\\\  & =2({x}^{2}+x+{t}^{3})\\cdot 3{t}^{2} \\\\  & =6{t}^{2}({x}^{2}+x+{t}^{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this back into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}^{′}(t) & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}6{t}^{2}({x}^{2}+x+{t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6{t}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}({x}^{2}+x+{t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's evaluate the remaining integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}({x}^{2}+x+{t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{-1}^{1}x\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}{|}_{-1}^{1}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}{|}_{-1}^{1}+{t}^{3}x{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}+0+2{t}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}+2{t}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}^{′}(t) & =6{t}^{2}(\\frac{2}{3}+2{t}^{3}) \\\\  & =4{t}^{2}(3{t}^{3}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308714 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308040 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308714 1069 "q-334939" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308714 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308714 1071 "Given that $I(t)={\\int }_{-1}^{0}(3{x}^{3}+{t}^{2})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x,$ calculate ${I}^{′}(t)$ for $t\\in R$ by differentiating under the integral sign.\n\n${I}^{′}(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308714 1072 #uuid "acaa4683-e9fb-5e55-9654-30631f619f8c" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308714 1074 "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWe wish to compute ${I}^{′}(t),$ where\n\n$$\nI(t)={\\int }_{-1}^{0}(3{x}^{3}+{t}^{2})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating under the integral sign, we get\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}(t)={\\int }_{-1}^{0}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(3{x}^{3}+{t}^{2})^{2}]\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNext, let's compute the partial derivative with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(3{x}^{3}+{t}^{2})^{2}] & =2(3{x}^{3}+{t}^{2})\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂t}(3{x}^{3}+{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =2(3{x}^{3}+{t}^{2})\\cdot 2t \\\\  & =4t(3{x}^{3}+{t}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this back into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{′}(t) & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}4t(3{x}^{3}+{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4t{\\int }_{-1}^{0}(3{x}^{3}+{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's evaluate the remaining integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}(3{x}^{3}+{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}3{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{4}}{4}{|}_{-1}^{0}+{t}^{2}x{|}_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}+{t}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{′}(t) & =4t({t}^{2}-\\frac{3}{4}) \\\\  & =t(4{t}^{2}-3).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308715 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308173 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308715 1069 "q-334946" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308715 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308715 1071 "A numerical method for approximating a solution to an initial value problem is known to be order $1.$  Suppose that using step size $Δx=0.5$ produces a local truncation error (LTE) of $τ(0.5)≈0.2.$  If we instead use a step size $Δx=0.25,$ what LTE should we expect?\n\n$τ(0.25)≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308715 1072 #uuid "6a7b9303-06ef-5983-bd7d-577c4a789987" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308715 1074 "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.5$ to $0.25.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.5}{0.25}=2.\n$$\n\nNow, since the numerical method is of order $1,$ the error is expected to decrease by a factor of\n\n$$\n{k}^{1+1}={2}^{2}=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using a step size of $Δx=0.25,$ the expected LTE is\n\n$$\nτ(0.25)≈\\frac{0.2}{4}=0.05.\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308716 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308072 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308716 1069 "q-334947" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308716 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308716 1071 "A numerical method for approximating a solution to an initial value problem is known to be order $4.$  Suppose that using step size $Δx=0.4$ produces a local truncation error (LTE) of $τ(0.4)≈1.024.$  If we instead use a step size $Δx=0.1,$ what LTE should we expect?\n\n$τ(0.1)≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308716 1072 #uuid "cfc83ddc-db4f-5e46-adbe-84eb415a3d1c" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308716 1074 "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.4$ to $0.1.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.4}{0.1}=4.\n$$\n\nNow, since the numerical method is of order $4,$ the error is expected to decrease by a factor of\n\n$$\n{k}^{4+1}={4}^{5}=1024.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using a step size of $Δx=0.1,$ the expected LTE is\n\n$$\nτ(0.1)≈\\frac{1.024}{1024}=0.001.\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308717 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307954 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308717 1069 "q-334948" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308717 1070 #edb/ref 1759218