K}^{′}(t),$ where\n\n$$\nK(t)={\\int }_{-2}^{3}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-2x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating under the integral sign, we get\n\n$$\n{K}^{′}(t)={\\int }_{-2}^{3}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-2x)^{2}]\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNext, let's compute the partial derivative with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-2x)^{2}] & =2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-2x)\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-2x) \\\\  & =2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-2x)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)) \\\\  & =-2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-2x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this back into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{K}^{′}(t) & ={\\int }_{-2}^{3}-2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-2x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t){\\int }_{-2}^{3}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-2x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's evaluate the remaining integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{3}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-2x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-2}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{-2}^{3}2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))\\,x{|}_{-2}^{3}-{x}^{2}{|}_{-2}^{3} \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{K}^{′}(t) & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\cdot (5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-5) \\\\  & =10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308720 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307686 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308720 1069 "q-334972" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308720 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308720 1071 "Given that $K(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+{e}^{t})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x,$ calculate ${K}^{′}(t)$ for $t\\in R$ by differentiating under the integral sign.\n\n${K}^{′}(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308720 1072 #uuid "d88174d2-4a3f-56d7-bbbd-4ef7d760dcb4" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308720 1074 "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWe wish to compute ${K}^{′}(t),$ where\n\n$$\nK(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+{e}^{t})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating under the integral sign, we get\n\n$$\n{K}^{′}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(2x+{e}^{t})^{2}]\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNext, let's compute the partial derivative with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(2x+{e}^{t})^{2}] & =2(2x+{e}^{t})\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂t}(2x+{e}^{t}) \\\\  & =2(2x+{e}^{t})\\cdot {e}^{t} \\\\  & =2{e}^{t}(2x+{e}^{t})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this back into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{K}^{′}(t) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2{e}^{t}(2x+{e}^{t})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{e}^{t}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+{e}^{t})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's evaluate the remaining integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+{e}^{t})\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}{|}_{0}^{1}+{e}^{t}x{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =1+{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{K}^{′}(t)=2{e}^{t}(1+{e}^{t}).\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308721 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307929 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308721 1069 "q-335019" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308721 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308721 1071 "Suppose $f(x)=3{x}^{2}-x.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f=O(1)$ as $x\\to \\infty $\n- $f=O(x{e}^{x})$ as $x\\to \\infty $\n- $f=O({x}^{3})$ as $x\\to \\infty $" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308721 1072 #uuid "28363017-b95e-52d8-9173-1894ab5a3d68" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308721 1074 "Suppose we wish to test whether the following statement is true:\n\n$$\nf(x)=O(g(x))\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\nTo do this, we can examine the limit\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}.\n$$\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- If the limit is *finite*, the statement is true.\n\n- If the limit is *infinite*, the statement is false.\n\n- If the limit does not exist, the test is inconclusive.\n\nHere, we must consider the polynomial\n\n$$\nf(x)=3{x}^{2}-x.\n$$\n\nNow, let us analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Let $g(x)=1.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(3{x}^{2}-x) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }x(3x-1) \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f\\ne O(1)$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\n- Statement II is true. Let $g(x)=x{e}^{x}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{3{x}^{2}-x}{x{e}^{x}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x(3x-1)}{x{e}^{x}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{3x-1}{{e}^{x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince now direct evaluation leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty },$ we can evaluate the limit using L'Hopital's rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{3x-1}{{e}^{x}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(3x-1)^{′}}{({e}^{x})^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{3}{{e}^{x}} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f=O(x{e}^{x})$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\n- Statement III is true. Let $g(x)={x}^{3}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{3{x}^{2}-x}{{x}^{3}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{3}{x}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f=O({x}^{8})$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308722 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308174 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308722 1069 "q-335020" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308722 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308722 1071 "Suppose $f(x)=2{x}^{4}+5{x}^{2}+10.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f=O({x}^{5})$ as $x\\to \\infty $\n- $f=O({x}^{2})$ as $x\\to \\infty $\n- $f=O(1)$ as $x\\to \\infty $" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308722 1072 #uuid "0e125563-3523-5218-87f9-08cecc055fc9" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308722 1074 "Suppose we wish to test whether the following statement is true:\n\n$$\nf(x)=O(g(x))\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\nTo do this, we can examine the limit\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}.\n$$\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- If the limit is *finite*, the statement is true.\n\n- If the limit is *infinite*, the statement is false.\n\n- If the limit does not exist, the test is inconclusive.\n\nHere, we must consider the polynomial\n\n$$\nf(x)=2{x}^{4}+5{x}^{2}+10.\n$$\n\nNow, let us analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Let $g(x)={x}^{5}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2{x}^{4}+5{x}^{2}+10}{{x}^{5}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{2}{x}+\\frac{5}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{10}{{x}^{5}}) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f=O({x}^{5})$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let $g(x)={x}^{2}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2{x}^{4}+5{x}^{2}+10}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(2{x}^{2}+5+\\frac{10}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f\\ne O({x}^{2})$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\n- Statement III is true. Let $g(x)=1.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2{x}^{4}+5{x}^{2}+10}{1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(2{x}^{4}+5{x}^{2}+10) \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f\\ne O(1)$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308723 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307673 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308723 1069 "q-335048" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308723 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308723 1071 "Suppose $f(x)={e}^{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f=O(1)$ as $x\\to \\infty $\n- $f=O(x)$ as $x\\to \\infty $\n- $f=O({e}^{x})$ as $x\\to \\infty $" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308723 1072 #uuid "38e1f503-d528-5681-bd45-38417a3415be" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308723 1074 "Suppose we wish to test whether the following statement is true:\n\n$$\nf(x)=O(g(x))\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is either finite or $+\\infty .$ To do this, we can examine the following limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}.\n$$\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- If the limit is *finite*, the statement is true.\n\n- If the limit is *infinite*, the statement is false.\n\n- If the limit does not exist, the test is inconclusive.\n\nHere, we must consider the function\n\n$$\nf(x)={e}^{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Let $g(x)=1.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({e}^{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f\\ne O(1)$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let $g(x)=x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince direct evaluation leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty },$ we can evaluate the limit using L'Hopital's rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{({e}^{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{′}}{(x)^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{({e}^{x}+\\frac{1}{x})}{1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({e}^{x}+\\frac{1}{x}) \\\\  & =\\infty +0 \\\\  & =\\infty \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f\\ne O(x)$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\n- Statement III is true. Let $g(x)={e}^{x}.$ Then, using L'Hopital's rule where needed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{e}^{x}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(1+\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{e}^{x}}) \\\\  & =1+{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{e}^{x}} \\\\  & =1+{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{′}}{({e}^{x})^{′}} \\\\  & =1+{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1/x}{{e}^{x}} \\\\  & =1+{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{x{e}^{x}} \\\\  & =1+0 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f=O({e}^{x})$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308724 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307964 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308724 1069 "q-335054" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308724 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308724 1071 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-2x(t)+3,\\end{aligned}\\,[\\begin{aligned}x(0) \\\\ y(0)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}].$*\n\n| $[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)$ |  |  |" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308724 1072 #uuid "03656a1f-f3fa-5cdf-b35f-3b5febfaad49" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308724 1074 "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t)+g(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]$ and $g(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as the sum of the homogeneous part and a particular solution:\n\n$$\nx(t)={{{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{homogeneous part}}+{{{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s}_{⏟}}_{\\text{particular solution}},\n$$\n\nwhere $x({t}_{0})$ is the initial condition vector. \n\nLet's evaluate each part separately:\n\n- First, we find the homogeneous part. We are given that \n\n$$\n{e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSince the initial conditions are given at $t={t}_{0}=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})} & ={e}^{At}\\,x(0) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Notice that \n\n$$\n{e}^{A(t-s)}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(t-s)) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(t-s)) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(t-s)) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(t-s))\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(t-s)) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(t-s)) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(t-s)) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(t-s))\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(t-s)) \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(t-s))\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(t-s)) \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(t-s))\\end{aligned}]\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(t-s)) \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(t-s))\\end{aligned}]}_{s=0}^{s=t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}3-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}3-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+3 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+3 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308725 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307577 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308725 1069 "q-335058" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308725 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308725 1071 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=4y(t)-4 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-4x(t),\\end{aligned}\\,[\\begin{aligned}x(0) \\\\ y(0)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 0\\end{aligned}].$*\n\n| $[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)$ |  |  |" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308725 1072 #uuid "411e1067-1bf2-5810-9a74-3f6443bfc23e" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308725 1074 "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t)+g(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]$ and $g(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as the sum of the homogeneous part and a particular solution:\n\n$$\nx(t)={{{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{homogeneous part}}+{{{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s}_{⏟}}_{\\text{particular solution}},\n$$\n\nwhere $x({t}_{0})$ is the initial condition vector. \n\nLet's evaluate each part separately:\n\n- First, we find the homogeneous part. We are given that \n\n$$\n{e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSince the initial conditions are given at $t={t}_{0}=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})} & ={e}^{At}\\,x(0) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Notice that \n\n$$\n{e}^{A(t-s)}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4(t-s)) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4(t-s)) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4(t-s)) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4(t-s))\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4(t-s)) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4(t-s)) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4(t-s)) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4(t-s))\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4(t-s)) \\\\ 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4(t-s))\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4(t-s)) \\\\ 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4(t-s))\\end{aligned}]\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4(t-s)) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4(t-s))\\end{aligned}]}_{s=0}^{s=t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ 1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ 1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ 1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308726 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307833 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308726 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307918 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308726 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308006 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308726 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308058 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308726 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308290 1319