\nand this follows by $\\text{the reflexive property}.$\n\n- Next, we consider line $\\text{L4}.$ From line $\\text{L1}$ we have\n\n$$\n2{e}^{4x}=O(2{e}^{4x}),\n$$\n\nand from line $\\text{L3}$ we have\n\n$$\nx=O(2{e}^{4x}).\n$$\n\nSince $f=2{e}^{4x}+x,$ by lines $\\text{L1 and L3}$ and $\\text{the sum property},$ we obtain\n\n$$\nf=2{e}^{4x}+x=O(2{e}^{4x}+2{e}^{4x})=O(4{e}^{4x}),\n$$\n\nwhich justifies line $\\text{L4}.$\n\n- Finally, we consider line $\\text{L5}.$ From line $\\text{L4}$ we have\n\n$$\nf=O(4{e}^{4x}),\n$$\n\nand by $\\text{the constant multiple property}$ with $c=4,$ we conclude\n\n$$\nf=O({e}^{4x}).\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308727 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307737 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308727 1069 "q-335073" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308727 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308727 1071 "$$\n{x}^{′}=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]x+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of ODEs above. The equilibrium point of the system is $[1,\\,2]^{T}.$ Which of the following is the system obtained by shifting the equilibrium to the origin?" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308727 1072 #uuid "6cde9d50-d619-5ea3-818d-eeeb232b8830" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308727 1074 "To shift the system's equilibrium from ${x}^{∗}$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nu=x-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,⇒\\,x=u+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}={u}^{′}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x$ and ${x}^{′}$ into the given system, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]x+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {u}^{′} & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}](u+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}])+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned