38 1074 "Recall that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{At})=A\\,{e}^{At}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{At}) & =A\\,{e}^{At} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 8 \\\\ -8 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(8t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(8t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8t) & 8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8t) \\\\ -8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8t) & -8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8t)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308739 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307666 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308739 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307790 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308739 1069 "q-335115" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308739 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308739 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem for the functions $y=y(x),z=z(x):$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=xy+z,\\, & y(0)=1 \\\\ {z}^{′}=x-y+2,\\, & z(0)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with two steps of equal size to approximate $y(1)$ and $z(1).$\n\n$y(1)≈$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,z(1)≈$\n{{field-2}}" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308739 1072 #uuid "45d5720b-f68d-5b35-9347-f940e6db3ca9" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308739 1074 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}=f(x,y,z),\\, & y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′}=g(x,y,z),\\, & z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEuler's method approximates the solution by applying the update rule component-wise to each equation. More precisely, the increments on $y$ and $z$ are\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}Δy={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx, \\\\ Δz={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe new values are then given by ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}=z+Δz.$\n\nFirst, note that we are given the values of $y$ and $z$ at $x=0$ and asked to find their values at $x=1.$ The distance from $x=0$ to $x=1$ is $1,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{1-0}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(0)=1$ and $z(0)=0.$\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ |  |  | $0$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nWe compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy,$ ${z}^{′}$ and $Δz$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the first row), and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}},$ ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =xy+z=0\\cdot 1+0=0 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =x-y+2=0-1+2=1 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=0\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=0 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=1\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+0=1 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=0+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ |  |  | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nWe compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy,$ ${z}^{′}$ and $Δz$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the second row), and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}},$ ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =xy+z=(\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot 1+\\frac{1}{2}=1 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =x-y+2=\\frac{1}{2}-1+2=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=1\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=1+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{3}{4}=\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add t