t \\frac{1}{2}=0 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=1\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=0+0=0 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=0+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $0$ |  |  | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nWe compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy,$ ${z}^{′}$ and $Δz$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the second row), and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}},$ ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x-y=\\frac{1}{2}-0=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {z}^{′} & =1+y=1+0=1 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=1\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=0+\\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ |  |  | $1$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny(1)≈\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{and}\\,z(1)≈1.\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308744 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308342 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308744 1069 "q-335135" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308744 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308744 1071 "A wildlife manager noted that the growth of the number of deer in a protected forest is directly proportional to the difference between the forest’s maximum deer population and the current population. Let $N(t)$ be the deer population, measured in number of deer, and let $t\\ge 0$ be the time in years. If the maximum deer population that the forest can support is $150$ deer, which differential equation could be used to model the forest’s deer population? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308744 1072 #uuid "54bac72f-651e-55b5-8b4b-fbc4e753ebc5" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308744 1074 "The rate at which the deer population $N(t)$ of the forest changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum deer population the forest can support and the current deer population. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=k(a-N),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum deer population the forest can support, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=150\\text{ deer},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}N}{\\text{d}t}=k(150-N).\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308745 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307868 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308745 1069 "q-335142" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308745 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308745 1071 "A tutoring center currently has $10$ enrolled students. The number of students in the center $T(t)$ increases at a rate that obeys an inhibited growth model, where $t\\ge 0$ is the time in months. If the maximum capacity of the tutoring center is $95$ students, which differential equation could be used to model the student population of the center? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308745 1072 #uuid "5651275c-fd64-5799-85e0-52ab15ebd288" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308745 1074 "Since the number of students in the center obeys an inhibited growth model, the rate at which the number of students changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum capacity and the number of students in the center. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=k(a-T),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum capacity of the tutoring center, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=95$ students, and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=k(95-T).\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308746 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308107 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308746 1069 "q-335145" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308746 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308746 1071 "A marine rehabilitation aquarium initially houses $25$ marine animals. The number of animals in the aquarium $M(t)$ increases at a rate that obeys an inhibited growth model, where $t\\ge 0$ is the time in months. If the maximum capacity of the aquarium is $60$ animals, which differential equation could be used to model the animal population? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308746 1072 #uuid "5e2af3a7-6f0f-5f5a-a5bb-60da7750b37e" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true] [17592186308746 1074 "Since the number of animals in the aquarium obeys an inhibited growth model, the rate at which the number of animals changes is proportional to the difference between the maximum capacity and the number of animals in the aquarium. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}M}{\\text{d}t}=k(a-M),\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ is the maximum capacity of the aquarium, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $a=60$ animals, and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}M}{\\text{d}t}=k(60-M).\n$$" 13194139533415 17592186045585 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-0039aee6-98b7-59c0-8d03-5b2d50aa3e57" 17592186305158 "answer-0041758a-5182-50fb-a219-5d3f3ef02024" 17592186305159 "answer-00555be1-a348-5be5-af69